/* Fuller answer explanations. Each one replaces the question's short explanation in feedback.
   Format: plain text; "\n" starts a new paragraph. Usually: why the answer is right, why the tempting
   wrong choices fail, and the rule or strategy to remember. */
window.INK_EXPLAIN = {
  /* ================================================================ questions.js */
  voc01: 'Corroborate means confirm with more evidence (a second witness corroborates the first). Undermine means weaken, often gradually. Mitigate means make something bad less severe without removing it. Ambivalent means having mixed feelings, pulled in two directions at once.\nTip: corroborate and undermine show up constantly in SAT evidence questions; one supports a claim, the other weakens it.',
  voc02: 'Ubiquitous means found everywhere (smartphones are ubiquitous). Tenuous means weak or thin, like a link resting on one coincidence. Pragmatic means practical, focused on what works. Disparate means very different from each other, so different they’re hard to compare.\nTip: don’t mix up disparate (very different) with desperate.',
  voc03: 'Correct: confirm. To corroborate is to back up an earlier claim with new, independent evidence. Here, new measurements agree with and support the earlier findings.\nWhy not the others: contradict is the opposite (new evidence that disagrees). Complicate and summarize don’t describe evidence supporting a finding.',
  voc04: 'Correct: ambivalent. The critics admired the visuals but disliked the plot, so they had mixed feelings, which is exactly what ambivalent means.\nWhy not the others: ecstatic (thrilled) and hostile (strongly against) are one-sided, but the critics felt both ways. Indifferent means not caring at all, yet the critics clearly had opinions.\nRule: a colon often introduces an explanation of the blank. Here, “admired … but … confusing” defines it.',
  voc05: 'False. As an adjective, novel means new or original, as in “a novel approach” that no one has tried before. The book meaning is the noun (“she wrote a novel”).\nTip: on the SAT, a familiar word used in an unfamiliar way is a common trap. Always check how the word is used in that sentence.',
  voc06: 'Correct: preclude. To preclude is to make something impossible in advance, to rule it out before it can happen: “The injury precluded her from playing.”\nWhy not the others: precede means come before, presume means assume without proof, and prescribe means officially order or recommend. They share the “pre-” beginning but mean different things.',
  voc07: 'Strengthens: corroborate (confirm with more evidence), substantiate (support with proof), and bolster (prop up) all make a claim stronger.\nWeakens: undermine (weaken from below), refute (prove false), and undercut (reduce the force of) all make a claim weaker.\nTip: in evidence questions, notice whether answer choices use a “strengthen” verb or a “weaken” verb. It tells you which direction the choice argues.',
  voc08: 'Correct: tenuous. A study with only twelve people gives weak evidence, so any link it finds is thin and easy to doubt, which is what tenuous means.\nWhy not the others: robust means strong, the opposite of what a tiny sample produces. Ubiquitous (everywhere) and pragmatic (practical) don’t describe how strong a link is.\nRule: “Because …” gives the reason. Small sample → weak evidence.',
  voc09: 'Correct: observation or experiment. Empirical evidence comes from actually observing, measuring, or testing the world, not from reasoning alone.\nWhy not the others: personal opinion, long tradition, and imagination are all sources of belief that involve no measurement or testing, so they’re the opposite of empirical.',
  voc10: 'Posit means propose something as true for the sake of argument. Reconcile means bring things into agreement (make conflicting facts fit together). Scrutinize means examine very closely. An anomaly is something that breaks the usual pattern.\nThink of a scientist who posits a theory, scrutinizes the data, and tries to reconcile any anomalies with the theory.',
  voc11: 'Correct: question the claim before accepting it. Skeptical means doubtful and wanting evidence before believing something.\nWhy not the others: repeating the claim or agreeing immediately shows the opposite of doubt. Ignoring the article completely isn’t skepticism either; a skeptic engages with the claim and asks for proof.',
  voc12: 'True. When something moderates an effect, the effect still happens, just less strongly. Exercise can moderate the effects of stress: the stress is still there, but weaker.\nTip: moderate (lessen) is different from reverse (turn into the opposite) or eliminate (remove completely). SAT answer choices often test this difference.',
  voc13: 'Correct: viable. Viable means able to work in practice. A plan the town can’t afford can’t actually be carried out, so it isn’t viable.\nWhy not the others: novel (new and original) and ambivalent (having mixed feelings) don’t relate to funding. Disparate means very different from each other.\nRule: “ambitious but not ___” sets up a contrast. The plan was bold, but it couldn’t work.',
  wic01: 'Correct: postpone. The clue is “set it aside until spring”: the committee is delaying the proposal, so to table it here means to postpone it.\nWhy not the others: display is the everyday “put it on a table” meaning, which doesn’t fit a vote. Organize and approve don’t match setting something aside.\nRule: in meaning-in-context questions, the everyday meaning of a word is often the trap. Let the surrounding words decide.',
  wic02: 'Correct: easy to handle. A trail can’t literally forgive anyone. The clues are “wide switchbacks” and “firm footing,” which make the climb manageable. Forgiving here means not punishing your mistakes, easy to handle.\nWhy not the others: merciful, apologetic, and generous are human qualities from the everyday meaning of forgiving. None makes sense describing a trail.',
  wic03: 'Correct: simplistic. “Anything but” means “the opposite of.” The second half shows the recipes are complex (a dozen spices, three continents), so they are the opposite of simplistic (too simple).\nWhy not the others: elaborate means complex, and “anything but elaborate” would mean simple, which contradicts the details. Authentic and costly don’t contrast with complexity.\nTip: watch for phrases that flip meaning: anything but, far from, hardly.',
  wic04: 'Correct: approximate. Blurry images and missing craters mean the maps were only roughly right, which is what approximate means. “At best” signals they were not exact.\nWhy not the others: exhaustive means complete, the opposite of missing many craters. Deliberate means on purpose. Hostile means unfriendly. None matches rough, incomplete maps.',
  wic05: 'Correct: restrained. The coach praised the passing but stopped short of calling the season a success, so she held her praise back. Measured praise is careful and controlled.\nWhy not the others: calculated in units is the literal meaning of measured (like measuring length), which doesn’t fit praise. Rhythmic and lengthy don’t describe holding back.',
  wic06: 'False. A cool reception means the audience was unenthusiastic, not impressed. In formal writing, cool often means lacking warmth or excitement.\nTip: slang meanings (“that’s cool!”) are almost never what the SAT intends. Use the context: a reception that’s “cool” at a conference means people weren’t excited.',
  wic07: 'Correct: demolish. “Rather than” introduces the option the city did NOT choose. They kept the brick walls and converted the building, so the rejected option must be the opposite: tearing it down.\nWhy not the others: preserve is what they actually did, so it can’t be the thing they did “rather than.” Celebrate and inspect aren’t opposites of keeping the building.',
  wic08: 'Correct: depends on. If an argument hinges on one survey, everything rests on that survey, which is why the argument is fragile: one weak survey could bring it down.\nWhy not the others: swings on, closes on, and attaches to come from the literal meaning of a door hinge. The context is about reasoning, so the figurative meaning “depends on” fits.',
  wic09: 'Correct: “yet.” Yet is a contrast word, like but. It sets the slow opening against the fast ending: the chapters are slow, yet the ending races along.\nTip: contrast signals include but, yet, however, although, though, and still. Spotting them tells you the text is about to shift direction.',
  wic10: 'Correct: impartial. Presenting both sides of a debate without revealing her own view means the narrator is fair and not taking sides, which is what impartial means.\nWhy not the others: passionate and sarcastic suggest strong feelings or attitude, which she’s hiding. Confused doesn’t match a narrator who clearly presents both sides.',
  wic11: 'Correct: showy. The text says the wing is “not ostentatious” and explains that its plain stone walls let the art stand out. So ostentatious must mean the opposite of plain: showy, meant to attract attention.\nWhy not the others: sturdy, expensive, and modern aren’t the opposite of plain walls that stay in the background.',
  tsp01: 'Correct: to challenge a common belief by describing a surprising event. The text starts with what “most people” believe (deserts are lifeless), then “Yet” introduces an event that contradicts it: the desert bursting into flowers.\nWhy not the others: the text never argues for protection, never compares deserts, and mentions a name only in passing.\nPattern: “Most people think X. Yet …” almost always signals a text challenging a common belief.',
  tsp02: 'Correct: the last sentence. “For these reasons” tells you the earlier sentences are the reasons and this sentence is the conclusion they support: native plantings are a smart choice.\nWhy not the others: the first three sentences give facts (cities replacing medians, less water, attracting bees). They are evidence, not the main claim.',
  tsp03: 'Order: topic sentence (bees share food locations through a dance) → first detail (the angle shows direction) → second detail (waggle length shows distance) → result (other bees fly straight to the food).\nWhy: a paragraph usually introduces the topic, explains how it works, then shows the outcome. The last sentence begins “Using this information,” which only makes sense after the details are given.',
  tsp04: 'Correct: it gives a specific example of how Ruiz balanced work and writing. The first sentence says she worked full-time while writing; the next sentence shows exactly how: two hours of writing before a six o’clock shift.\nWhy not the others: the sentence doesn’t mention the prize, other novelists, or the plot.\nRule: to find a sentence’s function, ask how it relates to the sentence before it. Here it illustrates.',
  tsp05: 'Describes: explaining how reefs form, listing steps, and recounting a journey report information without taking a side.\nArgues: claiming schools should start later, insisting a policy is unfair, and urging support all take a position and try to persuade.\nTip: words like should, unfair, and urge signal argument. Neutral verbs like explain, list, and recount signal description.',
  tsp06: 'True. If a passage only reports what happened in an experiment, it isn’t taking a side, so “to argue” overstates its purpose.\nTip: purpose choices can be wrong just because their verb is too strong. Match the verb to what the text actually does: describe, explain, compare, argue, criticize.',
  tsp07: 'Correct: it presents one view, then a competing view that the text favors. “Some accounts credit a single inventor” is view one; “Others point out…” is view two; and the last sentence (“This second view suggests…”) sides with view two.\nWhy not the others: there are no steps, the facts are connected, and the question of who invented the bicycle is answered.',
  tsp08: 'Correct: to convey a quiet, isolated winter moment. The details (a humming kettle, a fogged window, a slow clock, snow covering familiar roads) build a calm, cut-off mood.\nWhy not the others: the poem doesn’t argue for anything, explain how a kettle works, or describe traveling. The roads are mentioned only because snow has erased them.',
  tsp09: 'Correct: it describes an early benefit and then explains how that benefit was undone. “At first” introduces the benefit (time saved), and “But” introduces the reversal (shortcuts jammed, savings gone).\nWhy not the others: only one app is discussed, nothing is argued for banning, and no solutions are listed.\nPattern: “At first … But …” signals an initial result followed by a reversal.',
  tsp10: 'To illustrate is to give an example. To qualify is to add a limit or condition to a claim (“this works, but only for small groups”). To refute is to show a claim is wrong. To elaborate is to add more detail.\nTip: qualify is the trickiest. On the SAT it almost never means “to be eligible”; it means to narrow or limit a claim.',
  ctc01: 'Correct: by questioning whether breakfast actually improves focus as measured by performance. Text 1 claims breakfast sharpens focus. Text 2 found breakfast-skippers scored just as well, so its data doesn’t support Text 1’s claim.\nWhy not the others: Text 2 doesn’t agree, doesn’t say breakfast is harmful, and doesn’t claim hunger helps (it only notes skippers felt hungrier).\nMethod: sum up each text in a few words, then ask how Text 2’s evidence affects Text 1’s claim.',
  ctc02: 'Correct: it offers evidence that challenges the reasoning in Text 1. Text 1 says planners widen roads to reduce jams. Text 2 says new lanes attract more drivers, so jams return. That undercuts the logic behind widening roads.\nWhy not the others: Text 2 doesn’t support Text 1, isn’t a restatement, and is clearly on the same topic.',
  ctc03: 'True. Cross-text questions ask from one author’s point of view (“How would the author of Text 2 respond to Text 1?”). Reading the question first tells you whose view to take and which claim is being responded to, so you don’t answer backwards.',
  ctc04: 'Correct: printed books became less expensive. Text 1 says the press made books cheaper; Text 2 explicitly “agrees that printed books became cheaper.” That’s the shared point.\nWhy not the others: only Text 2 stresses literacy, and even it calls literacy “just as important,” not “the main cause.” Neither text says the press slowed ideas or that cheaper books had no effect.\nRule: for “both would agree,” look for something each text states or accepts.',
  ctc05: 'Agrees: concurs (agrees), echoes (repeats or supports), endorses (approves).\nDisagrees: disputes (argues against), challenges (questions), rejects (refuses to accept).\nTip: answer choices in cross-text questions often hinge on these verbs. Knowing them helps you quickly spot choices that point the wrong direction.',
  ctc06: 'Correct: the silent scenes serve an important emotional purpose. Text 1 calls the silences tedious; Text 2 says they let viewers “sit with the characters’ grief” and calls them the film’s greatest strength. So Text 2 would defend them for their emotional effect.\nWhy not the others: Text 2 never says the film is too short or that the scenes should be longer, and it explicitly mentions the characters’ grief.',
  cid01: 'Correct: cliff nests likely help chicks survive by keeping predators away. The text connects three facts: cliff birds raised more chicks, foxes are the main predator, and foxes almost never reached cliff nests. Together they suggest cliffs protect chicks.\nWhy not the others: the text never says why foxes hunt on beaches, never mentions nest materials, and never predicts the birds will move.',
  cid02: 'Correct: the last sentence. The earlier sentences give details (seeds borrowed like books, saved seeds returned, the collection tripled). The last sentence explains what those details show: a library can grow by relying on its community.\nRule: the central idea is the point the details add up to, often in the first or last sentence.',
  cid03: 'Correct: it makes them less visible. The last sentence states the result directly: moving red blood cells into the mirror-coated liver makes the sleeping frogs even harder to see.\nWhy not the others: digestion, warmth, and breathing are never mentioned.\nTip: for “according to the text, why” questions, look for a sentence that states the result or reason directly.',
  cid04: 'False. The text says only that sales doubled after the bread was introduced. Something happening after an event doesn’t prove the event caused it; other things might explain the increase.\nRule: correlation or timing isn’t causation. Only choose “caused” if the text actually says so.',
  cid05: 'Correct: she focuses on one small error despite a successful performance. The audience applauds, but “all she could think about was the restart,” one small mistake in an otherwise successful recital.\nWhy not the others: she did finish (the final chord), she had practiced for months, and nothing suggests she dislikes the piano.',
  cid06: 'Correct: plants bending toward light involves more factors than scientists once believed. The text moves from the old view (“a single plant hormone”) to the new one (several hormones plus temperature and time of day).\nWhy not the others: the text doesn’t compare growth at night, doesn’t make temperature the only cause, and doesn’t say scientists stopped studying hormones.',
  cid07: 'Supported: stations went from 50 to 200 (more stations, and 200 is four times 50, so quadrupled), and rides grew from 80,000 to 450,000.\nNot supported: the text never mentions car traffic, whether riders prefer bikes to buses, or what will happen in the future.\nRule: a statement is supported only if the text’s facts show it, not if it just seems likely.',
  cid08: 'Correct: it repairs an existing web instead of building a new one each night. The text contrasts most orb weavers (eat their webs and spin new ones nightly) with this species (keeps one web for weeks, fixing only broken threads).\nWhy not the others: the text says nothing about day spinning, catching prey, or web size.',
  cid09: 'False. Words like “earliest,” “only,” or “first” add claims the text must support. If the text doesn’t say them, an answer using them is wrong, even if it otherwise sounds right.\nRule: the correct main-idea answer stays within what the text actually says.',
  inf01: 'Correct: the reaction between seawater and volcanic ash may help explain why the Roman walls have lasted so long. The text connects volcanic ash, seawater reacting with it, and new minerals that strengthen the concrete over time. That chain explains the walls’ survival.\nWhy not the others: nothing says modern builders never use ash; “strongest on the day it was poured” contradicts “strengthened over time”; and “damages all concrete equally” contradicts the comparison between Roman and modern concrete.',
  inf02: 'Correct: the higher scores may reflect which students chose those seats rather than the sunlight. The teacher’s new information is that the top students picked the window seats, which offers a different explanation: the students, not the light.\nWhy not the others: nothing suggests sunlight lowers scores, the text gives no rule about choosing seats, and afternoon focus for all students isn’t discussed.\nPattern: when a text introduces a new fact after an initial guess, the blank usually completes the alternative explanation.',
  inf03: 'True. All officers are seniors, and Jaden is not a senior. If he were an officer, he would have to be a senior, so he can’t be one.\nRule: if every A is a B, then something that isn’t a B can’t be an A.',
  inf04: 'Supported: 62% is more than half (“most” and “over half”), and 100% − 62% = 38% did not report daily phone news.\nGoes too far: the survey covered 500 teens in one city, so it can’t speak for all U.S. teens; it never asked about printed newspapers; and it doesn’t show phones are the “best” source.\nRule: conclusions must stay within who was surveyed and what was asked.',
  inf05: 'Correct: people at the site obtained the fish through travel or trade with coastal groups. The fish couldn’t have swum there (no rivers) and the site was never underwater, so the only remaining explanation is that people brought them.\nWhy not the others: the text rules out the site being underwater; whether saltwater fish survive in fresh water is outside information; and nothing says people ate only fish.',
  inf06: 'Correct: turn its head. The owl’s eyes can’t move in their sockets, so the only way to look to the side is to turn its whole head, which it can do about 270 degrees.\nWhy not the others: “move its eyes” is exactly what the text says owls can’t do. Closing an eye or flying upward wouldn’t let it see to the side.',
  inf07: 'Correct: considered faster delivery worth more than $4 to them. Most chose to pay $4 instead of taking free delivery, which shows that, for them, speed was worth the money.\nWhy not the others: nothing suggests they missed the free option; one choice doesn’t show they “always pay for every upgrade”; and paying extra contradicts caring most about saving money.',
  inf08: 'False. An inference must be drawn from the passage’s own information. A choice that adds an outside fact, even a true one, isn’t supported by the text and is a common trap.',
  inf09: 'Correct: Lena does not play a string instrument. Every string player rehearses on Tuesdays; Lena rehearses only on Thursdays. If she played strings, she would rehearse on Tuesdays, so she can’t.\nWhy not the others: violin is a string instrument, which contradicts the logic; Lena herself rehearses on Thursdays, so others may too; and nothing says she’ll change.',
  inf10: 'Correct: the covers. The region had its “usual number of frosty nights,” which rules out warmer weather. The one thing that changed was covering the plants each evening, so that’s the most likely reason they survived.\nWhy not the others: warmer weather is ruled out, and new seeds and extra watering are never mentioned.\nPattern: when the text rules out one explanation, the answer is the factor that actually changed.',
  coe01: 'Correct: the students who used the app had already scored higher before the study began. If app users were ahead from the start, their higher scores might not come from the app at all. That offers another explanation for the difference.\nWhy not the others: popularity with teachers, students enjoying it, and the number of words in the app don’t tell us whether it raised scores.\nRule: the strongest way to weaken a cause-and-effect claim is another explanation for the same result.',
  coe02: 'Correct: students who slept 8+ hours recalled 30% more studied words than students who slept 5 hours. The claim has three parts: sleep amount, memory of studied material, and a comparison (“remember more”). This finding covers all three.\nWhy not the others: feeling tired in class isn’t memory; “sleep more also exercise more” suggests a different cause; and “sleep needs vary by age” doesn’t compare memory at all.\nRule: supporting evidence must match every key part of the claim, not just its topic.',
  coe03: 'Supports: a 40% drop in crashes after the lane opened, fewer pedestrian injuries, and cyclists feeling safer all point toward the lane making the street safer.\nWeakens: each of these offers another reason crashes could have dropped. Nearby streets without lanes dropped just as much (so something citywide happened), a police crackdown started the same month, and winter means fewer riders.\nRule: a finding weakens a cause-and-effect claim when it gives another explanation for the same result.',
  coe04: 'Correct: caffeine raised scores by 10 points in noisy rooms but only 3 points in quiet rooms. “Moderates” means the effect still points the same way (caffeine still helps) but is smaller. 10 → 3 shows exactly that.\nWhy not the others: lowering scores reverses the effect instead of shrinking it; equal gains show no moderation; and comparing quiet rooms to caffeine doesn’t test caffeine’s effect in each room.',
  coe05: 'Correct: “The river never wears the same face twice; / I, too, am carried on.” The claim needs two things: a water image and the idea of change. The river “never the same twice” and being “carried on” both express change.\nWhy not the others: the mountain is unmoved (no water, no change); the kitchen has neither; and “The river was cold and gray” has water but says nothing about change.\nRule: the right quotation matches every part of the claim.',
  coe06: 'True. If you can show the same evidence could be explained some other way, the evidence no longer proves the claim. For example, if app users scored higher but were already ahead before the study, the app may not be the reason.',
  coe07: 'Correct: records show a family from that city moved to the village and lived there for decades. The historian says the coin must have come through trade. A family moving there gives another way the coin could have arrived, so trade isn’t the only explanation.\nWhy not the others: more coins from that city and a weekly market would make trade more likely, strengthening the argument. The coin’s metal doesn’t tell us how it arrived.',
  coe08: '“More likely” needs a comparison between groups. “Caused” needs other explanations ruled out. “All” means no exceptions, so evidence about only some cases isn’t enough. “Moderates” needs a weaker effect in the same direction.\nTip: when you read a claim, circle these key words. Each one sets a requirement the evidence must meet.',
  coe09: 'Correct: gardens of native plants had twice as many bee visits per hour as similar-sized gardens of non-native plants. The claim compares pollinator attraction between native and non-native plants, and this finding measures exactly that, using fair, similar-sized gardens.\nWhy not the others: water needs and bloom length don’t measure pollinators, and “bees visited both” doesn’t show which attracted more.',
  qnt01: 'Correct: 17- to 18-year-olds increased by only 0.1 hour, compared with 0.8 and 0.6 hour for the younger groups. The increases are 4.1 → 4.9 = 0.8, 5.0 → 5.6 = 0.6, and 5.4 → 5.5 = 0.1. This shows both parts of the claim: every group rose, and the oldest rose least.\nWhy not the others: the 13–14 group had the least screen time in 2023 (4.9), not the most; 17–18 screen time rose rather than fell; and the 15–16 increase was 0.6, not 1.6.',
  qnt02: 'Answer: 95. September had 420 books and October had 515. The increase is the difference: 515 − 420 = 95.\nTip: “by how many” asks for a difference (subtract), not a percent or a total.',
  qnt03: 'True. Plants in full sun grew to 24 cm, and half of 24 is 12. Plants in full shade grew to only 9 cm, which is less than 12, so they reached less than half the full-sun height.',
  qnt04: 'Correct: Layer A. The ages are in “millions of years ago,” so a bigger number means further back in time. Layer A formed 12.5 million years ago, earlier than B (8.1) or C (3.4), so it’s the oldest.\nTip: always read the units. With “years ago,” bigger means older.',
  qnt05: 'Correct: 58% of teachers prefer practice tests, compared with 41% of students. The finding says teachers favor practice tests more strongly than students do, so the support must compare teachers and students on practice tests.\nWhy not the others: “most popular among students” is true but doesn’t compare with teachers; the flashcard vs. rereading choice is off-topic; and 22% for flashcards doesn’t address practice tests.',
  qnt06: 'Correct: between 7.6 and 9.1 mg/L. 25°C falls between the table’s 20°C (9.1 mg/L) and 30°C (7.6 mg/L) rows. Since oxygen drops steadily as temperature rises, the value at 25°C falls between those two values.\nWhy not the others: 9.1–11.3 is the range for 10–20°C; above 11.3 would be colder than 10°C; and below 7.6 would be warmer than 30°C.',
  qnt07: 'True statements: City X is wetter in July (6.3) than January (2.1); City Y gets more January rain (4.8) than City X (2.1); and City Y’s July rainfall (1.2) is the lowest value shown.\nFalse statements: “both cities are wetter in July” fails because City Y gets far less rain in July (1.2) than in January (4.8); “City X gets more January rain” fails because 2.1 < 4.8.',
  qnt08: 'Correct: May had the most volunteers. May’s bar is the tallest, at 30.\nWhy not the others: the count fell from May (30) to June (24), so it didn’t rise every month; April (18) had fewer than June (24); and March (12) and June (24) were different.\nTip: check every part of a choice against the graph; one month that breaks a pattern makes “every month” false.',
  trn01: 'Correct: In fact. The first sentence says terns migrate farther than any other bird; the second backs that up with a striking detail (over 70,000 km a year). “In fact” introduces information that strengthens or intensifies the previous point.\nWhy not the others: however and instead signal contrast, but the second sentence agrees with the first. Similarly would introduce a different but alike example, not a detail about the same terns.',
  trn02: 'Correct: However. The original design called for steel cables, but engineers switched to carbon fiber. A changed plan contrasts with the original one.\nWhy not the others: similarly and in addition would suggest the second sentence agrees with or adds to the first; for example would suggest the switch illustrates the steel design.\nMethod: name the relationship (here, plan A → changed to plan B = contrast), then choose a contrast word.',
  trn03: 'Contrast: however, nevertheless, and by contrast all signal that the next idea goes against or differs from the previous one.\nCause → effect: therefore, consequently, and as a result all signal that the next idea is a result of the previous one.\nTip: these two families cover a large share of SAT transition questions. If you can tell contrast from cause-and-effect, you can answer most of them.',
  trn04: 'Correct: for example. The first sentence makes a general statement (many desert plants store water in thick stems); the saguaro is one specific case of that.\nWhy not the others: however and in contrast would mean the saguaro is an exception, but it follows the pattern. Instead would mean it does something other than what was described.',
  trn05: 'Addition → furthermore (adds another point). Restatement → in other words (says the same idea differently). Alternative → instead (one option replacing another). Sequence → subsequently (what came next in time).\nTip: name the relationship between the two sentences first; the right transition family follows.',
  trn06: 'Correct: Next. “First” in the first sentence sets up a sequence of steps, and the second step should be introduced with a time-order word.\nWhy not the others: in contrast and similarly describe how ideas compare, not the order of actions. For instance would make the cleaning an example of repairing the frame, which it isn’t.',
  trn07: 'Correct: Therefore. Having only 14 participants is the reason the findings should be treated as preliminary, so the relationship is cause → result.\nWhy not the others: nevertheless signals a contrast (it would suggest the findings are reliable despite the small sample); similarly signals likeness; for example signals an illustration.',
  trn08: 'Correct: however. The first sentence gives the usual pattern (volcanoes form where plates meet); Hawaii’s volcanoes break that pattern by sitting in the middle of a plate. An exception to a rule is a contrast.\nWhy not the others: therefore would mean Hawaii’s location results from the rule; likewise and for instance would mean Hawaii follows the pattern.',
  trn09: 'False. Two sentences can be on the same topic but contrast (“Most liquids shrink when frozen. Water, however, expands.”) or show cause and effect. “Similarly” is right only when the second idea is alike in some way to the first.\nRule: decide the relationship between the ideas, not just whether they share a topic.',
  trn10: 'Correct: Still. Since bamboo is a grass, you wouldn’t expect it to grow taller than many trees, so the second sentence is surprising. “Still” signals a contrast (“even so”).\nWhy not the others: thus signals a result (being a grass doesn’t cause height); likewise signals similarity; in other words signals a restatement, but the second sentence adds a new, surprising fact.',
  trn11: 'Correct: By contrast. The text describes two groups with opposite preferences, printed versus digital, so the relationship is contrast.\nWhy not the others: therefore suggests the second preference results from the first; for instance suggests an example of the first group; in other words suggests a restatement.',
  syn01: 'Correct: “Mercury orbits the Sun in just 88 Earth days, whereas Neptune takes about 165 Earth years.” The goal is to emphasize a difference in orbit time, so the answer needs both planets, both orbit times, and a contrast word (“whereas”).\nWhy not the others: one only gives Mercury’s distance; one gives only Neptune’s orbit with nothing to compare it to; and “both orbit the Sun” is a similarity.',
  syn02: 'Correct: “Like the giraffe, the okapi uses a long, flexible tongue to pull leaves from plants.” The goal is a similarity, so the answer must mention both animals and something they share. “Like” signals the similarity.\nWhy not the others: rainforests vs. savannas is a difference; the other two mention only one animal.',
  syn03: 'Introduce to a new audience → explain what or who it is. Emphasize a contrast → name both things and show how they differ. Make a generalization → state a broad claim and back it with support. Describe a study’s method → explain how the study was done (not what it found).\nTip: in rhetorical synthesis questions, the goal in the question decides the answer, not how many notes a choice uses.',
  syn04: 'Correct: “Mary Golda Ross, a Cherokee engineer at Lockheed, is known as the first Native American woman to work as an engineer.” Introducing someone to readers who don’t know her means saying who she is and why she matters.\nWhy not the others: the coin is an interesting detail but doesn’t say who she is; the Lockheed sentence doesn’t mention Ross; and “her work lasted many years” assumes the reader already knows her.',
  syn05: 'Correct: “Researchers had 60 students read the same passage, with half reading in silence and half with café noise playing.” Methodology means how the study was done: who participated and what conditions they were in.\nWhy not the others: “finished 8% faster” is the result; “noise may slow reading” is a conclusion; “wanted to know whether…” is the research question.',
  syn06: 'Correct: “Curbside pickup tends to increase recycling: rates rose in both towns that added it but stayed flat in the town that did not.” A generalization makes a broad claim (“tends to”) and supports it with evidence. This choice does both.\nWhy not the others: each of them states a single fact without drawing a general conclusion.',
  syn07: 'True. In rhetorical synthesis questions, every choice is usually accurate to the notes, so accuracy can’t decide the answer. The choice that accomplishes the specific goal in the question is correct.',
  syn08: 'Contrast: “Unlike frogs, toads…” and “Frogs lay eggs in clusters, whereas toads…” both show a difference. Unlike and whereas signal contrast.\nSimilarity: “Both frogs and toads are amphibians” and “Like frogs, toads begin life as tadpoles” both show something shared. Both and like signal similarity.',
  bnd01: 'Correct: semicolon. “The storm knocked out power across the city” and “crews worked through the night to restore it” are both complete sentences. A semicolon can join two complete sentences.\nWhy not the others: a comma alone creates a comma splice; no punctuation creates a run-on; and “, which” would make “which crews worked…” ungrammatical.\nRule: if both sides could stand alone, use a period, a semicolon, or a comma plus and/but/so.',
  bnd02: 'Correct: colon. The first part is a complete sentence that mentions “one rule,” and what follows explains what that rule is. A colon introduces an explanation, list, or example after a complete sentence.\nWhy not the others: a comma alone creates a comma splice (two complete sentences); no punctuation creates a run-on; and “, and” would treat the rule as a separate, unrelated idea instead of the explanation.',
  bnd03: 'Correct: no punctuation. “Toni Morrison” tells you which author, so it’s essential information and doesn’t get commas. (Commas would mean the name is extra, as if there were only one author in the world.)\nWhy not the others: a comma would wrongly separate the subject from its identifying name; a colon or semicolon can’t go between a subject and its name.\nRule: essential information that identifies which one gets no commas.',
  bnd04: 'Correct: dash. The extra information (“the deepest known point in Earth’s oceans”) opens with a dash, so it must close with a dash too.\nWhy not the others: a comma would mismatch the opening dash; a semicolon would split the subject from its verb (“plunges”); and no punctuation would leave the interruption unclosed.\nRule: interruptions use matching marks on both sides: two commas, two dashes, or two parentheses.',
  bnd05: 'Correct: comma. “After the final bell rang” is an introductory dependent clause; it can’t stand alone. A comma separates it from the main clause.\nWhy not the others: a semicolon or colon must follow a complete sentence, and “After the final bell rang” isn’t one. No punctuation makes the sentence harder to read and isn’t standard after a long introductory clause.',
  bnd06: 'True. “I finished my essay” and “I submitted it online” are both complete sentences, and they’re joined only by a comma, which is a comma splice.\nHow to fix it: use a period (“…essay. I submitted…”), a semicolon (“…essay; I submitted…”), or a comma plus a conjunction (“…essay, and I submitted…”).',
  bnd07: 'Independent clauses: “The bus arrived late,” “She ran to the stop,” and “Everyone was surprised” each have a subject and a verb and express a complete thought.\nNot complete sentences: “Because the bus arrived late” starts with “because,” which makes it depend on another clause; “Running to the stop” has no subject or full verb; “Which surprised everyone” starts with “which” and needs a sentence to attach to.',
  bnd08: 'Correct: “, but”. “The team practiced for weeks” and “they still lost the opening match” are both complete sentences. A comma plus a coordinating conjunction (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) correctly joins them.\nWhy not the others: “but” without a comma is a common error with two long independent clauses; a comma alone is a comma splice; and a colon followed by “but” isn’t standard.',
  bnd09: 'Correct: comma. The extra information (“which cover less than 1% of the ocean floor”) closes with a comma before “support,” so it must open with a comma too.\nWhy not the others: a dash would mismatch the closing comma; a semicolon would split the subject from its verb; and no punctuation would leave the interruption unbalanced.\nRule: whatever mark closes an interruption must also open it.',
  bnd10: 'Correct: “The recipe needs three things: flour, water, and salt.” A colon must follow a complete sentence, and “The recipe needs three things” is complete.\nWhy not the others: “The recipe needs:” puts a colon after an incomplete sentence (the verb needs its object); a semicolon can’t introduce a list; and the last choice wrongly puts a comma between the subject and verb.',
  bnd11: 'Correct: no punctuation. “The students who finished early” is the whole subject, and “helped” is its verb. Never put punctuation between a subject and its verb.\nWhy not the others: a comma, semicolon, or dash would cut the subject off from its verb.\nTip: “who finished early” is essential (it tells which students), so it gets no commas either.',
  fss01: 'Correct: landed. The sentence is set in 1969, and the second verb, “returned,” is past tense. The verbs should match, so simple past: landed.\nWhy not the others: lands is present, will land is future, and has landed (present perfect) doesn’t fit a completed event at a specific past date.',
  fss02: 'Correct: students’. The lockers belong to many students, so you need the plural possessive: add an apostrophe after the s.\nWhy not the others: students (no apostrophe) is just a plural, not possessive; student’s means one student; and students’s isn’t standard.\nRule: plural ending in s → add only an apostrophe (students’ lockers).',
  fss03: 'Correct: is. The subject is “collection,” which is singular. “Of rare maps” is a prepositional phrase that describes the collection; it doesn’t change the subject.\nWhy not the others: are and have been are plural and match “maps,” the trap noun. Were is plural and past tense.\nRule: cross out “of …” phrases to find the true subject.',
  fss04: 'Correct: “Leo was amazed by the paintings.” The opening phrase “Walking through the museum” describes whoever comes right after the comma. Leo is the one walking, so “Leo” must come next.\nWhy not the others: the paintings, amazement, and the visit can’t walk through a museum. Those choices create dangling modifiers.',
  fss05: 'Correct: its. A committee is one group, so it takes a singular pronoun, and its (no apostrophe) shows possession.\nWhy not the others: their is plural; it’s means “it is”; they’re means “they are.”\nTest: say “it is” in the blank: “announced it is decision” makes no sense, so it’s is wrong.',
  fss06: 'Plural only: “The musicians played,” “Three dogs barked,” and “Several cities” just mean more than one; nothing belongs to the noun, so no apostrophe.\nPossessive: “The musician’s guitar,” “The dogs’ leashes,” and “The city’s mayor” all show something belonging to the noun, so they need apostrophes.\nRule: use an apostrophe only to show ownership (or in a contraction), never just to make a plural.',
  fss07: 'True. The subject is “list” (singular); “of ingredients” is a prepositional phrase describing it. The verb must agree with “list”: “The list of ingredients is long.”\nTip: cross out the “of …” phrase and read “The list is long” to check.',
  fss08: 'Correct: reads. The sentence lists three things Priya does: walks, makes, and ___. Items in a list should match in form (parallel structure), so the third verb is reads.\nWhy not the others: read, reading, and will read break the pattern of present-tense verbs ending in -s.',
  fss09: 'Correct: its. The logo belongs to the company, and the possessive form of “it” is its, with no apostrophe.\nWhy not the others: it’s means “it is” (“redesigned it is logo” makes no sense); its’ isn’t a word; it is is the full form of it’s.',
  fss10: 'Correct: “the cake was devoured by the children.” The opening phrase “Covered in chocolate frosting” describes whatever comes right after the comma. The cake is covered in frosting, so “the cake” must come next.\nWhy not the others: “the children devoured the cake” makes the children covered in frosting; the other choices are dangling or ungrammatical.',
  fss11: 'Correct: were. With neither … nor (and either … or), the verb agrees with the subject closest to it. “Players” is closest and plural, so were.\nWhy not the others: was, is, and has been are singular, matching “coach,” which is the farther subject.',
  lin01: 'Answer: 5.\nStep 1: subtract 7 from both sides: 3x + 7 − 7 = 22 − 7, so 3x = 15.\nStep 2: divide both sides by 3: x = 15 ÷ 3 = 5.\nCheck: 3(5) + 7 = 22 ✓.',
  lin02: 'Correct: y > 2. The boundary is a horizontal line at y = 2, so the inequality is about y. Shading above means y is greater than 2. The dashed line means points on the line are not included, so the sign is >, not ≥.\nWhy not the others: y ≥ 2 would need a solid line; y < 2 would shade below; x > 2 would have a vertical boundary.',
  lin03: 'Correct: x < −4. Divide both sides of −2x > 8 by −2. Dividing or multiplying an inequality by a negative number flips the sign: x < −4.\nCheck with a number: x = −5 gives −2(−5) = 10 > 8 ✓.\nTrap: x > −4 forgets to flip the sign.',
  lin04: 'Answer: 115. The sign-up fee is paid once ($25), and the monthly fee is paid 6 times ($15 × 6 = $90). Total: 25 + 90 = 115.\nTip: a one-time fee is the starting value; a per-month fee is the rate you multiply by the number of months.',
  lin05: 'Correct: 3. Slope = change in y ÷ change in x = (12 − 3) ÷ (4 − 1) = 9 ÷ 3 = 3.\nWhy not the others: 9 is only the change in y; 1/3 flips the fraction (change in x over change in y); 4 is just one of the x-values.',
  lin06: 'True. Multiplying or dividing both sides of an inequality by a negative number reverses it. Example: 2 < 5, but multiplying by −1 gives −2 > −5, since −2 is to the right of −5 on a number line.',
  lin07: 'Correct: 7. Add the two equations to cancel y: (x + y) + (x − y) = 10 + 4, so 2x = 14 and x = 7.\nCheck: then y = 3, and 7 + 3 = 10 ✓, 7 − 3 = 4 ✓.\nTrap: 14 is 2x, not x.',
  lin08: 'A dashed boundary line means the boundary is not included (< or >). A solid line means it is included (≤ or ≥). Shading above a horizontal line means y is greater; shading below means y is less.\nTip: the line style tells you whether to use “or equal to,” and the shading tells you the direction.',
  lin09: 'Answer: 10.\nStep 1: distribute: 2(x − 3) = 2x − 6, so 2x − 6 = 14.\nStep 2: add 6: 2x = 20.\nStep 3: divide by 2: x = 10.\n(Or divide both sides by 2 first: x − 3 = 7, so x = 10.)',
  lin10: 'Correct: n + 5 ≤ 12. “A number n increased by 5” is n + 5, and “at most 12” means it can be 12 or less: ≤ 12.\nWhy not the others: < 12 doesn’t allow 12 itself; ≥ 12 means at least 12; 5n means 5 times n, not 5 more than n.',
  lin11: 'Correct: x ≤ −1. The boundary is a vertical line, so the inequality is about x. Shading to the left means x is less than −1, and the solid line means −1 itself is included, so ≤.\nWhy not the others: x < −1 would need a dashed line; x ≥ −1 would shade to the right; y ≤ −1 would have a horizontal boundary.',
  exp01: 'Answer: 9. You don’t need x or y separately. Rewrite 6x/(2y) as (6/2) · (x/y) = 3 · (x/y). Since x/y = 3, the value is 3 · 3 = 9.\nTip: look for the given expression (x/y) hiding inside the new one.',
  exp02: 'Answer: 23. Factor out 4: 4a + 4b − 5 = 4(a + b) − 5. Since a + b = 7, that’s 4(7) − 5 = 28 − 5 = 23.\nTip: you can’t find a and b separately, and you don’t need to. Substitute the whole expression.',
  exp03: 'Correct: 36. Notice that 6x + 9y = 3(2x + 3y). Since 2x + 3y = 12, the value is 3 × 12 = 36.\nWhy not the others: 24 multiplies by 2; 12 is the original value; 48 multiplies by 4.',
  exp04: 'Correct: 10x − 15. Distribute the 3: 3(2x − 5) = 6x − 15. Then add 4x: 6x + 4x − 15 = 10x − 15.\nWhy not the others: 6x − 15 forgets to add the 4x; 10x − 5 forgets to multiply the 5 by 3; 6x − 1 combines unlike terms.',
  exp05: 'Answer: 55. x² − y² is a difference of squares, which factors as (x − y)(x + y). So x² − y² = 5 × 11 = 55.\nTip: you could solve for x = 8 and y = 3 and get 64 − 9 = 55, but the factoring shortcut is faster.',
  exp06: 'False. In 3m/(3n), the 3 in the numerator and the 3 in the denominator cancel, so 3m/(3n) = m/n = 4, not 12.\nTrap: multiplying only the top by 3 would triple the value, but here both top and bottom are multiplied by 3.',
  exp07: 'Steps: rewrite 24x/(ny) as (24/n) · (x/y) → substitute x/y = 4 to get (24/n) · 4 = 96/n → set 96/n = 4 → solve: n = 96 ÷ 4 = 24.\nStrategy: find the given expression inside the new one, substitute it, then solve the simpler equation.',
  exp08: 'Correct: 62. Notice 15k = 3 · 5k = 3 · 20 = 60. Then 15k + 2 = 62.\n(Or solve k = 4, then 15(4) + 2 = 62.)\nWhy not the others: 60 forgets the + 2; 22 adds 2 to 20; 302 treats “15k” as the digits 15 and k = 20 side by side.',
  exp09: '2(x + 3) = 2x + 6 (distribute). x² − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3) (difference of squares). (x + 2)² = x² + 4x + 4 (square a binomial: x² + 2·2·x + 2²). 3x + 3y = 3(x + y) (factor out the common 3).\nTrap: (x + 2)² is not x² + 4; don’t forget the middle term.',
  sta01: 'Answer: 3.5. The table lists values with frequencies, so write out every value: 2, 3, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5, 5, 5. That’s 10 values. With an even count, the median is the average of the 5th and 6th: (3 + 4) ÷ 2 = 3.5.\nTrap: taking the median of just 2, 3, 4, 5 ignores the frequencies.',
  sta02: 'Correct: the mean increases by 10; the standard deviation stays the same. Adding 10 to every value shifts the whole data set up by 10, so the center (mean) rises by 10. But every value moves together, so the distances between values (the spread) don’t change.\nRule: adding a constant changes the center, not the spread. Multiplying by a constant changes both.',
  sta03: 'Answer: 4. A margin of error is how far the range extends on each side of the estimate: 54 − 50 = 4 and 58 − 54 = 4. So it’s ±4 percentage points.\nTrap: 8 is the full width of the range (58 − 50), which is twice the margin of error.',
  sta04: 'Correct: 60. Mean = sum ÷ count, so sum = mean × count = 12 × 5 = 60.\nWhy not the others: 17 adds 12 + 5; 12 is the mean itself; 2.4 divides 12 by 5.',
  sta05: 'True. The mean uses the actual size of every value, so one very large value pulls it up a lot. The median only depends on the middle position, which moves over by at most half a spot. Example: 1, 2, 3 has mean 2 and median 2; add 100, and the mean becomes 26.5 while the median is only 2.5.',
  sta06: 'Correct: −5. Residual = actual − predicted = 35 − 40 = −5. A negative residual means the actual point is below the line of best fit, so the line overpredicted.\nTrap: 5 reverses the subtraction (predicted − actual).',
  sta07: 'Mean is the average (sum ÷ count). Median is the middle value when the data are in order. Range is the maximum minus the minimum. Standard deviation is the typical distance of values from the mean.\nTip: mean and median describe the center of the data; range and standard deviation describe how spread out it is.',
  sta08: 'Correct: the sets have the same mean, but Set B has a greater standard deviation. Set A’s mean is 10, and Set B’s mean is (4 + 8 + 12 + 16) ÷ 4 = 10. Set A’s values are all exactly 10, so its standard deviation is 0. Set B’s values are spread out, so its standard deviation is larger.',
  sta09: 'Answer: 8. Mean = sum ÷ count. Sum: 7 + 9 + 10 + 6 + 8 = 40. Count: 5. Mean: 40 ÷ 5 = 8.',
  sta10: 'Correct: the sample includes only drama club members, so it may not represent all students. Drama club members probably care more about the arts budget than typical students do, so their opinions could differ from the whole school’s.\nWhy not the others: 50 can be a reasonable sample size; random selection is good, not a problem; and drama club members can certainly have opinions.\nRule: random samples represent only the group they were drawn from.',
  sta11: 'Answer: 60. 25% of 80 is 0.25 × 80 = 20, so the sale price is 80 − 20 = 60.\nShortcut: 25% off means you pay 75%, and 0.75 × 80 = 60.',
  adv01: 'Answer: 15. Replace x with 3: f(3) = 2(3)² − 3. Square first: 3² = 9. Then 2 × 9 = 18, and 18 − 3 = 15.\nTrap: (2 × 3)² = 36 squares before multiplying in the wrong order. Exponents come before multiplication.',
  adv02: 'Correct: 2 and 3. Factor: find two numbers that multiply to 6 and add to −5, which are −2 and −3. So x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0, giving x = 2 or x = 3.\nWhy not the others: −2 and −3 are the numbers inside the factors, not the solutions (the signs flip). 1 and 6 multiply to 6 but add to 7.',
  adv03: 'Correct: x⁷. When multiplying powers with the same base, add the exponents: x³ · x⁴ = x³⁺⁴ = x⁷.\nWhy not the others: x¹² multiplies the exponents (that’s for a power of a power, like (x³)⁴); 2x⁷ adds a coefficient that isn’t there; x³⁄⁴ divides them.',
  adv04: 'Answer: 5. Set the function equal to 11: 3x − 4 = 11. Add 4: 3x = 15. Divide by 3: x = 5.\nCheck: f(5) = 3(5) − 4 = 11 ✓.',
  adv05: 'True. A parabola in vertex form, y = a(x − h)² + k, has its vertex at (h, k). Here h = 2 and k = 1, so the vertex is (2, 1).\nTrap: the sign inside the parentheses flips. (x − 2) means h = +2, not −2.',
  adv06: 'Correct: 500 · 2ᵗ. Starting at 500, doubling once gives 500 · 2; twice gives 500 · 2 · 2 = 500 · 2²; after t hours, 500 · 2ᵗ.\nWhy not the others: 500 · t² grows by squaring time, not by doubling; 500 + 2t adds only 2 per hour (linear); 2 · 500ᵗ raises the starting amount to a power.\nRule: exponential growth = starting amount × (growth factor)^time.',
  adv07: 'x⁰ = 1 (any nonzero number to the zero power is 1). x⁻² = 1/x² (a negative exponent means take the reciprocal). x^(1/2) = √x (a power of 1/2 is a square root). (2x)² = 4x² (square both the 2 and the x).\nTrap: (2x)² is not 2x², because the exponent applies to everything inside the parentheses.',
  adv08: 'Correct: 0. x² is never negative, so x² + 4 is always at least 4. The graph never reaches y = 0, so it never touches the x-axis.\nCheck with the discriminant: b² − 4ac = 0 − 16 < 0, so there are no real solutions.',
  geo01: 'Answer: 10. Use the Pythagorean theorem: a² + b² = c². 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100, and √100 = 10.\nShortcut: 6-8-10 is the 3-4-5 triple doubled.',
  geo02: 'Correct: 70°. The angles of a triangle add up to 180°: 180 − 50 − 60 = 70.\nWhy not the others: 90° assumes a right triangle; 110° is 50 + 60; 60° repeats a given angle.',
  geo03: 'Answer: 9. Area of a circle = πr². With r = 3, area = π · 3² = 9π, so k = 9.\nTrap: 6 comes from 2r (that’s the diameter), and 6π would be the circumference formula 2πr.',
  geo04: 'Correct: 6. Perimeter = 2(length) + 2(width): 30 = 2(9) + 2w = 18 + 2w. Subtract 18: 2w = 12, so w = 6.\nWhy not the others: 12 is 2w (forgot to divide by 2); 21 is 30 − 9; 3 is 12 − 9.',
  geo05: 'True. In a 30-60-90 triangle, the sides are in the ratio 1 : √3 : 2. The side opposite 30° is the shortest (1), and the hypotenuse is 2, so the short side is exactly half the hypotenuse.',
  geo06: 'Correct: equal. When two lines cross, they form two pairs of vertical angles (angles directly across from each other). Each pair is always equal.\nWhy not the others: supplementary angles add to 180° (those are angles next to each other on a straight line); complementary angles add to 90°; vertical angles are only 90° if the lines are perpendicular.',
  geo07: 'Answer: 64. Volume of a cube = side × side × side = 4 × 4 × 4 = 64.\nTrap: 16 is the area of one face (4 × 4), not the volume. For volume, multiply all three dimensions.',
  geo08: 'Correct: 4/5. sin = opposite ÷ hypotenuse (SOH). The side opposite angle A is BC = 4, and the hypotenuse is AB = 5, so sin A = 4/5.\nWhy not the others: 3/5 is cos A (adjacent AC = 3 over hypotenuse); 3/4 is tan B or 1/tan A; 5/4 is upside down.',
  /* ================================================================ questions-pt1.js */
  pt15: 'Correct: That said. The first sentence describes big hopes (fusion could supply abundant clean energy). The second describes a disappointing fact (until 2022, no experiment produced more energy than it used). Hope followed by a limitation is a contrast, and “That said” means “even so / however.”\nWhy not the others: “For this reason” would mean the hopes caused the failures; “Moreover” would add another point in the same direction; “Specifically” would give a detail of the first sentence.',
  pt16: 'Correct: alternatively. Most females laid eggs in other females’ nests. This one female did something different: she visited a few nests but then raised her own eggs. “Alternatively” introduces a different option or behavior.\nWhy not the others: “for example” and “in particular” would make her an example of the egg-dumping pattern, which she isn’t; “similarly” would say she behaved the same way.',
  pt17: 'Correct: “Like wolves, dog breeds with erect ears can make the ‘ears rotator’ movement, while breeds with floppy or semi-floppy ears cannot.” The goal is to compare two groups of dog breeds, so the answer must name both groups and show how they differ.\nWhy not the others: the second choice reverses the facts (it’s the erect-eared breeds that can do it); the third only says a study happened; the fourth mentions only wolves.',
  pt18: 'Correct: Additionally. The first sentence praises the book’s writing; the second names another strength (dozens of high-quality images). Adding a second point of the same kind calls for an addition transition.\nWhy not the others: “As a result” would mean the writing caused the images; “Even so” would signal a contrast; “Specifically” would mean the images are a more detailed version of the writing, but they’re a separate feature.',
  pt19: 'Correct: What’s more. The first sentence gives one theme of the poem (time and identity); the second adds a separate theme (poetry and other art forms). “What’s more” adds another point.\nWhy not the others: “In other words” would restate the first idea; “For instance” would give an example of it; “Conversely” would set up an opposite idea.',
  pt20: 'Correct: the choice describing collecting texts from many regions, translating them into Arabic, and copying them onto paper. The goal asks how the House of Wisdom preserved knowledge, so the answer must explain the process.\nWhy not the others: the second just says what the House of Wisdom was; the third and fourth each mention one detail without explaining how preservation happened.',
  pt21: 'Correct: as the gateway to the countryside, the station suggests to her the deeper, non-material riches of country life. Text 2’s critic says the novel contrasts money-minded London with an idealized countryside reached by train from King’s Cross. So Margaret’s sense of “something infinite” there fits the critic’s reading: the station points toward the countryside’s deeper values.\nWhy not the others: they make the station negative or materialistic, the opposite of Margaret’s feeling, or bring in noise and crowds, which neither text mentions.',
  pt22: 'Correct: it describes a natural phenomenon, rejects two possible explanations, and then offers a third that the researchers find likely. The phenomenon is Gault shedding dust; rejected explanation one is ice (it has none); rejected explanation two is a collision (the bursts repeated); and the favored explanation is fast spinning.\nWhy not the others: no earlier observations are discredited, an explanation is offered, and no other comets are compared.',
  pt23: 'Correct: it points out a conflict between research findings and recent events, which the text then explains with a complicating factor in the data. Studies said spending cuts don’t hurt governments; “Yet” introduces governments that lost power after cuts anyway. The last sentence resolves this: the studies missed the risk because governments timed cuts after elections.\nWhy not the others: no one is accused of dishonesty, the disagreement isn’t described as long-standing, and the sentence challenges the earlier studies instead of supporting them.',
  pt24: 'Correct: inconsequential to. Living far from the centers of scholarship might suggest his work didn’t matter to other philosophers. The research “undermined” that claim, and the text adds that his ideas were “widely read and debated.” So the undermined claim is that his works were inconsequential (unimportant) to them.\nWhy not the others: “controversial among” and “widely debated” fit together, so research wouldn’t undermine that; “hostile toward” and “imitative of” aren’t contradicted by being widely read.',
  pt25: 'Correct: negligible. The study challenges an old view, and the new evidence (up to half of those buried with hunting tools were women) shows women hunted a lot. So the old view must have been that their role was negligible (so small it didn’t matter).\nWhy not the others: “commonplace” is what the new evidence supports, not the view it challenges; “inevitable” and “celebrated” don’t match evidence about how often women hunted.',
  pt26: 'Correct: proxies for. A proxy is a stand-in for something you can’t observe directly. Because the meteorites’ chemistry matches Mars, it’s tempting to use them as stand-ins for the planet’s interior, but researchers warn a few rocks can’t represent all of Mars.\nWhy not the others: contrivances are artificial schemes; catalysts cause change; deterrents discourage action. None describes using rocks to represent a planet.',
  pt27: 'Correct: dogmatic. The editor gave writers freedom; her rivals “took the opposite approach.” The opposite of letting writers use their own voices is strictly imposing your own views, which is what dogmatic means.\nWhy not the others: tentative (hesitant, uncertain) and haphazard (random) aren’t the opposite of hands-off editing; generous is closer to how the first editor behaved.',
  pt28: 'Correct: it presents a production method, identifies two ways of carrying it out, and notes an advantage of each. The method is batik (wax blocks dye); the two ways are the canting (hand-drawn) and the cap (stamp); and the advantages are speed for the cap and one-of-a-kind pieces for the canting.\nWhy not the others: the text doesn’t discuss batik’s origins, a revival, or an argument about recognition.',
  pt29: 'Correct: the claim is likely correct, but the later genetic markers support it better than the original mutation did. Text 2 first says the mutation alone proves little (it’s found worldwide), but then reports markers shared only with Polynesian breeds. So Text 2 agrees with Text 1’s conclusion while finding better evidence for it.\nWhy not the others: Text 2 doesn’t end by calling the claim wrong, doesn’t reverse the direction of travel, and doesn’t say European chickens arrived first.',
  pt30: 'Correct: notional. Notional means existing only as an idea or theory. Before researchers claimed to find remnants of Theia, it was purely theoretical, with no physical evidence.\nWhy not the others: spurious means false or fake, which goes too far (Theia wasn’t shown to be false); obsolete means outdated; inevitable means certain to happen.',
  pt31: 'Correct: semicolon. “That the old lighthouse should be saved was agreed on by everyone at the meeting” is a complete sentence (its subject is the clause “That the old lighthouse should be saved”). “Deciding who would pay … proved far more contentious” is also complete. A semicolon joins two complete sentences.\nWhy not the others: a comma alone creates a comma splice; a colon would mean the second part explains the first, which it doesn’t (it contrasts); no punctuation creates a run-on.',
  pt32: 'Correct: identifies. The subject is “analysis,” which is singular, so the verb must be singular. “The researchers’” and “of more than 200 national constitutions” describe the analysis but aren’t the subject. The next sentence uses present tense (“are now used”), so present tense fits.\nWhy not the others: identify and have identified are plural, matching the trap nouns; were identifying is plural and past.',
  pt33: 'Correct: “findings from the study suggest that grains and vegetables, not meat, made up most people’s diets.” The opening phrase, “Supported by chemical analyses of more than 2,000 medieval skeletons,” must describe whatever comes right after the comma. Findings can be supported by analyses.\nWhy not the others: grains and vegetables, diets, and researchers can’t be “supported by chemical analyses.” Those choices create dangling modifiers.',
  pt34: 'Correct: colon. “The garden is planted to bloom in every season” is a complete sentence, and what follows explains it with specific examples (snowdrops in February, roses in June, asters in October). A colon introduces an explanation or list after a complete sentence.\nWhy not the others: no punctuation creates a run-on; “while” and “, so” create illogical relationships (the list isn’t a contrast or a result).',
  pt35: 'Correct: comma. “Rarely performed during her lifetime” is extra information between the subject (“symphonies”) and the verb (“have found”). It closes with a comma before “have,” so it must open with a comma too.\nWhy not the others: adding “were” or “had been” would turn the phrase into a second main verb, leaving “have recently found” with no proper place in the sentence.',
  pt36: 'Correct: “on her own, however;”. Both halves are complete sentences, so they need a semicolon between them. “However” here belongs to the first clause (“The director doesn’t decide … on her own, however”), set off by a comma before it, and the semicolon then separates the two clauses.\nWhy not the others: “; however” with no comma after it leaves however unpunctuated; “, however,” uses only commas between two complete sentences (comma splice); “however;” without a comma before it doesn’t set off however.',
  pt37: 'Correct: “pine trees and a fungus trade nutrients through their tangled roots and threads.” The opening phrase, “Forming vast underground networks through a partnership…,” describes whoever comes right after the comma. The trees and fungus are the ones forming the networks.\nWhy not the others: nutrients, “the trading,” and “it” can’t form networks through a partnership. Those choices create dangling modifiers.',
  pt38: 'Correct: no punctuation. There are three Baltic states (Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania). Since the students are Estonian, “neighboring Baltic states” needs the names to tell you which ones, so they’re essential and get no punctuation.\nWhy not the others: a comma or “which are” would treat the names as extra information, implying Latvia and Lithuania are all of the Baltic states; a semicolon would need a complete sentence after it.',
  pt39: 'Correct: “microsculptures. Creations”. The first sentence ends after “microsculptures.” The rest (“Creations so small … his sculptures are carved from materials such as grains of sand”) is a second complete sentence, so a period is needed.\nWhy not the others: a comma creates a comma splice; “and” or no punctuation would create a run-on with an unclear structure.',
  pt40: 'Correct: “, though;”. This works like the museum director question. Both halves are complete sentences, so a semicolon goes between them. “Though” belongs at the end of the first clause and is set off with a comma before it.\nWhy not the others: “; though” attaches though to the second clause awkwardly; “, though,” uses only commas between two complete sentences (comma splice); “though;” is missing the comma that sets it off.',
  pt41: 'Answer: 210.\nStep 1: substitute x = 90: 0.10(90) + 0.20y = 0.17(90 + y).\nStep 2: simplify: 9 + 0.20y = 15.3 + 0.17y.\nStep 3: subtract 0.17y and 9 from both sides: 0.03y = 6.3.\nStep 4: divide: y = 6.3 ÷ 0.03 = 210.',
  pt42: 'Correct: x − y = 20. A system has exactly one solution when the two lines have different slopes (they cross once). x + y = 20 has slope −1; x − y = 20 has slope +1, so they cross.\nWhy not the others: 3x + 3y = 60 simplifies to x + y = 20, the same line (infinitely many solutions). x + y = 15 and 2x + 2y = 10 (which is x + y = 5) are parallel to the first line (no solutions).',
  pt43: 'Correct: 3.78. In a linear function, the change in output equals the slope times the change in input. The slope here is 9/5, so the Fahrenheit change is (9/5)(2.10) = 3.78. The −273.15 and +32 are shifts; they don’t affect how much the output changes.\nWhy not the others: 2.10 assumes the scales change equally; 1.17 multiplies by 5/9 instead of 9/5; 35.78 adds 32.',
  pt44: 'Correct: the total cups of broth in the large jars. Each large jar holds 5 cups, and there are y large jars, so 5 × y is the total amount of broth in all the large jars.\nWhy not the others: y alone is the number of large jars; 5 alone is the cups in one large jar; x + y would be the total number of jars.',
  pt45: 'Answer: 37.8 (or 189/5). The y-intercept is where x = 0.\nStep 1: substitute x = 0: 0 = −5y/9 + 21.\nStep 2: add 5y/9 to both sides: 5y/9 = 21.\nStep 3: multiply by 9/5: y = 21 × 9/5 = 189/5 = 37.8.',
  pt46: 'Correct: 61c/4 + 105.\nStep 1: substitute x = c + 7 into g: g(c + 7) = b − 15(c + 7) = b − 15c − 105.\nStep 2: set it equal to c/4: b − 15c − 105 = c/4.\nStep 3: solve for b: b = c/4 + 15c + 105 = c/4 + 60c/4 + 105 = 61c/4 + 105.',
  pt47: 'Correct: 140(x + 3) + 220x ≥ 5,820. Door tickets cost x, and advance tickets cost $3 more, so x + 3. Money from advance tickets: 140(x + 3); from door tickets: 220x. They need at least $5,820, so the total is ≥ 5,820.\nWhy not the others: x − 3 makes advance tickets cheaper; ≤ means “at most”; the last choice puts the extra $3 on the wrong group.',
  pt48: 'Correct: −15. The graph shows a dashed line at y = −4 with shading above, so the solutions are y > −4. The given form is ry < 60. To turn y > −4 into that form, multiply both sides by −15, which flips the inequality: −15y < 60. So r = −15.\nCheck: dividing −15y < 60 by −15 (and flipping) gives y > −4 ✓.\nTrap: 15 gives 15y < 60, so y < 4, which is the wrong boundary and direction.',
  pt49: 'Answer: −12.\nStep 1: add the functions: h(x) = (x/4 − 9) + (3x/4 + 21) = x/4 + 3x/4 + 12 = x + 12.\nStep 2: the x-intercept is where h(x) = 0: x + 12 = 0, so x = −12.',
  pt50: 'Correct: 5.\nStep 1: write the equations: Store A: 5.5r + 3b = 37. Store B: 6.5r + 8b = 66.\nStep 2: solve. Testing the choices is quickest. If b = 5, Store A gives 5.5r = 37 − 15 = 22, so r = 4. Check Store B: 6.5(4) + 8(5) = 26 + 40 = 66 ✓.\nSo the order has 5 pints of blackberries.',
  pt51: 'Correct: 5x² − 14x + 49 = 0. A quadratic has no real solutions when its discriminant (b² − 4ac) is negative. Here: (−14)² − 4(5)(49) = 196 − 980 = −784 < 0.\nWhy not the others: x² − 6x + 9 gives 36 − 36 = 0 (one solution); 2x² + 5x − 3 gives 25 + 24 = 49 (two solutions); x² − 10x + 16 gives 100 − 64 = 36 (two solutions).',
  pt52: 'Correct: 3 + 2(55/4)². f(t) = −2t² + 55t is a downward parabola, so its maximum is at the vertex t = −b/(2a) = −55/(−4) = 55/4. Plug in: f(55/4) = 55(55/4) − 2(55/4)². Since 55 = 4 × (55/4), the first term is 4(55/4)², so f = 4(55/4)² − 2(55/4)² = 2(55/4)². g adds 3, so the maximum of g is 3 + 2(55/4)².',
  pt53: 'Correct: f(x) = −6(x − 5)² + 24. Vertex form, a(x − h)² + k, shows the maximum (or minimum) value directly as k. The vertex is at x = −60/(2 · −6) = 5, and f(5) = −6(25) + 300 − 126 = 24, so the maximum is 24.\nWhy not the others: the factored form −6(x − 3)(x − 7) shows the zeros (3 and 7), not the maximum; −2(3x² − 30x + 63) just factors out −2; −6(x + 5)² + 24 has the wrong vertex and isn’t equivalent.',
  pt54: 'Correct: (−7/4, 0). A parabola is symmetric about the vertical line through its vertex, x = −3. The x-intercept −17/4 = −4.25 is 1.25 (5/4) to the left of −3. The other intercept is the same distance to the right: −3 + 5/4 = −7/4.\nWhy not the others: 7/4 and 17/4 flip signs incorrectly; −11/4 uses the wrong distance.',
  pt55: 'Answer: 7. Revenue is a downward-opening parabola (the x² coefficient is negative), so it’s greatest at the vertex. The vertex x-value is −b/(2a) = −1,400/(2 × −100) = −1,400/(−200) = 7.\nMeaning: raising the price by $7 maximizes revenue.',
  pt56: 'Answer: 29. The quadratic formula gives x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a). Here a = 1, b = 7, c = 5, so x = (−7 ± √(49 − 20))/2 = (−7 ± √29)/2. Matching the form (−7 + √k)/2, k = 29.',
  pt57: 'Correct: −6.\nStep 1: set the two expressions for y equal: x + 9 = x² + 16x + 63.\nStep 2: move everything to one side: 0 = x² + 15x + 54.\nStep 3: factor: (x + 6)(x + 9) = 0, so x = −6 or x = −9.\nThe greater value is −6 (since −6 > −9).\nTrap: 6 and 9 drop the negative signs.',
  pt58: 'Correct: h(x) = 17(2)ˣ. h(x) = f(x − 4) = 272 · 2^(x − 4). Use the exponent rule 2^(x − 4) = 2ˣ ÷ 2⁴ = 2ˣ ÷ 16. So h(x) = (272 ÷ 16) · 2ˣ = 17 · 2ˣ.\nWhy not the others: 268 subtracts 4 from 272; “− 4” at the end shifts down instead of right; 4,352 multiplies by 16 instead of dividing.',
  pt59: 'Correct: zero. An absolute value measures distance from zero, so it’s never negative. |4x − 3| can be 0 or positive, but it can never equal −9.\nTip: whenever an absolute value is set equal to a negative number, there are no solutions.',
  pt60: 'Answer: 336.\nStep 1: the graph passes through (24, 0), so g(24) = 0: (24 + 14)(t − 24) = 38(t − 24) = 0. Since 38 ≠ 0, t − 24 = 0, so t = 24.\nStep 2: g(0) = (0 + 14)(24 − 0) = 14 × 24 = 336.',
  pt61: 'Answer: 14. The maximum is the largest value that actually appears in the data. Read the value column, not the frequency column: 14 has a frequency of 6, so it appears in the data, and no larger value is listed.\nTraps: 11 is the largest frequency (how often 11 appears); 13 has a frequency of 0, so it never appears.',
  pt62: 'Correct: B has the greater median, and the ranges are equal. Adding 46 to every value shifts the whole data set up, so the median rises from 23 to 69. The distance between the largest and smallest values doesn’t change: A’s range is 26 − 22 = 4, and B’s is 72 − 68 = 4.\nRule: adding a constant changes the center (mean, median) but not the spread (range, standard deviation).',
  pt63: 'Correct: 1.07. A 7% increase means the new amount is the old amount plus 7% of it: 100% + 7% = 107% = 1.07. So new = 1.07 × old.\nWhy not the others: 0.07 is only the increase; 7 and 1.7 misplace the decimal.',
  pt64: 'Correct: 8%. The margin of error is how far the range extends on each side of the sample result: 32 − 24 = 8 and 40 − 32 = 8, so it’s ±8%.\nWhy not the others: 16% is the full width of the range (twice the margin); 32% is the sample result itself.',
  pt66: 'Correct: −0.17. Slope = change in y ÷ change in x = (12.7 − 21.8) ÷ (70 − 15) = −9.1 ÷ 55 ≈ −0.165, about −0.17 degrees per meter of depth.\nWhy not the others: the larger slopes would mean temperature drops 2 to 8 degrees per meter, far too steep for a drop of only 9.1 degrees over 55 meters.',
  pt67: 'Answer: 52. “13 is p% of 25” means 13 = (p/100) × 25. Divide: 13 ÷ 25 = 0.52, which is 52%.\nCheck: 52% of 25 = 0.52 × 25 = 13 ✓.',
  pt68: 'Answer: 6. The line predicts a greater y-value than the actual one when the actual point is below the line (the line is too high there). Six points lie below the line.\nTip: residual = actual − predicted. Points below the line have negative residuals, meaning the line overpredicts.',
  pt69: 'Correct: they are equal. Standard deviation measures spread, how far values are from the mean. Adding 13 to every value moves the whole plot to the right, but the values are still the same distances apart, so the spread doesn’t change.\nRule: adding a constant to every value changes the mean, not the standard deviation.',
  /* ================================================================ questions-more.js (hand-written; g-* explanations are generated) */
  'm-wic01': 'Correct: clear. The colon introduces the evidence: even students with no physics background could follow every step. An explanation anyone can follow is clear, which is what lucid means here.\nWhy not the others: “bright” is another meaning of lucid (light-filled) that doesn’t fit an explanation; lengthy and playful don’t explain why beginners could follow it.',
  'm-wic02': 'Correct: resenting. “Rather than” introduces the opposite of what actually happened. The director thanked the critic, so the opposite reaction is resenting (feeling bitter about) the review.\nWhy not the others: welcoming is close to what she actually did; publishing and summarizing aren’t opposites of thanking someone.',
  'm-wic03': 'Correct: precarious. The semicolon introduces an explanation: one dry summer could leave people without enough water. A supply that could fail that easily is dangerously unstable, which is what precarious means.\nWhy not the others: plentiful is the opposite; ancient and decorative don’t describe a supply that could run out.',
  'm-wic04': 'Correct: lessen. The diplomat chose words carefully to calm things down, so to temper the tension means to lessen or soften it.\nWhy not the others: anger comes from the noun meaning (“she lost her temper”), which doesn’t fit a verb here; harden is the opposite; measure doesn’t fit easing tension.',
  'm-wic05': 'Correct: accelerates. “Although its first chapters are slow” sets up a contrast, so the second half must be fast. “Builds quickly” confirms that the pace speeds up.\nWhy not the others: stalls and retreats mean slowing or going backward, the opposite; concludes doesn’t describe pace.',
  'm-wic06': 'Correct: unoriginal. The critics noted that nearly every song echoed the style of earlier groups, meaning the album copied what came before. Derivative here means unoriginal.\nWhy not the others: financial and calculated come from other uses of the word (finance, calculus); complex doesn’t fit songs that copy others.',
  'm-wic07': 'Correct: nuanced. The documentary shows the musician’s failures and doubts along with her triumphs, so its portrait is balanced and shows subtle complexity, which is what nuanced means.\nWhy not the others: flattering would show only the good parts; brief and fictional don’t describe showing both sides.',
  'm-wic08': 'True. To “bear out” a theory is to confirm it, to show it’s true. The evidence did not bear it out (didn’t confirm it), which is why the researchers had to go back to the lab.',
  'm-tsp01': 'Correct: to present a puzzle about octopus intelligence that challenges an earlier assumption. The text lists impressive abilities, adds facts that don’t fit (short lives, no parental care), and says this puzzles biologists who linked intelligence to long lives and learning from parents.\nWhy not the others: there’s no comparison with mammals, no explanation of how escapes work, and the text says octopuses get no parental care.',
  'm-tsp02': 'Correct: it presents evidence that undercuts a common approach, setting up the alternative described next. The first sentence describes the common approach (more parking); “however” introduces evidence that more parking draws more cars; and the last sentence offers the opposite approach.\nWhy not the others: no specific city is named, the recommendation comes in the next sentence, and preferences for driving aren’t explained.',
  'm-tsp03': 'Correct: the study sentence (“In one study, children who took a year of piano lessons improved more…”). Evidence is the data or finding that backs up a claim.\nWhy not the others: the first sentence is the claim itself; the third offers a possible explanation (why it might work); the fourth is a caution about the evidence.',
  'm-tsp04': 'Order: misconception corrected (“not a solid island of trash”) → what it really is (“Instead, … tiny pieces of plastic”) → a consequence (“Because the pieces are so small, they are hard to see”) → the bigger result (“This makes the patch difficult to measure and … clean up”).\nClues: “Instead” must follow the “not” sentence, “the pieces” needs the pieces to be introduced first, and “This” refers back to being hard to see.',
  'm-tsp05': 'Correct: it describes an earlier view, presents findings that overturned it, and explains why those findings mattered. Early maps showed a flat floor (earlier view); 1950s sonar revealed mountains and trenches (new findings); this helped develop plate tectonics (why it mattered).\nWhy not the others: there’s only one theory, no steps of mapmaking, and no criticism of early mapmakers.',
  'm-tsp06': 'To concede is to admit a point to the other side. To speculate is to suggest a possibility without proof. To contrast is to show how two things differ. To dismiss is to reject something as unimportant.\nTip: answer choices often hinge on these verbs. Check that the text actually does what the verb says.',
  'm-tsp07': 'True. When a text walks through steps in the order they happen (first, next, then, finally), its structure is sequential, a sequence of steps.',
  'm-ctc01': 'Correct: the claim may hold for older students but is not supported for young children. Text 1 says homework helps young children. Text 2 found little effect for elementary students but some benefit for high schoolers. So Text 2 limits the claim instead of rejecting homework entirely.\nWhy not the others: Text 2 never says homework harms students, doesn’t recommend more for young children, and doesn’t fully support Text 1.',
  'm-ctc02': 'Correct: it offers evidence that challenges the older view described in Text 1. Text 1 describes the old picture of dinosaurs as slow and cold-blooded. Text 2’s growth rings show fast growth, a pattern of warm-blooded animals, which cuts against that picture.\nWhy not the others: Text 2 doesn’t support or repeat Text 1, and it’s about the same animals (dinosaurs).',
  'm-ctc03': 'Correct: pesticides play some role in the decline of wild bees. Text 1 says pesticides are the main cause; Text 2 “agrees that pesticides harm bees” but thinks habitat loss is bigger. Both accept that pesticides play some role.\nWhy not the others: only Text 2 says habitat loss is the main cause; neither says populations are growing; neither says pesticides have no effect.',
  'm-ctc04': 'True. To qualify a claim is to accept it with limits or conditions: “That’s true, but only for older students.” It isn’t a full rejection and it isn’t full agreement.',
  'm-ctc05': 'Correct: the difference may come from how people tend to read on screens, not from the screen itself. In Text 2, when screen readers slowed down and read carefully, the gap with paper disappeared. That suggests rushed reading habits, not screens themselves, caused Text 1’s findings.\nWhy not the others: Text 2 shows the effect can disappear, so it isn’t permanent damage; paper isn’t “always better”; and nothing says the earlier studies used the wrong texts.',
  'm-ctc06': 'Correct: the ending fits the concerns the novel develops throughout. Text 1 calls the unhappy ending a flaw that spoils a warm story. Text 2 says loss runs through every chapter, so the sad ending is consistent with the whole book: it’s “earned.”\nWhy not the others: Text 2 defends the ending, so it wouldn’t want it happier; it doesn’t deny the story is warm; and authorship never comes up.',
  'm-cid01': 'Correct: their low-oxygen surroundings prevent rot. The last sentence explains the cause: the water and mud around the pilings have very little oxygen, so rot-causing organisms can’t survive.\nWhy not the others: paint and replacement are never mentioned, and the text says the pilings are wooden, not stone.',
  'm-cid02': 'Correct: deserts are defined by low precipitation rather than by heat. The text corrects a common assumption (deserts are hot) using Antarctica, a cold desert, and the last sentence states the main idea directly: precipitation, not temperature, makes a desert.\nWhy not the others: Antarctica is the coldest place, not the hottest; the text doesn’t say most deserts are near the poles; and deserts get little precipitation, not heavy snowfall.',
  'm-cid03': 'Correct: the last sentence. The first three sentences give details (floating gardens, rafts that rise with floods, rafts becoming compost). The last sentence states what they show: a traditional method turning floodwater into an advantage.\nRule: the central idea is the point all the details support.',
  'm-cid04': 'Supported: 70% is most of those surveyed; younger visitors were “especially likely” to choose interactive exhibits; and 100% − 70% = 30%, which is fewer than a third (33%) naming something else.\nNot supported: the survey doesn’t recommend replacing paintings, doesn’t measure learning, and “especially likely” doesn’t mean every visitor under 18.',
  'm-cid05': 'Correct: he is determined to get his grandmother’s recipe right. After a lopsided loaf, Jonah pulls out her recipe card, finds the step he skipped, and starts measuring again. Trying again carefully shows determination.\nWhy not the others: he doesn’t give up; using her handwritten card shows he values her recipes; and skipping a step doesn’t mean he’s never baked.',
  'm-cid06': 'False. The text says the hours were extended in March and visitors rose in April. It gives two facts in time order but never says one caused the other; other things could explain the increase.\nRule: events happening one after another doesn’t prove cause and effect.',
  'm-cid07': 'Correct: to conserve energy on cold nights. The second sentence states the purpose: torpor saves energy the tiny birds would otherwise burn just to stay warm.\nWhy not the others: migration, attracting mates, and digestion are never mentioned. In fact, the birds stop feeding during torpor and resume in the morning.',
  'm-inf01': 'Correct: demand for the muffins was greater than the bakery’s original supply. The bakery sold out daily, and even after doubling production it still sold out. That means people wanted more muffins than it first made.\nWhy not the others: other baked goods, prices, and future plans aren’t mentioned. Those choices add outside information.',
  'm-inf02': 'Correct: the presence of predators may make small birds more cautious while feeding. The feeders were identical, so the main difference between the parks is the number of hawks. Small birds spent less time at feeders where hawks were common, which suggests caution.\nWhy not the others: hawks eating seeds, preferring one park’s seeds, and overall bird numbers aren’t supported. The feeders and seeds were the same.',
  'm-inf03': 'Correct: is not in. Every robotics club member takes physics. Maria isn’t taking physics, so she can’t be a club member.\nWhy not the others: “is in,” “leads,” and “might lead” all require her to be in the club, which the logic rules out.\nRule: if every A is a B, then anything that isn’t a B can’t be an A.',
  'm-inf04': 'Correct: the two landmasses were once connected or much closer together. The same freshwater reptile appears on both continents, but it couldn’t have swum across salt water. So the land itself must have been joined when the animal lived (this was early evidence for continental drift).\nWhy not the others: nothing suggests the reptile could fly; it was found in both places, not only Africa; and fresh-water oceans aren’t supported.',
  'm-inf05': 'Supported: the group’s average score rose; the study group may have helped (a cautious claim); and the change was measured over one semester.\nGoes too far: one school doesn’t show study groups always raise scores; an average can rise even if some students did worse, so not every student necessarily improved; and other factors could have contributed, so it may not be the only reason.',
  'm-inf06': 'Correct: differences between the two varieties themselves. The plants shared the same greenhouse, soil, water, and light. When every condition is the same, the remaining difference, the varieties, is the likely cause.\nWhy not the others: temperature and water were the same for both (same greenhouse, same water); which gardener tended them isn’t mentioned.',
  'm-inf07': 'False. “Most” means more than half, which leaves room for exceptions. “All” means no exceptions. Jumping from “most” to “all” is a classic overreach trap.',
  'm-coe01': 'Correct: a week after a lecture, students who handwrote notes answered more questions correctly than students who typed. The claim compares memory between handwriting and typing, and this finding measures memory in both groups.\nWhy not the others: typing speed, laptop preference, and note length don’t measure what students remember.',
  'm-coe02': 'Correct: accidents fell by the same amount at nearby intersections that got no new lights. The town says the light caused the drop. If accidents dropped just as much without new lights, something else (like weather or enforcement) probably caused it.\nWhy not the others: drivers liking the light, its cost, and how busy the intersection is don’t affect whether the light reduced accidents.',
  'm-coe03': 'Correct: the quotation where the narrator says “I remember that day perfectly” while his sister and his own diary disagree. An unreliable narrator is one whose account can’t be fully trusted. Here, the narrator’s confident memory conflicts with two other sources.\nWhy not the others: the house, the sister’s age, and the move are plain facts that don’t suggest the narrator is wrong.',
  'm-coe04': 'Supports: tree-lined streets were 3°C cooler than similar streets; temperatures fell after trees were planted while nearby streets didn’t change; shaded sidewalks were cooler on the same block.\nWeakens: being next to a large lake offers another explanation for cooler readings; temperatures rising equally with or without trees shows no effect; and measuring only on cloudy days could make the readings misleading.',
  'm-coe05': 'Correct: moss given more water grew faster even when its sunlight was kept the same. The claim says only sunlight matters. Showing that water also changes growth, with sunlight held constant, proves another factor matters.\nWhy not the others: sun vs. shade and slow winter growth are consistent with sunlight mattering; where the moss is found doesn’t address growth.',
  'm-coe06': 'True. A claim that X is “more effective than” Y is a comparison, so the evidence must measure both X and Y. Evidence about X alone can’t show it beats Y.',
  'm-coe07': 'Correct: participants who walked for 20 minutes reported better moods than participants who sat for 20 minutes. The claim says walking improves mood, and this compares mood after walking with a fair comparison group.\nWhy not the others: how many people walk to work, calories burned, and dog ownership don’t measure mood.',
  'm-qnt01': 'Correct: average sleep falls in every grade, from 7.6 hours in grade 9 to 6.7 hours in grade 12. The claim is about a trend (sleep decreases as grades rise), so the answer must show the decrease across all grades: 7.6 → 7.2 → 6.9 → 6.7.\nWhy not the others: 12th graders sleep the least, not the most; one grade’s value (7.2) doesn’t show a trend; and sleep falls (6.9 → 6.7) from grade 11 to 12, not rises.',
  'm-qnt02': 'Answer: 0.9. 9th graders sleep 7.6 hours and 12th graders sleep 6.7 hours. “How many fewer” asks for the difference: 7.6 − 6.7 = 0.9 hours.',
  'm-qnt03': 'Correct: paper was collected in the greatest amount. Paper’s bar is the tallest (120 kg).\nWhy not the others: metal (30) is less than plastic (45); glass (60) is less than paper (120); and metal, not plastic, is the smallest amount.',
  'm-qnt04': 'True. Spring had 1,200 rentals; twice that is 2,400. Summer had 2,600, which is more than 2,400, so summer was more than twice spring.',
  'm-qnt05': 'Correct: between 93.4°C and 96.7°C. 1,500 m is between the table’s 1,000 m (96.7°C) and 2,000 m (93.4°C) rows. The boiling point drops steadily with altitude, so at 1,500 m it falls between those values.\nWhy not the others: 96.7–100 is the range for 0–1,000 m; exactly 100 is sea level; below 90 would be above 3,000 m.',
  'm-qnt06': 'Correct: middle school fell from 48% to 39%, and high school fell from 35% to 28%. The claim says reading for fun declined for both groups, so the answer must show both declines.\nWhy not the others: comparing the groups in 2023 doesn’t show a decline; the 2015 middle school number alone shows no change; and the high school drop alone leaves out middle school.',
  'm-trn01': 'Correct: However. You’d expect the planet closest to the Sun to be the hottest, but it’s Venus. A surprising fact that goes against expectations is a contrast.\nWhy not the others: therefore would mean Mercury being closest causes Venus to be hottest; similarly and for example would mean the sentences agree.',
  'm-trn02': 'Correct: As a result. The faster oven (cause) lets the shop make twice as many loaves (effect).\nWhy not the others: however signals a contrast; for instance signals an example; likewise signals a similarity.',
  'm-trn03': 'Correct: for example. The first sentence makes a general statement (some frogs freeze almost solid); the wood frog is a specific case of such a frog.\nWhy not the others: however and in contrast would mean the wood frog is an exception, but it fits the pattern; instead would mean it does something different.',
  'm-trn04': 'Correct: Later. She wrote the novel first, then translated it, which is a time sequence.\nWhy not the others: in contrast signals a difference, for instance signals an example, and similarly signals likeness. None describes what happened next.',
  'm-trn05': 'Correct: By contrast. Wall color had no link to scores, while temperature had a clear effect. No effect versus a clear effect is a contrast.\nWhy not the others: likewise would mean temperature also had no effect; in other words would restate the first sentence; for example would make temperature an example of wall color.',
  'm-trn06': 'Correct: In other words. The second sentence explains what “eusocial” means (overlapping generations, shared care, division of labor). Explaining a term restates it in plainer language.\nWhy not the others: however and nevertheless signal contrast; meanwhile signals something happening at the same time.',
  'm-trn07': 'Adds a point: furthermore, moreover, and in addition pile another point on top of the previous one.\nGives an example: for instance, for example, and to illustrate introduce a specific case of the point just made.\nTip: if the next sentence is a new, separate point, use an addition word; if it’s a case of the same point, use an example word.',
  'm-trn08': 'Correct: Nevertheless. The plan was expensive and slow, so you might expect the council to reject it, but they approved it anyway. Doing something despite drawbacks is a contrast.\nWhy not the others: therefore would mean the cost caused the approval; similarly and for instance would mean agreement or an example.',
  'm-syn01': 'Correct: “Mauna Loa is a shield volcano with broad, gentle slopes, whereas Mount St. Helens is a steep, cone-shaped stratovolcano.” The goal is a difference in shape, so the answer needs both volcanoes, both shapes, and a contrast word (whereas).\nWhy not the others: one names only Mauna Loa’s location; one gives a similarity; and one describes only Mount St. Helens.',
  'm-syn02': 'Correct: “Like the ancient Egyptians, ancient Chinese inventors built water clocks to measure time.” A similarity needs both groups and what they share. “Like” signals it.\nWhy not the others: each of the other choices mentions only one group or just defines a water clock.',
  'm-syn03': 'Correct: “Ynes Mexia, a Mexican American botanist who began collecting plants in her fifties, gathered about 145,000 specimens, including roughly 500 species new to science.” An introduction for unfamiliar readers must say who she was and what she achieved.\nWhy not the others: the others leave out who Mexia is or give only one detail.',
  'm-syn04': 'Correct: “Students who chewed gum during the attention test scored 6% higher than students who did not.” Results are what the study found.\nWhy not the others: “two groups of 40 students” and “one group chewed gum” describe the method; “asked whether gum affects attention” is the research question.',
  'm-syn05': 'Correct: “Protected bike lanes tend to increase cycling more than painted lanes do: trips rose 30–40% in the cities that added them but only 5% in the city with painted lanes.” A generalization makes a broad claim (“tend to”) and supports it with data from all the cities.\nWhy not the others: each one states a single fact without drawing a general conclusion.',
  'm-syn06': 'Emphasize a difference → whereas. Emphasize a similarity → likewise. Give an example → for instance. Explain a cause → because.\nTip: spotting the signal word in an answer choice is a quick way to check it against the goal.',
  'm-bnd01': 'Correct: semicolon. “The museum closes at five on weekdays” and “on weekends, it stays open until eight” are both complete sentences, so they need a semicolon (or a period).\nWhy not the others: a comma alone creates a comma splice; no punctuation creates a run-on; and “and” without a comma before it is not standard for joining these two clauses.',
  'm-bnd02': 'Correct: colon. “The recipe calls for three spices” is a complete sentence that announces a list, and the colon introduces that list (cumin, coriander, turmeric).\nWhy not the others: a semicolon can’t introduce a list; no punctuation runs the list into the sentence; and “and” doesn’t make sense before a list of the three spices.',
  'm-bnd03': 'Correct: dash. The interruption (“finished in 1937 after four years of construction”) opens with a dash, so it must close with a dash.\nWhy not the others: a comma would mismatch the opening dash; a semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb (“remains”); no punctuation leaves the interruption unclosed.',
  'm-bnd04': 'Correct: comma. “Because the trail was flooded” is a dependent clause (it can’t stand alone). When it comes first, a comma separates it from the main clause.\nWhy not the others: a semicolon or colon must follow a complete sentence; no punctuation isn’t standard after an introductory dependent clause like this.',
  'm-bnd05': 'Correct: “, so”. Both halves are complete sentences, and “so” shows cause and effect (she wanted to see the exhibit, so she bought tickets). Two complete sentences joined by a conjunction need a comma before it.\nWhy not the others: “so” without a comma is not standard here; a comma alone creates a comma splice; a colon followed by “so” isn’t standard.',
  'm-bnd06': 'Correct: no punctuation. “Gwendolyn Brooks” tells you which poet, so it’s essential information and takes no commas.\nWhy not the others: a comma would wrongly treat the name as extra; a semicolon or colon can’t separate a subject from its name.',
  'm-bnd07': 'Correct: no punctuation. “The book that changed my mind about science” is the full subject, and “was written” is its verb. Never put punctuation between a subject and its verb.\nWhy not the others: a comma, semicolon, or dash would split the subject from the verb. (“That changed my mind about science” is essential, telling which book, so it gets no commas either.)',
  'm-bnd08': 'Correct: “The storm passed quickly; however, the power stayed out for hours.” Both halves are complete sentences, so a semicolon separates them. “However” starts the second clause and is followed by a comma.\nWhy not the others: using only commas creates a comma splice; “quickly however;” puts however in the wrong clause without a comma; and the last choice puts a comma between the subject (“the power”) and its verb.',
  'm-fss01': 'Correct: tells. The subject is “Each,” which is always singular. “Of the paintings in the exhibit” is a prepositional phrase that describes it.\nWhy not the others: tell, are telling, and have told are plural, matching the trap word “paintings.”\nRule: each, every, either, and neither take singular verbs.',
  'm-fss02': 'Correct: grabbed. The other verbs in the sentence, “rang” and “hurried,” are past tense, so this verb must be past tense too.\nWhy not the others: grab and are grabbing are present; will grab is future.',
  'm-fss03': 'Correct: team’s. The uniforms belong to one team, so use the singular possessive: team + ’s.\nWhy not the others: teams is a plain plural; teams’ would mean several teams own the uniforms; team has no possessive.',
  'm-fss04': 'Correct: “Ava felt ready for the exam.” The opening phrase “After studying for weeks” describes whoever comes right after the comma. Ava did the studying, so “Ava” must come next.\nWhy not the others: the exam, Ava’s confidence, and “it” didn’t study. Those choices create dangling modifiers.',
  'm-fss05': 'Correct: their. “Researchers” is plural, so the possessive pronoun is their.\nWhy not the others: its is singular; there refers to a place; they’re means “they are.”',
  'm-fss06': 'Correct: to get. The list is “to stretch, to drink water, and ___,” so the third item should match the “to + verb” form: to get.\nWhy not the others: getting, got, and gets break the parallel pattern.',
  'm-fss07': 'Correct: were. With neither … nor, the verb agrees with the subject closer to it. “Assistants” is closer and plural, so were.\nWhy not the others: was, is, and has been are singular, matching “manager,” the farther subject.',
  'm-fss08': 'True. “Who’s” is a contraction of “who is” (or “who has”): “Who’s coming?” “Whose” shows possession: “Whose book is this?”\nTest: replace the word with “who is.” If the sentence still makes sense, use who’s; if not, use whose.',
  /* ================================================================ questions-genres.js */
  'gn-lit01': 'Correct: he busies himself with small tasks to avoid talking directly about Amara’s news. He doesn’t look up, stirs “a pot that did not need stirring,” and adds “salt it did not need.” These unnecessary actions show he’s keeping his hands busy so he doesn’t have to talk about her leaving.\nWhy not the others: he doesn’t celebrate; the coffee ring shows he did notice the letter; and he never asks her to stay. His last words (“She was usually right”) accept that she’s going.',
  'gn-lit02': 'Correct: the father looked at the letter while Amara was away, even though he hasn’t mentioned it. The letter is still folded the way she left it that morning, but now there’s a coffee ring, a new mark, so someone set a coffee cup on it while she was out. That suggests he sat with the letter without saying so.\nWhy not the others: the ring appeared “now,” after she left, so she didn’t spill coffee that morning; nothing suggests the letter matters less to Amara or that he’ll throw it away.\nTip: in literary passages, small physical details often reveal what characters don’t say.',
  'gn-lit03': 'Correct: she senses her father is upset about her leaving and is trying to reassure him. No one has mentioned distance, so “Six hours away isn’t that far” answers a worry he hasn’t said out loud: that she’s leaving. She’s trying to comfort him.\nWhy not the others: she’s clearly going (she has the acceptance letter); six hours is far, not too close; and her tone is gentle, not angry.',
  'gn-lit04': 'Correct: sad but accepting. “Your mother always said you would leave … She was usually right” admits that Amara is going (acceptance), and bringing up her mother, likely no longer there, adds grief.\nWhy not the others: he doesn’t blame Amara (not bitter or accusing); nothing is cheerful; and he isn’t confused; he states it plainly.',
  'gn-lit05': 'Correct: loving, but strained by a feeling neither of them says outright. Care shows on both sides: he spent hours cooking for her, and she tries to reassure him. But the sadness about her leaving stays unspoken.\nWhy not the others: they clearly care about each other (not distant, uncaring, or hostile), and the scene is full of emotion (not purely practical).',
  'gn-lit06': 'Correct: how much effort the grandmother put into teaching a skill she did not have. The grandmother couldn’t swim, yet she taught confidently. The overdue library book with soft, worn pages and “small, careful notes” shows she studied hard so she could teach.\nWhy not the others: the text says she “could not swim a stroke,” so she wasn’t a competitive swimmer; the narrator did learn; and nothing says she disliked the lake.',
  'gn-lit07': 'Correct: spoke with confidence she had built from study rather than experience. “As if” means she sounded experienced but wasn’t; she couldn’t swim. The library book at the end explains where her confidence came from.\nWhy not the others: there’s no audience to impress; no mention of teaching other children; and her careful notes show she cared a lot.',
  'gn-lit08': 'Correct: it reveals a detail that changes how the reader understands the grandmother’s teaching. Before the last sentence, she seems naturally confident. The hidden library book reveals she worked quietly and hard to sound that way, which changes how we see the earlier scene.\nWhy not the others: the sentence isn’t about the narrator’s current swimming, doesn’t explain why the grandmother never learned, and mentions the library only because of the book.',
  'gn-lit09': 'Correct: admiring and affectionate. The narrator describes the grandmother with tender, specific details (her flowered housedress, the soft pages, her “small, careful notes”) that honor her effort.\nWhy not the others: nothing suggests resentment or mockery, and the careful, loving details are the opposite of neutral and distant.',
  'gn-lit10': 'Correct: he values a fair race more than taking advantage of Leo’s bad luck. Marcus “glanced over” (so he saw Leo’s shoe slipping) and then deliberately “eased off” until Leo recovered. Giving up an advantage on purpose shows sportsmanship.\nWhy not the others: he had just drawn even, so he wasn’t too tired; easing off doesn’t help Leo lose; and glancing over shows he noticed.',
  'gn-lit11': 'Correct: to set up the problem Leo faces during the race. The loose fit and the tissue stuffed in the toes prepare the reader for what happens at the turn: the left shoe slips.\nWhy not the others: the uncle’s gift isn’t criticized; Marcus’s “Nice shoes” comment isn’t explained by the fit; and Leo clearly cares about running.\nTip: when a story mentions a small problem early, it often matters later.',
  'gn-lit12': 'Correct: Marcus’s choice surprised Leo and affected him deeply. Replaying a moment “for weeks” means Leo couldn’t stop thinking about it. His rival’s generosity changed how he saw Marcus.\nWhy not the others: the passage doesn’t say who won; “replay” refers to memory, not video; and nothing suggests anger.',
  'gn-lit13': 'Correct: was guarded and unsure how to read his rival’s comment. Leo always beats Marcus, so “Nice shoes” from him could be friendly or a jab. A simple nod is noncommittal, a way to respond without revealing anything.\nWhy not the others: the race hasn’t started, so he isn’t out of breath; he responds, so he heard; and the shoes are too large, so he isn’t proud of the fit.',
  'gn-lit14': 'True. This is called subtext. Characters often show feelings through what they do (stirring a pot that doesn’t need it, nodding instead of answering) rather than saying them. Literary questions frequently ask what an action implies.',
  'gn-soc01': 'Correct: the sharp drop in the cost of shipping goods. The text says shipping a ton from Buffalo to New York fell from about $100 to about $10, and the next sentence says farmers “could now sell grain in eastern markets at a profit.” Cheaper shipping is what made selling profitable.\nWhy not the others: machinery and Midwest grain prices aren’t mentioned; New York’s growth was a result of the canal, not the reason farmers profited.',
  'gn-soc02': 'Order: the canal linked the Great Lakes to the Hudson River → shipping costs fell sharply → Midwestern farmers could sell grain in the East at a profit → New York City grew into the busiest port.\nWhy: each step causes the next. The connection made shipping cheap, cheap shipping opened eastern markets, and the trade flowing through made New York the busiest port.',
  'gn-soc03': 'Correct: by sharply lowering the cost of moving goods, the Erie Canal transformed trade and settlement. Every detail supports this: costs fell, farmers reached new markets, settlers moved west, and New York grew.\nWhy not the others: construction time isn’t discussed; New York became the busiest port after the canal; and canal travel was faster (weeks → a week or less).',
  'gn-soc04': 'False. The text links the canal to New York’s growth, but it never says the canal was the only reason. A claim with “only” needs the text to rule out every other cause.\nRule: watch for answers that add strong words like only, always, or all that the text doesn’t use.',
  'gn-soc05': 'Correct: each new session requires retrieving earlier learning, which strengthens the memory. The last sentence gives the explanation: spacing forces the brain to retrieve what it learned before, and that effort strengthens the memory.\nWhy not the others: both groups studied for the same total time; tiredness and easier material are never mentioned.',
  'gn-soc06': 'Correct: to rule out the possibility that the spaced group did better simply by studying longer. If the spaced group had studied more, the extra time could explain their better memory. Keeping total time equal means the only difference is spacing, so spacing must be the cause.\nWhy not the others: equal time makes the experiment fair, not unfair; it doesn’t claim time doesn’t matter; and it doesn’t explain the one-week delay.',
  'gn-soc07': 'Correct: studying for one hour on each of three different days. This keeps the total time the same (three hours) but spreads it across days, which is exactly the spacing effect the text describes.\nWhy not the others: cramming three hours into one night is the single-session approach that did worse; skipping study or stopping after one day doesn’t use spacing.',
  'gn-soc08': 'Correct: the value of the next-best option given up when making a choice. The first sentence defines opportunity cost this way. It isn’t money spent; it’s what you could have had instead.\nWhy not the others: money spent, the price of an event, and profit from a job are ordinary costs or earnings, not what you give up by choosing.',
  'gn-soc09': 'Correct: the $80 the student would have earned working. Choosing the workshop means giving up the shift, and the text says the opportunity cost of the workshop is “the $80 the student did not earn.”\nWhy not the others: the workshop being free doesn’t make its opportunity cost zero; the skills are the opportunity cost of working (the reverse choice); travel cost isn’t mentioned.',
  'gn-soc10': 'False. The free workshop example shows the opposite: even a free choice has an opportunity cost, because choosing it means giving up the next-best option (here, $80 in wages).',
  'gn-soc11': 'Correct: people decide better when they account for the hidden costs of their choices. The last sentence states the author’s claim, and the student example illustrates it.\nWhy not the others: the text doesn’t say which option the student should choose, doesn’t call free events a waste, and doesn’t mention economists disagreeing.',
  'gn-soc12': 'Push factors (reasons to leave the South): segregation laws, limited economic opportunity, and racial violence.\nPull factors (attractions elsewhere): factory jobs in northern cities and industries’ urgent need for workers during the world wars.\nTip: the text itself uses “pushed to leave” and “pulled north,” so match each factor to the sentence it appears in.',
  'gn-soc13': 'Correct: the growth of new centers of Black culture in cities such as New York and Chicago. The last sentence names this as a result of the migration (Harlem and Chicago’s South Side).\nWhy not the others: segregation laws were a cause (a push factor), not an effect; the world wars were context that increased factory jobs; and factory jobs grew in the North.',
  'gn-soc14': 'Correct: it took place over roughly six decades. The text says “between about 1910 and 1970,” which is about 60 years.\nWhy not the others: it lasted far longer than World War I (1914–1918), continued long after 1920, and began around 1910.',
  'gn-hum01': 'Correct: the outward push of a heavy roof on tall walls. The first sentence states the problem: the higher a wall rose, the more the roof’s weight pushed it outward. The buttress “carried the roof’s outward push down to the ground.”\nWhy not the others: stained glass became possible because of buttresses, not a cost problem they solved; leaking roofs and crowd space aren’t mentioned.',
  'gn-hum02': 'Correct: it describes a problem, an earlier solution with drawbacks, and a new solution with its effects. Problem: walls pushed outward. Earlier solution: thick walls with few windows (dim churches). New solution: the flying buttress, leading to thinner walls and huge stained-glass windows.\nWhy not the others: no specific churches are compared, no builder’s life story is told, and the text doesn’t argue Romanesque churches are more beautiful.',
  'gn-hum03': 'Correct: their thick walls had few windows. Builders used thick walls with few windows to resist the roof’s push. Few windows meant little light, which is why the text calls them dim.\nWhy not the others: the churches weren’t underground; stained glass isn’t said to be newly invented; and darkness is described as a side effect of engineering, not a religious preference.',
  'gn-hum04': 'Correct: convey the emotional effect that the engineering made possible. The passage is mostly about engineering (walls, weight, arches), but this final phrase describes how the light felt to worshippers: heavenly.\nWhy not the others: it doesn’t compare strength, criticize cost, or explain how buttresses were built.',
  'gn-hum05': 'Correct: to correct a misconception by showing that jazz improvisation follows structure. The text starts with what new listeners believe (players just play whatever they want), then “In fact” introduces the truth: improvisers work within “tight limits” of chords, rhythm, and learned phrases.\nWhy not the others: classical music isn’t compared, no list of musicians is given, and sheet music isn’t discussed.',
  'gn-hum06': 'Correct: spontaneous yet shaped by shared conventions. The text says it directly: a jazz solo is like a conversation, “spontaneous, but shaped by a shared language.” A conversation is made up as you go, but follows rules everyone understands.\nWhy not the others: “completely planned” contradicts spontaneous; “random and without rules” contradicts the tight limits; and a conversation involves others, not playing alone.',
  'gn-hum07': 'Correct: appreciative and explanatory. The author explains how improvisation works (chords, rhythm, memorized phrases) and describes players admiringly (“great improvisers”).\nWhy not the others: the author doesn’t criticize jazz, clearly cares about it (not indifferent), and isn’t sad or nostalgic.',
  'gn-hum08': 'False. The author calls solos “spontaneous.” They follow structure (chord progressions, rhythm), but they aren’t scripted ahead of time.',
  'gn-hum09': 'Still the same ship: its unbroken history and its continuing purpose are what make it the same, according to the first group of philosophers.\nNot the same ship: the second group says identity depends on the material, so once the original planks are gone, it’s a different ship.',
  'gn-hum10': 'Correct: to show why the puzzle remains relevant beyond ships. The last sentence explains why the puzzle has lasted 2,000 years: the same question applies to cities whose residents change and to people whose cells are replaced.\nWhy not the others: the author doesn’t pick a side, doesn’t explain ship repairs, and mentions no new discovery.',
  'gn-hum11': 'Correct: is a band still the same band after every original member has been replaced? Like the ship, the band keeps its name, history, and purpose while every part changes, which is the same identity question.\nWhy not the others: ship speed, wood choice, and the first shipbuilder are about ships but not about the identity puzzle.',
  'gn-hum12': 'Correct: it presents a puzzle, describes two competing answers, and explains why it endures. Puzzle (the ship with replaced planks) → two answers (yes, because of history and purpose; no, because of material) → why it lasts (it applies to cities and people).\nWhy not the others: there’s no shipwreck, no criticism of philosophers, and no list of ship parts.',
  'gn-nat01': 'Correct: the dry, curled form a tardigrade takes during cryptobiosis. The text defines it: tardigrades “curl into a dry, barrel-shaped form called a tun.”\nWhy not the others: moss is where they live, radiation is something they survive, and eggs aren’t mentioned.\nTip: science passages define their key terms. Use the passage’s definition.',
  'gn-nat02': 'Correct: their surroundings drying out. The text says it directly: “When their surroundings dry out, many species enter a state called cryptobiosis.”\nWhy not the others: outer space, cold, and radiation are things tardigrades can survive while in cryptobiosis, not what triggers it; lack of food isn’t mentioned.',
  'gn-nat03': 'Order: surroundings dry out (the trigger) → the tardigrade loses water and curls into a tun (physical change) → metabolism slows almost to nothing → when water returns, it rehydrates and becomes active again (recovery).\nThe text describes these in exactly this order.',
  'gn-nat04': 'False. The text says tardigrades have survived “exposure to outer space,” but it says nothing about how long. “Indefinitely” (forever) adds a claim the text doesn’t make.\nRule: don’t add details or extend claims beyond what the passage says.',
  'gn-nat05': 'Correct: by measuring how much the star’s light dips. A bigger planet blocks more light, so “the depth of the dip lets astronomers estimate the planet’s size.”\nWhy not the others: the text says exoplanets are too dim to photograph directly; timing how often dips repeat gives the orbit length, not size; and star temperature isn’t mentioned.',
  'gn-nat06': 'Correct: how long the planet takes to orbit its star. The text says “By timing how often the dips repeat, they can also determine how long the planet takes to orbit its star.” Each dip is one pass in front of the star, so the time between them is one full orbit.\nWhy not the others: size comes from the depth of the dip; distance and temperature aren’t discussed.',
  'gn-nat07': 'Correct: a planet whose orbit never passes between its star and Earth. The transit method only works if the planet crosses in front of the star “as seen from Earth.” If it never does, the star never dims, so the planet can’t be detected this way.\nWhy not the others: large planets cause bigger dips (easier to detect); short orbits cause more frequent dips; and the star’s brightness doesn’t prevent detection.',
  'gn-nat08': 'Correct: Planet A is larger than Planet B and has a shorter orbit. A bigger dip means a bigger planet, and A’s dip (1.0%) is ten times B’s (0.1%). Less time between dips means a shorter orbit, and A’s is 12 days versus B’s 300.\nWhy not the others: B has the smaller dip and the longer orbit; the dips differ, so sizes differ; and A’s orbit is shorter, not longer.',
  'gn-nat09': 'Order: carbon dioxide dissolves in seawater → it forms carbonic acid, lowering pH → less carbonate is available → shell-building organisms have a harder time building shells.\nThis is a cause-and-effect chain: each step leads to the next.',
  'gn-nat10': 'Correct: oysters need carbonate for their shells, and acidification reduces it. The text connects two facts: acidification reduces carbonate in the water, and organisms like oysters use carbonate to build shells.\nWhy not the others: cold water isn’t mentioned; acid doesn’t dissolve oysters “instantly” (the text says building shells becomes more difficult); and oysters aren’t said to absorb CO₂ from the air.',
  'gn-nat11': 'Correct: the lowering of seawater’s pH as it absorbs carbon dioxide. The second sentence defines it: carbon dioxide dissolves, forms carbonic acid, and lowers pH, “a process called ocean acidification.”\nWhy not the others: coastal pollution, reef growth, and rising temperatures are different topics.',
  'gn-nat12': 'False. The text says acidification “can make” building shells “more difficult.” Saying all coral reefs will disappear is a much stronger claim the text never makes.\nRule: an answer that goes beyond the passage’s cautious wording is an overreach.',
  'gn-nat13': 'True. Even if you know more about a topic, the correct answer must be supported by the passage. Outside knowledge can lead you to choices that are true in real life but not stated in the text.',
  /* ================================================================ questions-extra.js */
  'x-voc01': 'candid = frank and honest. A candid answer says what the person really thinks and hides nothing ("To be candid, I didn’t like the movie").\nmeticulous = extremely careful about details. Meticulous work checks every small thing.\nbenevolent = kind and generous. A benevolent person wants good things for others.\nobsolete = no longer in use. An obsolete tool has been replaced by something newer, like a pager after cell phones.',
  'x-voc02': 'Correct: meticulous (extremely careful about details). Recording every measurement to the hundredth of a millimeter is a sign of great care with tiny details, which is what meticulous means.\nWhy not the others: haphazard means careless or random, the opposite; candid means honest and doesn’t describe notes; obsolete means outdated.\nTip: find the clue in the sentence (“to the hundredth of a millimeter”) and pick the word that matches it.',
  'x-voc03': 'Correct: direct and critical. A “pointed” remark is aimed clearly at someone, and the mayor names the three agencies that missed their deadlines. That is a direct criticism.\nWhy not the others: “sharp-tipped” is the literal meaning (a pointed stick), which doesn’t fit a speech; “aimed upward” is unrelated; “polite and vague” is the opposite, since she names names.\nTip: in words-in-context questions, the most common meaning is often the trap.',
  'x-voc04': 'True. Inevitable means certain to happen; it can’t be avoided or stopped. Example: “With the river rising that fast, flooding was inevitable.” Related word: evitable (avoidable) is rare, but the “in-” means “not,” so inevitable = not avoidable.',
  'x-voc05': 'Positive: thrifty, confident, curious. Negative: stingy, arrogant, nosy.\nWhy: each pair describes similar behavior with a different attitude. Thrifty (careful with money, a good habit) vs. stingy (unwilling to share, selfish). Confident (believes in yourself) vs. arrogant (thinks you’re better than others). Curious (eager to learn) vs. nosy (prying into others’ business).\nThis feeling a word carries is called connotation, and the SAT often tests it.',
  'x-voc06': 'Correct: niche (appealing to a small, specialized group). “Practiced by a few collectors” describes a small, specialized hobby, and “Once” tells you it used to be small before it grew to millions of users.\nWhy not the others: ubiquitous means found everywhere, which describes what birdwatching became, not what it was; mandatory means required; lucrative means profitable.\nTip: words like “once” and “now” set up a contrast between before and after.',
  'x-wic01': 'Correct: postpone discussing. “Until next month, when more data will be available” shows the committee is putting off the discussion until later, not ending it. In meetings, to “table” a proposal means to set it aside for now.\nWhy not the others: “place on furniture” is the literal meaning; “arrange in rows” describes a data table; “reject completely” ignores the word “until,” which shows it will come back.',
  'x-wic02': 'Correct: valid. A “sound” argument is logical and well supported. The colon explains it: each step follows logically from the evidence.\nWhy not the others: loud and musical refer to sound as noise; asleep comes from “sound asleep.” None of these describe an argument.\nTip: a colon often introduces an explanation of the word before it, so read what follows.',
  'x-wic03': 'Correct: efficient (doing a job with little waste). Using 30 percent less steel while carrying the same load means doing the same work with fewer resources, which is the definition of efficient.\nWhy not the others: ornate means heavily decorated; fragile means easily broken, but the bridge carries the same load; controversial means causing disagreement, which isn’t mentioned.',
  'x-wic04': 'Correct: “Admittedly.” This word concedes, or admits, a weakness: the survey was small. Then “but” turns to the author’s real point: its results match larger studies.\nTip: concession words (admittedly, granted, of course, although) admit a point before the author pushes back.',
  'x-wic05': 'Correct: influenced. Her admiration for the general affects what she writes. She leaves out his mistakes, so her account is “colored,” or shaped and biased, by her feelings.\nWhy not the others: painted, brightened, and illustrated are literal meanings of color, but an account isn’t literally painted.',
  'x-wic06': 'Correct: cautious (careful not to claim too much). Warning reporters that the findings still need to be repeated is a careful, hedged attitude.\nWhy not the others: exuberant means overflowing with excitement, the opposite; dismissive would mean she thinks the results don’t matter, but she’s just careful; indifferent means not caring, but she clearly cares enough to warn reporters.',
  'x-tsp01': 'Correct: it presents a puzzle that the research described in the next sentence tries to explain. Octopuses change color to match surroundings, yet they’re colorblind. How can that be? The last sentence describes researchers testing whether proteins in their skin sense light, which is an attempt to solve the puzzle.\nWhy not the others: speed of color change is in the first sentence; the colorblind fact doesn’t disprove that they change color, it makes it surprising; and no test results are given.',
  'x-tsp02': 'Order: Fleming found mold growing in his dish → he noticed bacteria near the mold had died → he named the substance penicillin → years later, others mass-produced it as a medicine.\nWhy: this is chronological order. You must notice something before you can name it, and “More than a decade later” clearly comes last. “He” in sentence 2 needs Fleming introduced first.',
  'x-tsp03': 'Correct: it challenges a common belief and then gives evidence against it. “Many people assume deserts are nearly lifeless” is the belief. “In fact” introduces the evidence that it’s wrong: 2,000+ plant species and how they survive.\nWhy not the others: there’s no problem with a solution; only one desert is discussed; and no scientist’s expedition is described.\nTip: “Many people assume/believe…” followed by “In fact” or “However” is a classic challenge-the-belief structure.',
  'x-tsp04': 'Correct: “Critics, however, worry that longer school days tire younger students.” A counterargument is an objection to the view just presented. Supporters praise the four-day week; “Critics, however” introduces the opposing concern.\nWhy not the others: the first sentence introduces the topic, the second gives the supporters’ view, and the last says research is ongoing, which is neutral.',
  'x-tsp05': 'Correct: to highlight a surprising contrast between the bird’s small size and its endurance. The text sets “weighs about as much as a penny” against flying 500 miles nonstop, joined by “yet.” That contrast is the whole point.\nWhy not the others: the text doesn’t explain why they migrate, mentions no other birds, and says nothing about food.',
  'x-tsp06': 'False. A main purpose question asks what the text is doing: arguing, explaining, challenging a belief, or highlighting a contrast. A topic is just what it’s about.\nExample: for a text about hummingbirds, “to discuss hummingbirds” names the topic, but “to highlight how surprisingly far tiny hummingbirds fly” names the purpose. Answers that only name the topic are usually traps.',
  'x-ctc01': 'Correct: by noting that their comparison did not show the predicted drop in jobs. Text 1 predicts that raising the minimum wage reduces hiring. Text 2 studied an area that raised its wage and found employment did not fall. Their evidence goes against Text 1’s prediction.\nWhy not the others: Text 2 doesn’t agree; it never says the wage should be lowered; and it doesn’t claim restaurants ignore costs, it just reports what happened in this study.',
  'x-ctc02': 'Correct: note-taking can help students learn lecture material. Text 1 says handwritten notes help memory, and Text 2 says studying typed notes helps students do well. Both assume notes are useful; they disagree only about the best method.\nWhy not the others: Text 2 says typing can work as well (so not “always worse”); neither says to stop taking notes; and Text 2 says reviewing notes helps.\nTip: for “both would agree,” find the idea underneath both arguments.',
  'x-ctc03': 'Text 1: writing slowly forces you to summarize; putting ideas in your own words helps you process them.\nText 2: typing captures more of a lecture; studying detailed notes can close the gap.\nWhy: Text 1 is about what happens while writing (slow, so you summarize). Text 2 is about how much you record and reviewing it later.',
  'x-ctc04': 'Correct: it offers evidence for an additional cause of the city’s decline. Text 1 blames drought. Text 2 finds signs of flooding and says floods “also” damaged farmland. “Also” adds a cause without rejecting drought.\nWhy not the others: Text 2 never says drought had nothing to do with it; it agrees the city “emptied”; and it adds a cause, so drought isn’t the only one.',
  'x-ctc05': 'False. One conflicting study raises doubts about a claim, but it doesn’t prove it wrong. Other studies might support the original claim, or the new study might have flaws.\nTip: answer choices with “proves,” “always,” or “completely” are usually too strong for paired-text questions.',
  'x-cid01': 'Correct: although reefs cover a tiny share of the ocean floor, they support a remarkably large share of marine life. The text’s key point is the contrast: less than 1 percent of the floor, but about a quarter of species. The rest explains why (food, shelter, many small spaces).\nWhy not the others: reefs cover less than 1 percent, not most of the floor; the text says animals live “side by side”; and it never mentions food for people.',
  'x-cid02': 'False. Reefs cover less than 1 percent of the ocean floor. “A quarter” is the share of known marine species that live on reefs. This mix-up is a common trap: the right number attached to the wrong thing.',
  'x-cid03': 'Correct: “Today it also hosts free tax help, coding clubs, and job fairs.” The claim is that the library is more than a place to borrow books, and these services go beyond lending books.\nWhy not the others: the 1962 sentence says it was built to lend books (the old purpose); the brick front and parking lot are about the building, not what it offers.',
  'x-cid04': 'Correct: a position that stopped traffic in every direction. The text says his signal “added a position stopping traffic in all directions,” giving drivers time to clear the intersection.\nWhy not the others: the breathing hood was a separate invention for rescuers; automatic night colors and pedestrian signs aren’t mentioned.',
  'x-cid05': 'Correct: it eased public doubts about the bridge’s safety. People doubted such a long bridge could be safe. Barnum marched 21 elephants across it, and the text says this “helped convince the public the bridge was sturdy.”\nWhy not the others: the text never says it was the first animal crossing; the bridge was already open, so no money was needed to finish it; and elephants walked across, they didn’t swim.',
  'x-inf01': 'Correct: placing feeders close to windows may help reduce bird collisions. Birds hit windows far less often when feeders were within one meter. So moving feeders close may help; “may” keeps the claim appropriately cautious.\nWhy not the others: nothing says birds avoided close feeders; windows vs. feeders isn’t compared; and the study suggests moving feeders, not removing them.\nTip: the right completion follows directly from the results, no further.',
  'x-inf02': 'Correct: Marcus does not own a dog. The survey says every dog owner walked at least 30 minutes. Marcus walked only 10 minutes. If he owned a dog, he would have reported at least 30, so he can’t own one.\nWhy not the others: we don’t know whether he has a cat or how he feels about dogs, and the text doesn’t say how many residents own dogs. These might be true but aren’t certain.\nTip: “must be true” means it has to follow with no extra assumptions.',
  'x-inf03': 'Correct: people carried or traded the shells inland and strung them together, possibly as jewelry. Ocean shells found 300 miles inland, threaded on a cord, were clearly handled by people. Animals don’t string shells.\nWhy not the others: nothing suggests the land was once ocean; the shells are from species that live only in the ocean; and animals wouldn’t thread shells on a cord.',
  'x-inf04': 'True. An inference takes one logical step beyond what the text says. It doesn’t have to be stated word for word, but the text must support it. If you need outside facts to justify an answer, it’s probably wrong.',
  'x-inf05': 'Correct: consider using more blue light. Red light made seedlings taller, but blue light made thicker leaves and stems. “Short and sturdy” matches the blue-light result.\nWhy not the others: red light makes plants taller, the opposite of short; darkness and no light weren’t tested.\nTip: match the goal (short and sturdy) to the result that produced it.',
  'x-coe01': 'Correct: readers who read a 3,000-word article on paper scored higher on comprehension questions than readers who read it on tablets. The claim is about understanding long texts on paper vs. screens. This choice tests exactly that: a long text, both formats, and a measure of understanding.\nWhy not the others: preferences aren’t understanding; price and videos have nothing to do with comprehension.\nTip: the supporting evidence must match every part of the claim.',
  'x-coe02': 'Correct: accidents fell by the same amount on nearby streets that did not get bike lanes. If accidents dropped just as much without a bike lane, something else (weather, a new speed limit, less traffic) probably caused the drop. That weakens the claim.\nWhy not the others: more bike riders doesn’t address accidents; cost is irrelevant; and drivers feeling safer slightly supports the lane rather than weakening the claim.\nTip: to weaken a cause-and-effect claim, look for evidence the effect happened without the cause.',
  'x-coe03': 'Supports: people who drank water before lunch ate fewer calories; people felt fuller after drinking water.\nWeakens: a second study found no difference; water drinkers snacked more later and made up the difference.\nWhy the last one weakens: the claim is that water helps people eat less overall. Eating less at lunch doesn’t count if they eat the calories back later.',
  'x-coe04': 'Correct: “Teo climbed the old water tower on a dare, laughing the whole way up.” Climbing something high and dangerous while laughing shows a lack of fear, which is what fearless means.\nWhy not the others: reading about pirates relates to adventure but doesn’t show courage; height and forgetting homework have nothing to do with fearlessness.',
  'x-coe05': 'True. A finding must address what the claim actually says, not just its general topic. Example: for “paper reading improves understanding,” a finding that students prefer paper is on topic but doesn’t show better understanding.',
  'x-qnt01': 'Correct: screen time was highest for the 16–18 age group. 7.1 hours is the largest value in the table.\nWhy not the others: it rises from 6.2 to 7.1, then falls to 6.8, so it doesn’t rise steadily; 13–15 is the lowest; and the values differ.\nTip: check the whole pattern before choosing words like “steadily.”',
  'x-qnt02': 'Answer: 0.9 hours. Read both values from the table: 16–18 averages 7.1 hours and 13–15 averages 6.2 hours. “How many more” means subtract: 7.1 − 6.2 = 0.9.',
  'x-qnt03': 'Correct: of 200 seedlings planted with mulch, 164 survived, compared with 118 of 200 without mulch. To show mulch improved survival, you need both groups’ results so you can compare them: 164 vs. 118.\nWhy not the others: these choices are accurate but show no comparison. “Both started with 200” and “some didn’t survive” say nothing about mulch’s effect, and one group alone can’t show a difference.',
  'x-qnt04': 'Answer: 82%. Percent survived = survived ÷ planted × 100. For mulch: 164 ÷ 200 = 0.82, and 0.82 × 100 = 82%.',
  'x-qnt05': 'False. Two quantities rising together is a correlation, which isn’t proof of cause. Something else could cause both. Example: ice cream sales and sunburns both rise in summer, but ice cream doesn’t cause sunburns; hot, sunny weather drives both.',
  'x-trn01': 'Correct: Subsequently (meaning “afterward”). The wing opened first, then attendance rose the next year. The relationship is time order.\nWhy not the others: Nevertheless and In contrast signal opposition, but nothing contrasts; For example would make the second sentence an example of the first, which it isn’t.\nTip: name the relationship between the sentences first (time, contrast, example, result), then pick the word.',
  'x-trn02': 'Correct: by contrast. Most liquids shrink when they freeze; water expands. Water does the opposite, so the relationship is contrast.\nWhy not the others: for instance would make water an example of shrinking liquids; therefore signals a result; similarly signals sameness. All three suggest water behaves like other liquids.',
  'x-trn03': 'Correct: however. The recipe calls for buttermilk; if you don’t have any, you can do something different instead. Offering an alternative to what’s expected is a mild contrast.\nWhy not the others: therefore signals a result; in other words restates the same idea; likewise signals similarity. The second sentence isn’t a result, a restatement, or the same.',
  'x-trn04': 'Correct: Consequently (meaning “as a result”). Otters eat urchins, and urchins eat kelp. So where otters are protected, there are fewer urchins, and kelp thrives. The second sentence is a result of the first.\nWhy not the others: Nonetheless signals something unexpected; Meanwhile signals something at the same time; Instead signals a replacement.',
  'x-trn05': 'furthermore = adds another point (“and also”).\nnevertheless = continues despite an obstacle (“even so”).\nfor instance = gives an example.\nthus = states a result (“so”).\nTip: name the relationship between the sentences first, then match it to the word.',
  'x-trn06': 'Correct: ultimately (meaning “in the end”). After two years of cutting, the final result was 350 pages. “Ultimately” signals the outcome of a long process.\nWhy not the others: similarly signals sameness; for example introduces an example; moreover adds another point. None of these describe a final outcome.',
  'x-syn01': 'Correct: “The Svalbard Global Seed Vault stores crop seeds from around the world to protect crop diversity in case of disaster.” The goal is the vault’s purpose, and this choice states it.\nWhy not the others: the opening date, the location in Norway, and the distance from the North Pole are true facts from the notes, but none says what the vault is for.\nTip: read the goal first, then find the choice that does exactly that. Extra notes are distractions.',
  'x-syn02': 'Correct: “When Annie Allen won the 1950 Pulitzer Prize for Poetry, Gwendolyn Brooks became the first Black author to receive a Pulitzer.” What makes the prize historically significant is that she was the first Black author to win one.\nWhy not the others: the publication date, the Poet Laureate role, and describing the book are accurate but don’t mention the prize or why it mattered historically.',
  'x-syn03': 'Correct: “While some bamboo can grow nearly three feet in a day, oak trees can take 40 years or more to be ready for harvest.” A contrast needs both plants’ growth rates and a contrast word (“while”).\nWhy not the others: “both are used for floors” and “both are used in building” show similarity, not contrast; the bamboo-only choice doesn’t compare anything.',
  'x-syn04': 'Correct: “The axolotl, a salamander native to lakes near Mexico City, is famous for its ability to regrow lost limbs.” An introduction for a reader who doesn’t know the animal must say what it is (a salamander) and give an interesting fact.\nWhy not the others: “Wild axolotls are critically endangered” doesn’t say what an axolotl is; the two choices starting with “it” assume the reader knows what “it” refers to.',
  'x-syn05': 'False. The goal decides which notes to use, and the best answer often uses only one or two. Choices packed with extra facts are often traps if they don’t do what the goal asks.',
  'x-bnd01': 'Correct: semicolon. “The hikers reached the summit at noon” and “clouds rolled in an hour later” are both complete sentences (independent clauses). Two complete sentences must be joined by a period, a semicolon, or a comma plus a FANBOYS word (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so).\nWhy not the others: a comma alone creates a comma splice; no punctuation creates a run-on; and “, meanwhile” is still a comma splice because meanwhile is not a FANBOYS word.',
  'x-bnd02': 'Correct: colon. “The kit includes three tools” is a complete sentence that promises a list, and the list follows. A colon introduces a list or explanation after a complete sentence.\nWhy not the others: a semicolon needs a complete sentence on both sides, but “a hammer, a level, and a tape measure” isn’t one; a comma or no punctuation doesn’t clearly set off the list.',
  'x-bnd03': 'Correct: comma. “The tallest mountain in Africa” is extra information (an appositive) that renames Kilimanjaro. Extra information is set off with a comma on both sides, and the second comma is already after “Africa.”\nWhy not the others: a semicolon and colon would cut the sentence’s subject from its verb; no punctuation leaves the closing comma unmatched.\nTest: remove the phrase. “Mount Kilimanjaro rises more than 19,000 feet” still works, so it’s extra.',
  'x-bnd04': 'Correct: no punctuation. “Who finish early” tells which students may begin the bonus problem. It’s essential, so it takes no commas.\nWhy not the others: a comma would separate the subject from its verb (“Students… may begin”), and semicolons and colons don’t belong between a subject and its verb.\nTest: remove the clause. “Students may begin the bonus problem” changes the meaning (now all students), so the clause is essential.',
  'x-bnd05': 'Correct: comma. When “however” starts a new clause after a semicolon, it’s followed by a comma: “The forecast called for rain; however, the parade went on as planned.”\nWhy not the others: a second semicolon or colon is wrong here; no punctuation leaves however running into the clause.\nPattern: complete sentence; however, complete sentence.',
  'x-bnd06': 'Correct: dash. The interruption “which hung in a farmhouse for decades” opens with a dash, so it must close with a dash.\nWhy not the others: you can’t open with a dash and close with a comma; a semicolon would split the sentence; no punctuation leaves the dash unmatched.\nRule: extra information is set off with a matching pair: two commas, two dashes, or two parentheses.',
  'x-fss01': 'Correct: is. The subject is “collection” (singular), not “maps.” “Of rare maps” is a prepositional phrase that describes the collection. Cross it out: “The collection is displayed.”\nWhy not the others: are, were, and have been are plural forms that would wrongly agree with “maps.”',
  'x-fss02': 'Correct: were. With “neither… nor” (or “either… or”), the verb agrees with the subject closest to it. Here that’s “players,” which is plural, so use “were.”\nWhy not the others: was, is, and has been are singular, which would only work if “coach” were the closer subject.',
  'x-fss03': 'Correct: “Priya noticed that the stage lights were already on.” The opening phrase “Walking into the auditorium” must describe whoever comes right after the comma. Only Priya can walk.\nWhy not the others: the lights and “it” can’t walk. These create a dangling modifier, where the phrase describes the wrong thing.\nRule: the person doing the action in an opening phrase must come right after the comma.',
  'x-fss04': 'Correct: had waited. The climbers were waiting before the rescue team arrived, which was itself in the past. An action finished before another past action takes the past perfect (had + past participle).\nWhy not the others: waits and are waiting are present tense; will wait is future. None fits a story told in the past.',
  'x-fss05': 'Correct: its. “Museum” is singular, so use the singular possessive “its” (no apostrophe).\nWhy not the others: their is plural; it’s means “it is” (“The museum updated it is hours” doesn’t make sense); they’re means “they are.”',
  'x-fss06': 'Correct: swallows’. “Several” means more than one swallow owns the nests, so use the plural possessive: add an apostrophe after the s.\nWhy not the others: swallow’s is singular possessive (one swallow); swallows is plural but shows no ownership; swallows’s is never correct.\nSteps: write the plural (swallows), then add an apostrophe to show ownership (swallows’).',
  'x-lin01': 'Answer: x = 9.\nStep 1: distribute: 5(x − 2) = 5x − 10. Now 5x − 10 = 3x + 8.\nStep 2: subtract 3x from both sides: 2x − 10 = 8.\nStep 3: add 10: 2x = 18.\nStep 4: divide by 2: x = 9.\nCheck: 5(9 − 2) = 35 and 3(9) + 8 = 35. ✓',
  'x-lin02': 'Answer: 8 months.\nWrite the equation: total = sign-up fee + monthly cost × months, so 40 + 25m = 240.\nSubtract 40: 25m = 200.\nDivide by 25: m = 8.\nCheck: 40 + 25(8) = 40 + 200 = 240. ✓',
  'x-lin03': 'Correct: x < −5.\nStep 1: subtract 4: −3x > 15.\nStep 2: divide both sides by −3. Dividing (or multiplying) by a negative flips the inequality sign: x < −5.\nWhy not the others: x > −5 forgets to flip the sign; ±5 comes from a sign error.\nCheck: try x = −6. −3(−6) + 4 = 22, and 22 > 19. ✓',
  'x-lin04': 'Correct: 3.\nSlope = change in y ÷ change in x = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁).\nUsing (1, 4) and (3, 10): (10 − 4) ÷ (3 − 1) = 6 ÷ 2 = 3.\nWhy not the others: 6 is just the change in y; 1/3 flips the fraction (x over y); 2 is just the change in x.',
  'x-lin05': 'Answer: b = 5.\nIf the line passes through (3, 11), then x = 3 and y = 11 make the equation true.\nSubstitute: 11 = 2(3) + b, so 11 = 6 + b.\nSubtract 6: b = 5.',
  'x-lin06': 'Correct: x = 7.\nAdd the two equations so y cancels: (x + y) + (x − y) = 10 + 4, which gives 2x = 14, so x = 7.\nThen y = 10 − 7 = 3.\nCheck: 7 − 3 = 4. ✓\nWhy not the others: 3 is y, 5 is 10 ÷ 2, and 14 is 2x (forgetting to divide).',
  'x-lin07': 'Correct: y = −4x − 7. Parallel lines have the same slope and never meet. In y = mx + b, the slope is m, so a parallel line needs slope −4. Only the y-intercept (b) can change.\nWhy not the others: 4 has the wrong sign; 1/4 and −1/4 are slopes of other lines (−1/4 would not be parallel; perpendicular lines have slope 1/4).',
  'x-lin08': 'Answer: 65 texts.\nEquation: 30 + 0.10t = 36.50, where t is the number of texts.\nSubtract the $30 base: 0.10t = 6.50.\nDivide by 0.10: t = 65.\nCheck: 65 × $0.10 = $6.50, plus $30 = $36.50. ✓',
  'x-lin09': 'False. Both lines have slope 2 but different y-intercepts (3 and −1). Same slope, different intercepts means parallel lines that never cross, so there is no solution.\nRemember: different slopes → one solution; same slope, different intercepts → no solution; same slope and same intercept (the same line) → infinitely many solutions.',
  'x-exp01': 'Answer: 21.\nNotice that 6a + 9b is 3 times 2a + 3b: 3 × 2a = 6a and 3 × 3b = 9b.\nSo 6a + 9b = 3(2a + 3b) = 3 × 7 = 21.\nTip: when you can’t solve for each variable separately, look for a way to rewrite the expression using what you’re given.',
  'x-exp02': 'Correct: x² − 16.\nMultiply using FOIL: x·x = x², x·(−4) = −4x, 4·x = 4x, 4·(−4) = −16.\nThe middle terms cancel (−4x + 4x = 0), leaving x² − 16.\nShortcut: this is a difference of squares, (a + b)(a − b) = a² − b².\nWhy not the others: x² + 16 has the wrong sign; the middle terms don’t remain; and 4² is 16, not 8.',
  'x-exp03': 'Answer: 15.\nSubstitute x = −2 in each term, using parentheses so you don’t lose signs:\n3(−2)² = 3(4) = 12 (a negative squared is positive).\n−(−2) = +2.\nAdd: 12 + 2 + 1 = 15.',
  'x-exp04': 'Correct: 6x − 2.\nStep 1: distribute 4: 8x − 12.\nStep 2: distribute −2 to both terms: −2x + 10. (−2 × −5 = +10; a common mistake is writing −10.)\nStep 3: combine like terms: (8x − 2x) + (−12 + 10) = 6x − 2.\nWhy not the others: 6x − 22 forgets that −2 × −5 is positive; 10x − 2 adds 2x instead of subtracting.',
  'x-exp05': 'Correct: 3x²y.\nDivide each part separately:\nNumbers: 6 ÷ 2 = 3.\nx: x³ ÷ x¹ = x³⁻¹ = x² (subtract exponents when dividing).\ny: y² ÷ y¹ = y.\nResult: 3x²y.\nWhy not the others: 4x²y subtracts 6 − 2; 3x³y² forgets to divide the variables; 3x⁴y³ adds exponents (that’s for multiplying).',
  'x-exp06': 'Answer: k = 6.\nExpand (x + 3)² = (x + 3)(x + 3) = x² + 3x + 3x + 9 = x² + 6x + 9.\nMatch with x² + kx + 9: k = 6.\nCommon mistake: (x + 3)² is not x² + 9. You must include the middle term, 2 × x × 3 = 6x.',
  'x-sta01': 'Answer: 12.\nMean = total ÷ count, so total = mean × count = 12 × 5 = 60.\nThe four known numbers add to 10 + 14 + 9 + 15 = 48.\nThe fifth number = 60 − 48 = 12.',
  'x-sta02': 'Correct: the mean is greater than the median.\nMean: (3 + 7 + 7 + 9 + 24) ÷ 5 = 50 ÷ 5 = 10.\nMedian: the middle value of 3, 7, 7, 9, 24 is 7.\n10 > 7. The outlier 24 pulls the mean up, but the median stays in the middle.\nWhy not the others: the mode (7) equals the median, not greater.',
  'x-sta03': 'Answer: 0.3.\nProbability = favorable outcomes ÷ total outcomes.\nTotal marbles: 4 + 6 + 10 = 20. Blue marbles: 6.\n6 ÷ 20 = 0.3 (or 3/10, or 30%).',
  'x-sta04': 'Correct: 360.\nStep 1: find the sample rate: 48 ÷ 200 = 0.24 (24%).\nStep 2: apply it to the whole school: 0.24 × 1,500 = 360.\nWhy not the others: 48 is only the sample; 240 and 1,140 don’t come from this proportion.',
  'x-sta05': 'Correct: it doubles. Standard deviation measures how spread out values are. Multiplying every value by 2 doubles every distance between values, so the spread doubles too.\nCompare: adding a constant to every value shifts everything over by the same amount; the spread doesn’t change.',
  'x-sta06': 'Answer: 23. Range = largest value − smallest value. The largest is 27 and the smallest is 4 (the data isn’t in order, so check every value). 27 − 4 = 23.',
  'x-sta07': 'Correct: the sample isn’t random and likely overrepresents people who exercise often. People leaving a gym exercise more than the average resident, so they don’t represent the whole town. This is called sampling bias.\nWhy not the others: a large sample isn’t a problem; “always dishonest” is an unsupported generalization; and surveys can study a town if the sample is random.\nRule: you can only generalize to the population the random sample came from.',
  'x-adv01': 'Answer: 10.\nf(5) means replace every x with 5: f(5) = 5² − 3(5) = 25 − 15 = 10.',
  'x-adv02': 'Correct: x = −5 and x = 3.\nFactor: find two numbers that multiply to −15 (the constant) and add to 2 (the x-coefficient): 5 and −3.\nSo x² + 2x − 15 = (x + 5)(x − 3) = 0.\nSet each factor to zero: x + 5 = 0 → x = −5; x − 3 = 0 → x = 3.\nWhy not the others: 5 and −3 is the classic sign mix-up (the roots have the opposite signs of the numbers in the factors).',
  'x-adv03': 'Correct: 2,000 · 3^(d/4).\nExponential growth: starting amount × growth factor^(number of times it grows).\nStart = 2,000. Growth factor = 3 (tripling). It triples once every 4 days, so in d days it triples d/4 times.\nCheck: at d = 4, 2,000 · 3¹ = 6,000, tripled once. ✓\nWhy not the others: 3^(4d) triples 4 times a day; 4^(d/3) mixes up the numbers; 2,000 + 3d/4 is linear, not exponential.',
  'x-adv04': 'Answer: −10. The y-intercept is where the graph crosses the y-axis, which is where x = 0.\nSubstitute x = 0: y = (0 − 2)(0 + 5) = (−2)(5) = −10.',
  'x-adv05': 'Correct: (4, 7). The vertex form of a parabola is y = (x − h)² + k, with vertex (h, k).\nIn y = (x − 4)² + 7, h = 4 (the sign inside the parentheses flips: x − 4 means +4) and k = 7.\nWhy not the others: (−4, 7) forgets to flip; (4, −7) flips the wrong number; (7, 4) swaps them.',
  'x-adv06': 'Answer: x = 4. Rewrite 81 as a power of 3: 3 × 3 = 9, × 3 = 27, × 3 = 81. That’s four 3s, so 81 = 3⁴, and 3^x = 3⁴ means x = 4.',
  'x-adv07': 'Correct: the car loses 15% of its value each year. In exponential decay, the base is the fraction kept each year. 0.85 means the car keeps 85% of its value each year, so it loses 100% − 85% = 15%.\nWhy not the others: losing 85% confuses kept with lost; $0.85 treats it as dollars; and the starting value is $24,000.\nRule: base = 1 − rate for decay (1 − 0.15 = 0.85), and 1 + rate for growth.',
  'x-adv08': 'True. A graph crosses the x-axis where y = 0.\nSet x² − 9 = 0, so x² = 9, and x = 3 or x = −3 (both 3² and (−3)² equal 9).\nAlso: x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3), which gives the same answers.',
  'x-geo01': 'Answer: 15.\nUse the Pythagorean theorem: a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse (the longest side, opposite the right angle).\n9² + 12² = 81 + 144 = 225, and c = √225 = 15.\nShortcut: 9-12-15 is the 3-4-5 triangle multiplied by 3.',
  'x-geo02': 'Answer: 65°. The angles of any triangle add up to 180°. Subtract the known angles: 180 − 47 − 68 = 65.',
  'x-geo03': 'Correct: 36π. Area of a circle = πr². With r = 6: π × 6² = π × 36 = 36π.\nWhy not the others: 12π is the circumference (2πr), a common mix-up; 6π forgets to square; 144π uses the diameter (12²) instead of the radius.',
  'x-geo04': 'Answer: 200 cubic centimeters. Volume of a rectangular box = length × width × height = 4 × 5 × 10 = 200.',
  'x-geo05': 'Correct: 4/5.\nSOH-CAH-TOA: sin = opposite ÷ hypotenuse, cos = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse.\nsin A = 3/5 means the opposite side is 3 and the hypotenuse is 5. Find the adjacent side: 3² + b² = 5², so 9 + b² = 25, b² = 16, b = 4 (a 3-4-5 triangle).\ncos A = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse = 4/5.\nWhy not the others: 3/4 is tan A; 5/3 and 5/4 are flipped.',
  'x-geo06': 'Answer: 45.\nSimilar triangles have the same shape; every length grows by the same scale factor.\nScale factor = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5.\nPerimeter is a length, so it scales by 2.5 too: 18 × 2.5 = 45.\nNote: area would scale by 2.5² = 6.25, but perimeter scales by just 2.5.',
  'x-geo07': 'Correct: 70°. When a transversal crosses two parallel lines, same-side interior angles (between the parallel lines, on the same side of the transversal) add up to 180°. So 180 − 110 = 70.\nWhy not the others: 110° would be true for alternate interior or corresponding angles; 20° and 90° have no basis here.',
  'x-geo08': 'Answer: 31.\nCircumference = π × diameter (or 2πr).\nC = 3.14 × 10 = 31.4, which rounds to 31.\nCommon mistake: using area (πr² = 25π ≈ 78.5).',
  'x-lit01': 'Correct: they share a quiet friendship built on small, unspoken kindnesses. Each week Mr. Okafor sweeps her sidewalk and she leaves him a warm bag, without words. After eleven years, these gestures are their way of caring for each other.\nWhy not the others: they don’t avoid each other; the warm bag shows she appreciates him, not resentment; and he never asks for payment, he just sweeps.\nTip: in literary passages, repeated actions often reveal relationships.',
  'x-lit02': 'Correct: their routine expresses their appreciation without words. After eleven years, the sweeping and the warm bag say everything a “thank you” would, so words aren’t needed.\nWhy not the others: they haven’t stopped speaking; nothing suggests being too busy; and the gestures show they notice each other.',
  'x-lit03': 'False. The text says the bag comes “with no note.” Literary questions test careful reading of details as much as reading between the lines.',
  'x-lit04': 'Correct: seeing her brother’s support gives her the confidence to speak from memory. Right after she sees her brother’s homemade sign, she sets her notes aside and doesn’t look at them. Her forty practice runs mean she knows the speech; his support gives her the confidence to trust that.\nWhy not the others: the notes aren’t lost or blank, and no teacher’s instruction is mentioned.',
  'x-lit05': 'Correct: show the homemade, heartfelt nature of her brother’s support. A marker running dry, a lopsided star: these imperfect details make the sign feel personal and sincere. He made it himself.\nWhy not the others: the sign shows he cared a lot; it doesn’t explain her nerves (it calms them); and school supplies aren’t criticized.',
  'x-lit06': 'Correct: warm and quietly triumphant. Affectionate details (her brother’s sign) create warmth, and her confident ending (not looking at her notes once) is a quiet victory.\nWhy not the others: the ending is confident, not gloomy; nothing mocks anyone; and the loving details aren’t detached or clinical.',
  'x-soc01': 'Order: a disease destroyed potato crops → families who relied on potatoes lost their main food → about a million died and another million emigrated → Ireland’s population kept falling for decades.\nWhy: each event causes the next. Losing crops caused hunger, hunger caused death and emigration, and ongoing emigration kept the population falling.',
  'x-soc02': 'Correct: they depended almost entirely on potatoes for food. The first sentence explains it: many poor families relied “almost entirely on potatoes,” so when the potatoes failed, they had nothing else.\nWhy not the others: they emigrated after the famine, not before; wheat and corn aren’t mentioned; and nothing says they lived in cities.',
  'x-soc03': 'False. The text says the population “kept falling for decades afterward” as people continued to emigrate. It did not recover quickly.',
  'x-soc04': 'Correct: each witness assumes someone else will take action. The text calls this diffusion of responsibility: in a crowd, responsibility feels shared, so “each witness assumes someone else will act, so no one does.”\nWhy not the others: physical crowding, not knowing how to call, and people being less kind are not reasons the text gives.',
  'x-soc05': 'Correct: it makes clear who is responsible for acting. The bystander effect happens because everyone assumes someone else will help. Pointing at one person and saying “You, call for help” “removes that uncertainty”: now that person knows it’s their job.\nWhy not the others: it doesn’t make the crowd leave, prove anything is real, or help the person rest.',
  'x-soc06': 'Correct: a crowd can make help less likely because responsibility feels shared, but naming one helper can counter this. The text does three things: names the pattern, explains the cause, and gives a fix. The best answer covers all three.\nWhy not the others: the text doesn’t tell people to avoid crowds; psychologists do have an explanation; and it supports safety training.\nTip: the central idea should cover the whole passage, not one detail.',
  'x-hum01': 'Correct: it describes an earlier tradition, explains how a group broke from it, and notes the first reaction to their work. Tradition: painters hid brushstrokes. Break: Impressionists used visible dabs of color. Reaction: critics mocked them.\nWhy not the others: Monet’s life story isn’t told; the author doesn’t argue for smooth painting; and no paintings are ranked by value.',
  'x-hum02': 'Correct: how light looked at a particular moment. The text says they tried “to capture how light looked at a particular moment rather than every detail of a scene.”\nWhy not the others: every detail and smooth surfaces describe the earlier tradition they rejected; and critics mocked them, so approval wasn’t their aim.',
  'x-hum03': 'Correct: the movement’s name began as a critic’s insult. A critic borrowed Monet’s painting title, “Impression, Sunrise,” to mock the group, and the name “Impressionists” stuck.\nWhy not the others: a critic named the group, not Monet; critics mocked the paintings at first; and the name came from a painting’s title, not a sunrise itself.',
  'x-hum04': 'Kant: a moral rule must hold in every case; lying is wrong even to protect a friend.\nLater critics: the results of an action matter; saving a life can make a lie right.\nWhy: Kant judges an action by the rule alone (rules hold in every case). His critics say consequences, like saving a life, also count.',
  'x-hum05': 'Correct: the author presents both positions without taking a side. The author reports what Kant argued and what “many later thinkers” argue, but never says which is right.\nWhy not the others: the author doesn’t argue for Kant, doesn’t mock anyone, and doesn’t say the debate is settled.\nTip: look for opinion words (“rightly,” “wrongly,” “should”). If there are none, the author is likely neutral.',
  'x-hum06': 'False. Kant argued that lying is always wrong, even to protect a friend from a murderer. Saving a life as a justification is the critics’ view.',
  'x-nat01': 'Correct: by how long an echo takes to return. The text says: “The time an echo takes to return tells the bat how far away an object is.” A farther object means the sound travels a longer path, so the echo takes longer.\nWhy not the others: loudness, color, and smell aren’t mentioned as ways to judge distance.',
  'x-nat02': 'Correct: a rapid series of calls a bat makes as it closes in on prey. The text defines it: as the bat closes in, it calls “faster and faster, a burst known as a ‘feeding buzz,’” which gives it quick updates on the insect’s position.\nWhy not the others: it’s the bat’s own calls, not insect sounds, calls to other bats, or wing noise.',
  'x-nat03': 'Order: the bat sends out a high-pitched call → the sound bounces off an insect → the echo returns to the bat → as it closes in, the bat calls faster and faster.\nThis follows the passage’s explanation: call, echo, return, then the feeding buzz as the final step.',
  'x-nat04': 'Correct: the collapse of a massive star’s core after it runs out of fuel. The text says that when a star much more massive than the Sun runs out of fuel, its core can collapse in less than a second, triggering a supernova.\nWhy not the others: galaxy collisions, planets falling into stars, and the Sun brightening aren’t mentioned.',
  'x-nat05': 'Correct: they scatter elements that can later become part of new planets. The last sentence explains that the explosion scatters oxygen, calcium, and iron into space, where they can later become part of new stars and planets.\nWhy not the others: supernovae don’t pull planets together, cool into planets, or stop stars from forming according to the text.',
  'x-nat06': 'False. The text describes stars “much more massive than the Sun” and says nothing about the Sun exploding. Answering “true” would require outside assumptions; answer only from the text.',
  /* ================================================================ essay.js */
  'ess-th1': 'Correct: “Although art can’t pave roads, cities should fund modest public art because it builds the civic pride that makes residents invest in their neighborhoods.”\nA strong thesis does three things: takes a clear side (cities should fund it), gives a reason (civic pride makes residents invest), and acknowledges the other view (“Although art can’t pave roads”).\nWhy not the others: “good arguments on both sides” takes no side; the definition only explains what public art is; and “I think public art is nice” is a vague opinion with no real reason.',
  'ess-th2': 'Correct: it doesn’t take a position. Saying uniforms have advantages and disadvantages is true of nearly everything, so the reader still doesn’t know what you think.\nA better version: “Schools should require uniforms because they reduce morning decisions and clothing-based teasing, even though they limit self-expression.”\nWhy not the others: the thesis is short, not too long; its language is formal; and acknowledging a counterargument is a strength, not a problem.',
  'ess-ev1': 'Correct: the student who kept volunteering at the food bank through college and later organized a campus food drive. The claim is about long-term habits, and this example shows a specific person whose habit lasted for years.\nWhy not the others: “many schools require it” doesn’t show any effect on habits; “everyone knows” is an assumption, not evidence; and “some students don’t like it” works against the claim.\nTip: strong evidence is specific (who, what, how long) and matches exactly what the claim says.',
  'ess-an1': 'Correct: “This shows that the savings from automation landed with the company while the costs landed on workers, which is why the company should fund their next step.” Analysis explains how the example proves the claim. This sentence connects the lost jobs to the claim that automation should come with retraining.\nWhy not the others: speed, how common robots are, and when it happened are more facts, not reasoning. They leave the reader to figure out why the example matters.\nTip: after every example, write a sentence that starts like “This shows that…” and ties it to your thesis.',
  'ess-ca1': 'Correct: “Critics are right that art can’t fix potholes; however, a mural that costs less than a single block of repaving can draw visitors who support the businesses that fund those repairs.” This does all three parts of a good counterargument: states the opposing view fairly, concedes what’s true (“Critics are right”), and explains why your position still holds (the mural’s low cost and benefit).\nWhy not the others: “they are wrong” gives no reason; “everyone should agree” ignores the other side; and refusing to discuss it skips the counterargument entirely.',
  'ess-pe1': 'Correct: “Perspective Two is right that attention matters most, but it overlooks how a phone can replace a $100 calculator for a student who can’t afford one.” Engaging a perspective means agreeing or disagreeing with it and giving a reason. This sentence agrees with part of it, then points out what it misses, with a specific example.\nWhy not the others: summarizing all three adds no analysis; agreeing with all of them “in some ways” is too vague; and calling them “interesting” says nothing.',
  'ess-or1': 'Order: topic sentence that states the reason → a specific example → explanation of how the example supports the reason → link back to the thesis.\nWhy: the reader needs to know your point before seeing the example. After the example, you explain it (analysis) so the reader sees why it matters. The last sentence ties the paragraph back to your overall argument. A memory aid: Claim → Evidence → Reasoning → Link.',
  'ess-or2': 'Order: context about the issue → why the issue is hard to decide → your thesis.\nWhy: start broad so the reader understands the topic, then show the tension (why reasonable people disagree), then take your position. The thesis usually comes at the end of the introduction so the reader knows exactly what you will argue.',
  'ess-st1': 'Specific: the 2019 bus route cut with attendance falling by a third; library evening hours doubling teen visits; a night-shift nurse who can’t reach a pharmacy that closes at 6 p.m.\nVague: “Many people have problems with transportation”; “Libraries are important to communities”; “Things like this happen all the time.”\nWhy: specific support names who, what, when, or how much, so it proves something. Vague support could be pasted into any essay and doesn’t prove anything.',
  'ess-la1': 'Correct: “Constant notifications fragment students’ attention.” It uses a precise verb (fragment, meaning to break into pieces) and specific nouns (notifications, students), and it’s formal.\nWhy not the others: “super bad” and “kind of bad” are informal and vague; “and other things” and “you know?” are filler that weaken the sentence.\nTip: replace intensifiers like “really” and “very” with a stronger, more specific word.',
  'ess-la2': 'Correct: the self-checkout opening. It drops the reader into a concrete situation that shows the tension (the store saves money, but a worker needs a new job), which is exactly the issue the essay will address.\nWhy not the others: “Since the beginning of time” and “In today’s society” are clichés that say nothing specific; “This essay will talk about…” announces instead of engaging the reader.',
  'ess-co1': 'Correct: “If cities treat art as part of their infrastructure, they invest not just in walls and parks but in the pride that keeps people caring for them.” A strong conclusion restates the main claim in new words and points to a larger meaning (why it matters).\nWhy not the others: “as I said in my introduction” just repeats; “That is all I have to say” adds nothing; and “many pros and cons” abandons your position.',
  'ess-tr1': 'Correct: However. The first sentence gives a benefit of a phone ban; the second gives a cost that works against it. Moving from a point to a point against it is a contrast.\nWhy not the others: Therefore signals a result; For example introduces an example of the same idea; Similarly signals sameness. None shows the shift to a downside.',
  'ess-tf1': 'False. On the ACT Writing test, you can agree with one perspective, partly agree, combine ideas from more than one, or argue your own view. What matters is that you clearly explain how your view relates to at least one of the given perspectives.',
  'ess-tf2': 'False. The SAT stopped offering its optional essay in 2021. The ACT still offers an optional 40-minute Writing test; check whether the colleges you’re applying to want it.',
  'ess-tm1': 'Correct: about 8 minutes planning, 27 writing, 5 proofreading. A short plan (thesis, reasons, examples) keeps your argument organized. Most of the time goes to writing. The last few minutes let you finish a conclusion and fix errors.\nWhy not the others: no plan often leads to a rambling essay; 20 minutes of planning leaves too little writing time; and stopping mid-sentence means no conclusion.',
  /* ================================================================ questions-hard.js */
  'h-lin01': 'Answer: k = 5.\nIdea: a system has no solution when its lines are parallel: same slope, different intercepts. For equations in standard form (ax + by = c), the lines are parallel when the x and y coefficients are in the same ratio.\nStep 1: compare coefficients: 3/6 = k/10. 3/6 = 1/2, so k/10 = 1/2, and k = 5.\nStep 2: check that they aren’t the same line: the constants are 12 and 30, and 12/30 = 2/5, which isn’t 1/2. So the lines are parallel but different. ✓',
  'h-lin02': 'Correct: c = −7/2.\nIdea: an equation has infinitely many solutions when both sides are identical for every x.\nStep 1: expand both sides: 4x − 4c = 4x + 14.\nStep 2: the x-terms already match (4x and 4x), so the constants must match too: −4c = 14.\nStep 3: divide by −4: c = −14/4 = −7/2.\nWhy not the others: 7/2 has the wrong sign; −7 forgets to divide by 2 (or uses 2 instead of 4).',
  'h-lin03': 'Answer: 1.\nStep 1: slope = (y₂ − y₁)/(x₂ − x₁) = (−7 − 5)/(4 − (−2)) = −12/6 = −2.\nStep 2: use point-slope form with (−2, 5): y − 5 = −2(x + 2).\nStep 3: the y-intercept is where x = 0: y − 5 = −2(2) = −4, so y = 1.\nCheck with the other point: from x = 0 to x = 4, y drops by 2 × 4 = 8: 1 − 8 = −7. ✓',
  'h-lin04': 'Correct: 40 + 0.10(t − 200) ≤ 65.\nStep 1: only texts after the first 200 cost extra. If t is the total number of texts, the extra texts are t − 200, costing 0.10(t − 200).\nStep 2: the bill is $40 plus that: 40 + 0.10(t − 200).\nStep 3: “at most $65” means less than or equal to: ≤ 65.\nWhy not the others: 0.10t charges every text; (t + 200) adds texts instead of subtracting free ones; and ≥ means “at least.”',
  'h-lin05': 'Answer: 20/3 (about 6.67).\nStep 1: add the equations. The y-terms cancel (3y − 3y = 0): 7x = 21, so x = 3.\nStep 2: substitute into the first equation: 2(3) + 3y = 17, so 3y = 11 and y = 11/3.\nStep 3: x + y = 3 + 11/3 = 9/3 + 11/3 = 20/3.\nTip: notice when the coefficients of one variable are opposites (+3y and −3y); adding the equations eliminates it.',
  'h-lin06': 'Correct: (0, 9).\nStep 1: find the slope of the given line by solving for y: 3x − 4y = 8 → −4y = −3x + 8 → y = (3/4)x − 2. Slope = 3/4.\nStep 2: perpendicular lines have negative reciprocal slopes: flip the fraction and change the sign: −4/3.\nStep 3: line ℓ through (6, 1): y − 1 = −4/3(x − 6).\nStep 4: at the y-axis, x = 0: y − 1 = −4/3(−6) = 8, so y = 9.\nWhy not the others: (0, −7) comes from using slope +4/3 (forgetting the sign change).',
  'h-lin07': 'Answer: b = 5.\nStep 1: find the slope between (2, 11) and (2 + k, 11 + 3k): rise = (11 + 3k) − 11 = 3k; run = (2 + k) − 2 = k. Slope = 3k/k = 3, the same for every nonzero k.\nStep 2: substitute (2, 11) into y = 3x + b: 11 = 6 + b, so b = 5.',
  'h-lin08': 'Correct: V = 300 − 8t.\nStep 1: the net change per minute is what goes in minus what goes out: +4 − 12 = −8 liters per minute.\nStep 2: start at 300 and change by −8 each minute: V = 300 − 8t.\nWhy not the others: −16t adds the rates instead of combining them; +8t has the wrong direction (the tank drains faster than it fills); 288 − 4t has no basis.',
  'h-lin09': 'Answer: −1/3.\nStep 1: solve for y: 2y = 6x + 10, so y = 3x + 5.\nStep 2: shifting down 4 units subtracts 4 from y: y = 3x + 1.\nStep 3: the x-intercept is where y = 0: 0 = 3x + 1, so x = −1/3.',
  'h-lin10': 'Correct: 25.\nNotice that 9a − 6b is 3 times 3a − 2b: 3 × 3a = 9a and 3 × (−2b) = −6b.\nSo 9a − 6b = 3 × 7 = 21, and 9a − 6b + 4 = 21 + 4 = 25.\nWhy not the others: 21 forgets the + 4; 11 just adds 4 to 7; 28 adds 7 instead of 4.\nTip: when you can’t solve for each variable, look for a multiple of the given expression.',
  'h-exp01': 'Correct: (x − 3)/(x − 2).\nStep 1: factor the top. x² − 9 is a difference of squares: (x − 3)(x + 3).\nStep 2: factor the bottom: find two numbers that multiply to −6 and add to 1: 3 and −2. So x² + x − 6 = (x + 3)(x − 2).\nStep 3: cancel the common factor (x + 3): (x − 3)/(x − 2).\nWhy not the others: (x + 3)/(x − 2) cancels the wrong factor; you can’t cancel x² terms directly to get −9/(x − 6).',
  'h-exp02': 'Answer: 23.\nStep 1: square both sides of x + 1/x = 5: (x + 1/x)² = 25.\nStep 2: expand: x² + 2(x)(1/x) + 1/x² = x² + 2 + 1/x² (since x × 1/x = 1).\nStep 3: x² + 2 + 1/x² = 25, so x² + 1/x² = 23.\nCommon mistake: answering 25 by forgetting the middle term 2.',
  'h-exp03': 'Correct: 8x⁶/y³.\nRaise each part to the 3/4 power (multiply exponents):\n16^(3/4): the 4th root of 16 is 2, and 2³ = 8.\n(x⁸)^(3/4) = x^(8 × 3/4) = x⁶.\n(y⁻⁴)^(3/4) = y^(−4 × 3/4) = y⁻³ = 1/y³ (a negative exponent means it goes in the denominator).\nWhy not the others: 12 multiplies 16 × 3/4 instead of using roots; y³ in the numerator ignores the negative exponent.',
  'h-exp04': 'Answer: 2.\nExpand the left side: (ax + 3)(2x + b) = 2ax² + abx + 6x + 3b = 2ax² + (ab + 6)x + 3b.\nMatch to 6x² + 3x − 3:\nx² terms: 2a = 6, so a = 3.\nConstants: 3b = −3, so b = −1.\nCheck the middle term: ab + 6 = (3)(−1) + 6 = 3. ✓\na + b = 3 + (−1) = 2.',
  'h-exp05': 'Correct: −5.\nComplete the square:\nStep 1: factor 3 from the x terms: 3(x² − 4x) + 7.\nStep 2: half of −4 is −2, and (−2)² = 4. Add and subtract 4 inside: 3(x² − 4x + 4 − 4) + 7.\nStep 3: 3((x − 2)² − 4) + 7 = 3(x − 2)² − 12 + 7 = 3(x − 2)² − 5.\nSo k = −5.\nWhy not the others: 7 is the original constant; 19 adds 12 instead of subtracting; −12 forgets the + 7.',
  'h-exp06': 'Answer: x = 5.\nStep 1: write every number as a power of 2: 32 = 2⁵ and 4^x = (2²)^x = 2^(2x).\nStep 2: the right side becomes 2⁵ · 2^(2x) = 2^(5 + 2x) (when multiplying the same base, add exponents).\nStep 3: now 2^(3x) = 2^(2x + 5), so the exponents must be equal: 3x = 2x + 5, x = 5.',
  'h-exp07': 'Correct: 4/(x² − 4).\nStep 1: common denominator (x − 2)(x + 2).\nStep 2: rewrite each fraction: (x + 2)/((x − 2)(x + 2)) − (x − 2)/((x − 2)(x + 2)).\nStep 3: subtract the numerators carefully: (x + 2) − (x − 2) = x + 2 − x + 2 = 4.\nStep 4: (x − 2)(x + 2) = x² − 4, so the answer is 4/(x² − 4).\nWhy not the others: −4 comes from subtracting in the wrong order; 2x comes from adding instead of subtracting.',
  'h-exp08': 'Answer: x = 7.\nStep 1: cross-multiply: 5(x − 3) = 2(x + 3).\nStep 2: distribute: 5x − 15 = 2x + 6.\nStep 3: 3x = 21, so x = 7.\nCheck: 5/10 = 1/2 and 2/4 = 1/2. ✓',
  'h-adv01': 'Answer: c = 9.\nIdea: the discriminant b² − 4ac tells how many x-intercepts a quadratic has: positive → two, zero → one, negative → none.\nStep 1: here a = 1, b = −6, and the constant is c. Discriminant = (−6)² − 4(1)(c) = 36 − 4c.\nStep 2: exactly one intercept means 36 − 4c = 0, so c = 9.\nCheck: x² − 6x + 9 = (x − 3)², which touches the x-axis only at x = 3. ✓',
  'h-adv02': 'Correct: k = 7.\nIdea: no real solutions means the discriminant is negative: b² − 4ac < 0.\nStep 1: a = 2, b = k, c = 8: k² − 4(2)(8) < 0, so k² < 64.\nStep 2: that means −8 < k < 8.\nStep 3: only 7 is in that range.\nWhy not the others: k = 8 gives a discriminant of 0 (exactly one solution); −9 and 10 give positive discriminants (two solutions).',
  'h-adv03': 'Answer: x = 3.\nStep 1: where the graphs meet, their y-values are equal: x² − 4x + 1 = 2x − 8.\nStep 2: move everything to one side: x² − 6x + 9 = 0.\nStep 3: factor: (x − 3)² = 0, so x = 3.\nThe squared factor (a double root) is why there’s exactly one intersection point; the line just touches the parabola.',
  'h-adv04': 'Correct: 69 feet.\nIdea: for h(t) = at² + bt + c with a negative a, the maximum is at the vertex, t = −b/(2a).\nStep 1: t = −64/(2 × −16) = −64/−32 = 2 seconds.\nStep 2: h(2) = −16(4) + 64(2) + 5 = −64 + 128 + 5 = 69 feet.\nWhy not the others: 64 is just b; 5 is the starting height (t = 0); 133 forgets the negative sign on −64.',
  'h-adv05': 'Answer: −4.\nWork from the inside out:\nf(g(2)): g(2) = 2² + 1 = 5, then f(5) = 3(5) − 2 = 13.\ng(f(2)): f(2) = 3(2) − 2 = 4, then g(4) = 4² + 1 = 17.\nDifference: 13 − 17 = −4.\nNote: f(g(x)) and g(f(x)) are usually different. Order matters.',
  'h-adv06': 'Correct: the value decreases by 15% each year.\nIdea: in a(b)^t, the base b is the fraction kept each period. 0.85 means 85% is kept, so 15% is lost each year.\nWhy not the others: 85% confuses kept with lost; $180 is 15% of the first year only (the dollar drop shrinks each year because it’s 15% of a smaller number); after 2 years: 1,200 × 0.85² = 1,200 × 0.7225 = $867, not $1,020 (that’s after 1 year).',
  'h-adv07': 'Answer: 1/2.\nTrap: (x − 3)(2x + 5) = 7 is not set to zero, so you can’t just read off 3 and −5/2.\nStep 1: expand: 2x² + 5x − 6x − 15 = 2x² − x − 15.\nStep 2: set equal to 7 and move it over: 2x² − x − 22 = 0.\nStep 3: for ax² + bx + c = 0, the sum of the solutions is −b/a = −(−1)/2 = 1/2.',
  'h-adv08': 'Correct: {9}.\nStep 1: square both sides: x + 7 = (x − 5)² = x² − 10x + 25.\nStep 2: x² − 11x + 18 = 0, which factors to (x − 2)(x − 9) = 0, so x = 2 or 9.\nStep 3: check both in the original, since squaring can create false (extraneous) solutions:\nx = 9: √16 = 4 and 9 − 5 = 4. ✓\nx = 2: √9 = 3, but 2 − 5 = −3. ✗ (a square root is never negative)\nSo only x = 9 works.',
  'h-adv09': 'Answer: 4.\nStep 1: f(x) = 0 at x = 2 and x = −4.\nStep 2: f(x − 3) shifts the graph 3 units to the right, so its zeros are where x − 3 = 2 or x − 3 = −4: x = 5 or x = −1.\nStep 3: a + b = 5 + (−1) = 4.\nCommon mistake: shifting left (x − 3 shifts right, despite the minus sign).',
  'h-adv10': 'Correct: 12%.\nIdea: growth compounds; each year’s growth builds on the last. If the yearly factor is r, then six years of growth is r⁶ = 2.\nStep 1: r = 2^(1/6) ≈ 1.122.\nStep 2: a factor of 1.122 means about 12.2% growth per year, about 12%.\nCheck: 1.12⁶ ≈ 1.97, close to 2. ✓\nWhy not the others: 17% comes from 100% ÷ 6, which ignores compounding (1.17⁶ ≈ 2.6, too much).',
  'h-adv11': 'Answer: k = 5.\nIdea (factor theorem): if (x − 3) is a factor, the polynomial equals 0 when x = 3.\nStep 1: substitute 3: 3³ − 4(3²) + 3k − 6 = 27 − 36 + 3k − 6 = 3k − 15.\nStep 2: set to 0: 3k − 15 = 0, so k = 5.',
  'h-adv12': 'Correct: f(x) = 2(x − 2)² − 3.\nStep 1: in vertex form a(x − h)² + k, a minimum (opening up) needs a > 0, and the minimum value is k. So we need a positive a and k = −3. That rules out the + 3 choice and the −2 choice (which opens down and has a maximum).\nStep 2: check the y-intercept (x = 0) for the remaining two:\n2(0 − 2)² − 3 = 2(4) − 3 = 5 ✓\n(0 − 2)² − 3 = 4 − 3 = 1 ✗',
  'h-sta01': 'Answer: 30.\nStep 1: total of the original 5 numbers = mean × count = 18 × 5 = 90.\nStep 2: total of all 6 numbers = 20 × 6 = 120.\nStep 3: the sixth number is the difference: 120 − 90 = 30.',
  'h-sta02': 'Correct: the mean increases by 5 and the median is unchanged.\nMean: changing 15 to 40 increases the total by 25. Spread across 5 values, the mean rises by 25 ÷ 5 = 5.\nMedian: the middle of 4, 8, 8, 10, 40 is still 8. The median depends only on position, not how big the largest value is.\nWhy not the others: the mean rises by 5, not 25; and the median doesn’t change.',
  'h-sta03': 'Answer: 5/33.\nWithout replacement, the second draw depends on the first.\nFirst draw blue: 5 blue out of 12 total = 5/12.\nSecond draw blue: now 4 blue out of 11 left = 4/11.\nBoth: multiply: (5/12)(4/11) = 20/132 = 5/33 ≈ 0.152.',
  'h-sta04': 'Correct: 9/20.\nThe condition (“a student who prefers online”) tells you which group to look at: only the 80 online students. Of those, 36 are juniors.\n36/80 = 9/20.\nWhy not the others: 2/5 (36/90) is the chance a junior prefers online, the reverse question; 9/50 (36/200) uses all students; and 9/25 has no basis here.\nTip: the “given” group is your denominator.',
  'h-sta05': 'Correct: the percent of all voters who support the measure is plausibly between 47% and 57%. A margin of error of 5 points means the true value is likely within 5 points of the sample result: 52 − 5 = 47 and 52 + 5 = 57.\nWhy not the others: a sample can’t say exactly what all voters think; since the range dips below 50%, passing isn’t certain; and larger samples give smaller margins of error, not larger.',
  'h-sta06': 'Answer: 14.\nWith 9 values, the median is the 5th value (4 below it, 4 above).\nAdding one value smaller than all of them and one larger than all of them makes 11 values, where the median is the 6th. The new smallest value pushes everything up one position, so the old 5th value is now the 6th, still the middle.\nSo the median stays 14.',
  'h-sta07': 'Correct: set Y has the greater standard deviation. Standard deviation measures how spread out values are from the mean. Both sets have mean and median 50, but X’s values are within 2 of 50 while Y’s are as far as 20 away. More spread means a larger standard deviation.\nWhy not the others: the medians are both 50; and the spreads are clearly different.',
  'h-sta08': 'Answer: 60.\nStep 1: a 5 : 3 ratio means walkers = 5k and bikers = 3k for some number k.\nStep 2: after 12 more bikers: 5k/(3k + 12) = 5/4.\nStep 3: cross-multiply: 4(5k) = 5(3k + 12) → 20k = 15k + 60 → 5k = 60 → k = 12.\nStep 4: walkers = 5k = 60.\nCheck: 36 bikers + 12 = 48, and 60 : 48 = 5 : 4. ✓',
  'h-sta09': 'Correct: 4% lower.\nStep 1: a 20% increase multiplies by 1.20; a 20% decrease multiplies by 0.80.\nStep 2: 1.20 × 0.80 = 0.96, which is 96% of the original, 4% lower.\nExample: $100 → $120 → $120 − $24 = $96.\nWhy “the same” is wrong: the 20% decrease is taken from a bigger number ($120), so it removes more than was added.',
  'h-sta10': 'Answer: 60 mph.\nAverage speed = total distance ÷ total time (not the average of the speeds).\nTime going: 150 ÷ 50 = 3 hours. Time returning: 150 ÷ 75 = 2 hours.\nTotal: 300 miles in 5 hours = 60 mph.\nTrap: (50 + 75) ÷ 2 = 62.5 ignores that the car spends more time at the slower speed.',
  'h-geo01': 'Answer: 6.\nThe standard circle equation is (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r². Complete the square to get there:\nx terms: x² − 8x. Half of −8 is −4; (−4)² = 16. Add 16 → (x − 4)².\ny terms: y² + 6y. Half of 6 is 3; 3² = 9. Add 9 → (y + 3)².\nAdd the same 16 and 9 to the right side: 11 + 16 + 9 = 36.\n(x − 4)² + (y + 3)² = 36, so r² = 36 and r = 6.\nCommon mistake: answering 36 (that’s r², not r).',
  'h-geo02': 'Correct: 12/5.\nStep 1: sin A = opposite ÷ hypotenuse = 5/13. The side opposite A is 5 and the hypotenuse is 13.\nStep 2: find the third side: 5² + b² = 13² → 25 + b² = 169 → b = 12 (a 5-12-13 triangle).\nStep 3: from angle B, the opposite side is 12 (the side next to A) and the adjacent side is 5.\ntan B = opposite ÷ adjacent = 12/5.\nWhy not the others: 5/12 is tan A; 12/13 is cos A (or sin B).',
  'h-geo03': 'Answer: 6 inches.\nCylinder volume: V = πr²h.\n72π = πr²(8). Divide both sides by 8π: r² = 9, so r = 3.\nThe question asks for the diameter, which is 2r = 6.\nCommon mistake: stopping at the radius (3).',
  'h-geo04': 'Correct: 60°.\nAn arc is a fraction of the full circle, and its central angle is the same fraction of 360°.\nStep 1: circumference = 2πr = 2π(9) = 18π.\nStep 2: fraction = 3π ÷ 18π = 1/6.\nStep 3: 1/6 × 360° = 60°.',
  'h-geo05': 'Answer: 10.\nFor similar figures, if the side ratio is a : b, the area ratio is a² : b².\nStep 1: area ratio = 18 : 50 = 9 : 25.\nStep 2: take square roots for the side ratio: 3 : 5.\nStep 3: 6 × 5/3 = 10.\nTrap: using the area ratio directly (6 × 50/18 ≈ 16.7).',
  'h-geo06': 'Correct: 50.\nRule: the sine of an angle equals the cosine of its complement (the angle that adds with it to 90°): sin(θ) = cos(90° − θ).\nSo sin(40°) = cos(50°), and x = 50.\nWhy not the others: 40 assumes sine and cosine are equal at the same angle; 140 adds to 180 (supplementary, not complementary).',
  'h-geo07': 'Answer: 6.72 (or 168/25).\nStep 1: hypotenuse = √(7² + 24²) = √(49 + 576) = √625 = 25.\nStep 2: find the area using the legs as base and height: (7 × 24)/2 = 84.\nStep 3: the area is the same using the hypotenuse as the base and the altitude h: (25 × h)/2 = 84, so 25h = 168, h = 6.72.\nKey idea: you can compute a triangle’s area with any side as the base.',
  'h-geo08': 'Correct: 50.\nStep 1: a square inscribed in a circle has its diagonal running through the center, so the diagonal equals the diameter: 2 × 5 = 10.\nStep 2: if the side is s, the diagonal is s√2. So s√2 = 10 → s² = 100/2 = 50.\nArea = s² = 50. (Shortcut: area of a square = diagonal² ÷ 2.)\nWhy not the others: 100 squares the diagonal; 25 squares the radius; 25π is the circle’s area.',
  'h-geo09': 'Answer: 10.\nStep 1: read the centers from (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r²: the first is (1, −2) (note y + 2 means k = −2); the second is (7, 6).\nStep 2: distance formula: √((7 − 1)² + (6 − (−2))²) = √(6² + 8²) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10.\nNote: the radii (3 and 2) don’t matter here.',
  'h-geo10': 'Correct: 70°.\nStep 1: the angles add to 180°: (3x + 10) + (5x − 20) + 70 = 180.\nStep 2: combine: 8x + 60 = 180 → 8x = 120 → x = 15.\nStep 3: compute the angles: 3(15) + 10 = 55° and 5(15) − 20 = 55°. So the angles are 55°, 55°, and 70°.\nThe largest is 70°.\nTrap: stopping at 55 or forgetting to compare with the given 70°.',
  'h-wic01': 'Correct: sanguine (optimistic, sometimes too optimistic). The timeline assumed every permit would be approved on the first try, “an outcome that almost never occurs.” That’s overly hopeful.\nWhy not the others: pragmatic means practical and realistic, the opposite; meticulous means very careful, but a careful plan would allow for delays; arbitrary means random, but the problem is too much optimism, not randomness.\nNote: sanguine is a hard word; remember it as “cheerfully confident.”',
  'h-wic02': 'Correct: pervasive (spreading through everything). The treaty is rarely named, but its ideas “quietly shaped nearly every agreement that followed.” That influence is everywhere, even if hidden.\nWhy not the others: negligible means so small it doesn’t matter, and ephemeral means short-lived, both the opposite; contentious means causing arguments, which isn’t mentioned.',
  'h-wic03': 'Correct: justified by what came before. The critic explains the surprises at the end were “prepared by details planted chapters earlier.” So the ending feels deserved; the story set it up.\nWhy not the others: money, competitions, and physical labor are literal meanings of “earned” that don’t fit a novel’s ending.',
  'h-wic04': 'Correct: equivocal (deliberately unclear; open to more than one interpretation). The statement “neither confirmed nor denied” the reports, leaving room for either reading.\nWhy not the others: emphatic means forceful and clear, the opposite; candid means frank and honest, which a dodging statement isn’t; redundant means repetitive.',
  'h-tsp01': 'Correct: it presents a common assumption, describes a study that challenges it, and draws a methodological recommendation.\nAssumption: the loudest birds are the most numerous. Study: Nand found some frequently heard species were rare; their calls carried farther. Recommendation: calibrate acoustic surveys with visual counts (a methodological recommendation means advice about how research should be done).\nWhy not the others: Nand calls acoustic surveys efficient and says to calibrate them, not abandon them; only general species are discussed; and the study challenges rather than confirms the assumption.',
  'h-tsp02': 'Correct: explain why certain species were heard more often than their numbers would suggest. Some species were heard often but were rare. Why? Their calls carried farther, so more recorders picked them up.\nWhy not the others: the phrase doesn’t say louder species are more successful, doesn’t doubt Nand’s counts, and doesn’t introduce a new species.',
  'h-tsp03': 'Correct: to argue that Wei’s later poems are as demanding as her earlier work, despite a contrary view. Critics called the plain style a loss of ambition. “Yet” introduces the author’s counterargument: the spare poems demand just as much because readers must fill in what’s left unsaid.\nWhy not the others: Wei still writes poetry; the text doesn’t criticize readers; and critics responded to her style, they didn’t cause it.',
  'h-ctc01': 'Correct: she would suggest that the argument overlooks savings that can offset higher wages. Text 1 reasons that higher wages mean fewer workers. Okafor found no employment drop and discovered lower turnover, which saves hiring and training costs. Those savings can balance out higher wages, a factor Text 1 ignores.\nWhy not the others: she doesn’t agree (she found no drop); she measured employment, so she thinks it can be measured; and she did find an effect (lower turnover), so “no effects of any kind” is too strong.',
  'h-ctc02': 'Correct: Text 1 presents a general prediction, while Text 2 reports evidence from a specific comparison. Text 1 says what “some economists argue” will happen in general. Text 2 describes a specific study comparing counties across a state line.\nWhy not the others: Text 1 has no data and Text 2 isn’t just opinion; neither relies mainly on interviews; and the roles are reversed in the last choice.',
  'h-cid01': 'Correct: Butler uses time travel to suggest that the past continues to affect people in the present. The last sentence states the main point, and the injury detail supports it: wounds from the past come back to the present.\nWhy not the others: the novel isn’t a scientific explanation; it doesn’t compare which era was more dangerous; and Dana doesn’t travel for research.\nTip: the main idea is often stated in the last sentence of a literary analysis passage.',
  'h-cid02': 'Correct: how suddenly the water reaches the glacier’s bed. Gradual meltwater carves channels that drain the bed and can slow the ice. Sudden pulses overwhelm the channels and speed it up. So timing decides the effect.\nWhy not the others: total yearly amount, temperature, and location aren’t given as the deciding factor. Greenland is just where the measurements were taken.',
  'h-inf01': 'Correct: the protective effect depends on something released by marigold roots into the soil. When the roots were sealed in plastic, the protection disappeared, even though the marigolds were still there. So the benefit must come from the roots’ contact with the soil.\nWhy not the others: sealing roots wouldn’t change shade, so shade can’t explain it; nematode preferences and plastic increasing nematodes aren’t supported.\nTip: in experiments, ask what one thing changed. That change explains the different result.',
  'h-inf02': 'Correct: the prospect of future fees, not just past fines, kept some patrons away. Both groups cleared old fines, so that can’t explain the difference. The only difference was that one group also removed future fees, and only that group saw more patrons return.\nWhy not the others: advertising, collection size, and on-time returns aren’t addressed by the study.\nTip: when two groups share something, it can’t explain their difference; look at what differs.',
  'h-inf03': 'Correct: the increase in fire likely helped oaks replace pines. Fires began 9,000 years ago, and oaks replaced pines within 500 years. Oaks resprout after fire, but pines don’t. So fire likely favored the oaks.\nWhy not the others: fire came first, so oaks didn’t cause it; the trees replacing each other suggests they overlapped for a time; and climate isn’t mentioned.',
  'h-inf04': 'Correct: survey responses may overstate how much residents would actually use the pool. In a similar town, 72% said they would use the pool, but only 8% actually went each week. So the new survey (70%) may also overstate real use.\nWhy not the others: the pool’s construction, swimming preferences, and the survey season aren’t mentioned.\nTip: “may” keeps the claim cautious, which is typical of correct inference answers.',
  'h-coe01': 'Correct: “In 2020, the driest year, yields were 29 bushels per acre higher with cover crops, compared with 4 in 2019 and 9 in 2021.”\nThe claim: cover crops helped most in dry years. To support it, you need the size of the advantage each year and the rainfall:\n2020 (14 in, driest): 160 − 131 = 29\n2021 (22 in): 175 − 166 = 9\n2019 (30 in, wettest): 182 − 178 = 4\nThe advantage is biggest when it’s driest.\nWhy not the others: each gives a single number without comparing the advantage across years.',
  'h-coe02': 'Correct: the crows scolded the researcher even in different clothes but ignored a stranger in her old jacket. This separates face from clothing: the crows followed the person (her face), not the jacket.\nWhy not the others: time of day is unrelated; gathering near bright clothes suggests clothing matters; and avoiding all masked researchers doesn’t show they recognize individuals.\nTip: the best evidence isolates the one factor the hypothesis is about.',
  'h-coe03': 'Correct: nearby cities without new bike lanes saw a similar 15% drop that same year. If accidents dropped just as much without bike lanes, something else (weather, gas prices, a new law) likely caused the drop, which weakens the claim that the bike lanes did it.\nWhy not the others: residents liking the lanes, cost, and more bike riders say nothing about what caused fewer accidents.',
  'h-qnt01': 'Answer: 123.\n41% of 300 = 0.41 × 300 = 123.\n(Convert the percent to a decimal by dividing by 100, then multiply.)',
  'h-qnt02': 'Correct: the 60+ group had the highest percentage of daily readers. 52% is the largest percentage in the table.\nWhy not the others: the table shows percentages, not the number of people in each group, so you can’t compare counts (“more people”) or say half of all respondents read daily. And the percentage dips from 41% to 38% for 45–59, so it doesn’t rise with each older group.',
  'h-trn01': 'Correct: Even so (meaning “despite that”). The first sentence gives a weakness (bulky and unreliable), and the second gives a success that happened anyway. That’s a contrast.\nWhy not the others: Consequently suggests their flaws caused their success; Similarly signals sameness; For example would make the second sentence an example of being bulky.',
  'h-trn02': 'Correct: Nevertheless. A tiny area (less than 1%) supporting a huge share of species (a quarter) is surprising. The second fact is true despite the first, which is a contrast.\nWhy not the others: Therefore signals a result, but small size doesn’t cause biodiversity; Likewise signals sameness; In other words restates, but the second sentence adds new information.',
  'h-trn03': 'Correct: Accordingly (meaning “for that reason; as a result”). A small sample from one city is a weakness, so the findings should be treated as preliminary. The second sentence is a consequence of the first.\nWhy not the others: Nonetheless signals contrast; Meanwhile signals simultaneous events; By contrast signals opposition.',
  'h-syn01': 'Correct: “The Svalbard Global Seed Vault in Norway stores backup copies of seeds from gene banks worldwide so that lost collections can be restored.” For an audience that doesn’t know the vault, you need to say what it is and what it’s for (backup seeds so collections can be restored).\nWhy not the others: the 2015 withdrawal, the number of samples, and the location are true facts, but none explains the vault’s purpose.',
  'h-syn02': 'Correct: “In 2015, researchers withdrew seeds from the Svalbard vault to rebuild a collection damaged by war in Syria, showing that the backup system works.” The goal is to show the vault has actually been used. The 2015 withdrawal is the only note that shows this.\nWhy not the others: these describe what the vault stores or where it is, but none shows it being used.',
  'h-bnd01': 'Correct: semicolon. Each list item already has a comma (Lyon, France), so commas alone would make the list confusing. When list items contain commas, separate the items with semicolons, as the rest of the sentence already does (“Italy; and Geneva”).\nWhy not the others: a comma would blur where one city ends and the next begins; a colon is already used to introduce the list; and no punctuation runs the items together.',
  'h-bnd02': 'Correct: semicolon. “The researchers had expected the samples to degrade quickly” and “instead, the samples remained stable for months” are both complete sentences. “Instead” is a transition word, not a joining word (FANBOYS), so it can’t join sentences with just a comma.\nWhy not the others: a comma creates a comma splice; no punctuation creates a run-on; and “because” changes the meaning (it doesn’t explain why).',
  'h-fss01': 'Correct: were. With “neither… nor,” the verb agrees with the closer subject. “Two coauthors” is closer and plural, so use “were.”\nWhy not the others: was, is, and has been are singular, which would only work if “lead author” were closer to the verb.',
  'h-fss02': 'Correct: is. The subject is “collection,” which is singular. “Of rare maps” and the clause “which the museum acquired from three private donors” are both interruptions. Cross them out: “The collection is now on display.”\nWhy not the others: are, were, and have been are plural, which would wrongly agree with “maps” or “donors.”',
  'h-fss03': 'Correct: “the analysts concluded that the error came from a faulty sensor.” The opening phrase “Having reviewed the data twice” must describe whoever comes right after the comma, and only the analysts reviewed the data.\nWhy not the others: “the error,” “a faulty sensor,” and “it” didn’t review anything. These create dangling modifiers, even though the analysts are mentioned later in one choice.',
  /* ================================================================ questions-act.js */
  'ae-a1': 'Correct: “recorded the weather” (no comma). “Recorded” is the verb and “the weather” is what was recorded (its object). Never put punctuation between a verb and its object; they belong together.\nWhy not the others: the original comma, the semicolon, and the colon all cut the verb off from its object. A semicolon would also need a complete sentence on both sides.',
  'ae-a2': 'Correct: NO CHANGE. “When a team of climate scientists realized their value” is a dependent clause: it can’t stand alone as a sentence. Here it adds information about “recently,” so a comma correctly attaches it.\nWhy not the others: a period or semicolon would leave the “when” clause as a fragment; “recently when,” puts the comma in the wrong place, splitting the clause from what follows.',
  'ae-a3': 'Correct: are. The subject is “entries,” which is plural. “Written by hand and often smudged by salt spray” is an interruption between the commas. Cross it out: “The keepers’ entries are difficult for computers to read.” The passage describes the present (computers today), so use present tense.\nWhy not the others: is, was, and has been are singular, and was is past tense.',
  'ae-a4': 'Correct: kept, because it shows the scale of some volunteers’ commitment. The paragraph is about volunteers transcribing logbooks, and “more than ten thousand pages” is a specific, relevant detail showing how dedicated some are.\nWhy not the others: the sentence isn’t about how the database stores data; it doesn’t contradict anything; and it’s about volunteers, not computers.\nTip: for keep/delete questions, ask whether the sentence supports the paragraph’s main point.',
  'ae-a5': 'Correct: NO CHANGE (“For instance”). The first sentence says the data have proven useful; the study about severe winters is an example of that usefulness, so an example transition fits.\nWhy not the others: However, Instead, and In contrast all signal opposition, but the study supports the previous sentence.',
  'ae-a6': 'Correct: NO CHANGE. “They were concerned mostly with whether a storm would arrive before the supply boat did” is direct and clear, with a clear subject (they) and verb (were concerned).\nWhy not the others: “Concerning them was…” is awkward; “their concern, which was their main one” repeats itself; and “The thing they were concerned with mostly was if…” is wordy and informal.\nTip: on the ACT, the shortest grammatically correct choice that keeps the meaning is usually right.',
  'ae-a7': 'Correct: “Yet together, these entries.” Brief and short mean the same thing, and the previous sentence already says the entries were brief. Saying it again (twice) is redundant.\nWhy not the others: all other choices still include repeated words or extra clauses that say the same thing.',
  'ae-a8': 'Correct: “stranded on the mainland.” “On the mainland” tells where she was stranded, so it completes the idea and shouldn’t be separated by punctuation.\nWhy not the others: the comma and semicolon split the phrase from the word it describes, and “on, the mainland” puts a comma inside the phrase.',
  'ae-a9': 'Correct: “More than a century later, strangers at their computers are finishing the work those keepers began.” The goal is to connect the keepers to today’s volunteers, and this sentence names both (“those keepers” and “strangers at their computers”) and links them through shared work.\nWhy not the others: automated lighthouses, how logbooks are stored, and the growth of climate science don’t connect the two groups.',
  'ae-a10': 'Correct: “surprisingly absorbing.” The passage is informative and fairly formal, so the word choice should match. “Absorbing” means fully holding your attention, and it matches the diary comparison that follows.\nWhy not the others: “super addictive,” “totally insane,” and “a whole vibe” are slang that clashes with the passage’s tone.',
  'ae-b1': 'Correct: “trees. The.” “In the early 1900s, a fungus arrived… that would kill billions of American chestnut trees” is a complete sentence, and “the blight spread through the eastern forests within fifty years” is another. Joining two complete sentences with just a comma is a comma splice. A period separates them correctly.\nWhy not the others: the original is a comma splice; no punctuation is a run-on; “, and, the” has an extra comma after “and.”',
  'ae-b2': 'Correct: its. “Its” (no apostrophe) shows possession: the wood belongs to the tree. “It’s” always means “it is” or “it has.” Test it: “it is rot-resistant wood was prized” makes no sense.\nWhy not the others: its’ is never correct; their is plural, but “the tree” is singular.',
  'ae-b3': 'Correct: “and then breeds.” The sentence has two actions done by the same subject (one approach): “crosses” and “breeds.” Parallel verbs need the same form and a joining word: “crosses… and then breeds.”\nWhy not the others: without “and,” the original is a comma splice-like run of verbs; “breeding” and “bred” don’t match “crosses” in form or tense.',
  'ae-b4': 'Correct: NO CHANGE. A colon must be followed by something that explains what came before. “It inserted a single wheat gene…” is a complete clause that explains the “direct route.” “Team” is a singular collective noun, so “it” matches.\nWhy not the others: “inserting” and “having inserted” leave no complete sentence after the colon; “they” doesn’t match the singular “team.”',
  'ae-b5': 'Correct: “survived blight infections that killed nearby unmodified trees.” The question asks for specific information about the results. This choice tells you exactly what happened: modified trees survived while normal ones nearby died.\nWhy not the others: “did pretty well” is vague and informal; where they were planted and that they grew aren’t results showing whether the approach worked.',
  'ae-b6': 'Correct: “forests; others.” “Some critics worry about releasing a genetically engineered species into wild forests” and “others argue that the tree should be restored as quickly as possible” are both complete sentences. A semicolon joins two complete, closely related sentences.\nWhy not the others: a comma alone is a comma splice; no punctuation is a run-on; and adding a comma after “others” breaks up the subject and verb.',
  'ae-b7': 'Correct: after the sentence describing the wheat gene that helps neutralize an acid the fungus produces. The new sentence starts with “This acid,” so it must come right after a sentence that mentions an acid. Otherwise “this acid” has nothing to refer to.\nWhy not the others: those sentences don’t mention an acid, so “This acid” would be confusing after them.',
  'ae-b8': 'Correct: NO CHANGE. “It raises questions about…” has a subject (It) and a main verb (raises), so it’s a complete sentence.\nWhy not the others: “It raising” has no proper verb; “Raising” has no subject; and “Which raises” starts a dependent clause, leaving a fragment.',
  'ae-b9': 'Correct: “Few trees have fallen as far, or as fast, as the American chestnut.” It names the main topic (the chestnut’s decline) and draws the reader in by making a striking claim.\nWhy not the others: what fungi are and that many trees grow in eastern forests are too general; roasting chestnuts is off-topic.',
  'ae-b10': 'Correct: “trees’ roots.” “Many surviving trees” is plural, and the roots belong to them, so use the plural possessive: trees’ (apostrophe after the s). “Roots” is just plural (no ownership), so no apostrophe.\nWhy not the others: tree’s is singular possessive; “trees roots’” puts the possessive on the wrong word; “tree’s root’s” adds apostrophes to both.',
  'ae-c1': 'Correct: NO CHANGE. “In the 1950s, Marie Tharp plotted… in a Columbia University lab” and “Women were not allowed on the research ships…” are both complete sentences, so a period between them is correct.\nWhy not the others: a comma alone makes a comma splice; no punctuation makes a run-on; and “; and” uses both a semicolon and a conjunction, which is incorrect.',
  'ae-c2': 'Correct: “range, which.” “Which suggested the seafloor was splitting apart” is a dependent clause; it can’t stand alone. It adds information about the valley, so a comma attaches it to the sentence.\nWhy not the others: the original leaves “Which suggested…” as a fragment; “range which,” misplaces the comma; and a semicolon must have a complete sentence after it.',
  'ae-c3': 'Correct: “brushed off.” To brush off an idea means to dismiss it quickly without serious thought, which matches “quick and dismissive.”\nWhy not the others: doubted is milder (he could have doubted carefully); considered and looked into suggest he gave it serious thought, the opposite of dismissive.',
  'ae-c4': 'Correct: “oceans, and their.” “Tharp and Heezen mapped the floors of the world’s oceans” and “their maps revealed a ridge system…” are both complete sentences. A comma plus a coordinating conjunction (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so) can join them.\nWhy not the others: the original is a comma splice; “and,” puts the comma after the conjunction; no punctuation is a run-on.',
  'ae-c5': 'Correct: “made the ridge visible at a glance, turning columns of numbers into a picture anyone could understand.” The goal is to explain why the maps persuaded scientists. This choice gives the reason: they made a complex data set easy to see and understand.\nWhy not the others: being drawn by hand, being published later, and taking years to finish don’t explain why they convinced anyone.',
  'ae-c6': 'Correct: NO CHANGE (“Today”). The previous paragraph described early doubts; this sentence jumps to the present, when the idea is widely accepted. “Today” clearly marks that time shift.\nWhy not the others: Similarly signals sameness (but acceptance contrasts with doubt); For example introduces an example; Even earlier goes the wrong direction in time.',
  'ae-c7': 'Correct: NO CHANGE (“did she receive”). When a sentence starts with “only” plus a time phrase (“only late in life”), English flips the normal order and adds “did”: “Only late in life did she receive…” Compare: “Never have I seen…”\nWhy not the others: “she did receive” and “she received” keep normal order, which sounds wrong after “only late in life”; “receiving” makes a fragment.',
  'ae-c8': 'Correct: “appear.” The sentence already says the maps “are still reproduced today” in the middle clause. Saying they “continue to be reproduced” again is redundant. “Her maps, which are still reproduced today, appear in classrooms around the world” says it once.\nWhy not the others: the other choices repeat “reproduced” or add “again.”',
  'ae-c9': 'Correct: no, because it is a general fact unrelated to the paragraph’s focus on Tharp’s legacy. The Atlantic’s size is true but says nothing about Tharp’s influence, so adding it would distract.\nWhy not the others: the fact doesn’t explain the ridge, has nothing to do with classrooms, and doesn’t contradict anything (it’s irrelevant, not wrong).',
  'ae-c10': 'Correct: “was a rare chance to change how people understood the planet.” It keeps a formal tone and explains why the discovery mattered.\nWhy not the others: “once-in-a-lifetime thing that was totally amazing,” “super cool,” and “blew her mind” are casual and don’t fit a formal passage.',
  'am-a1': 'Correct: infinitely many.\nStep 1: simplify the left side: 3(x − 4) + 2x = 3x − 12 + 2x = 5x − 12.\nStep 2: now both sides are 5x − 12. They’re identical, so any value of x works.\nRule: if simplifying gives the same expression on both sides (or something like 0 = 0), there are infinitely many solutions. If you get a false statement like 0 = 5, there are none.',
  'am-a2': 'Correct: 3.\nA logarithm asks: what power do I raise the base to?\nlog₂ 64: 2 to what power is 64? 2⁶ = 64, so it’s 6.\nlog₃ 27: 3 to what power is 27? 3³ = 27, so it’s 3.\n6 − 3 = 3.',
  'am-a3': 'Correct: 11 − 10i.\nStep 1: FOIL: 3(1) + 3(−4i) + 2i(1) + 2i(−4i) = 3 − 12i + 2i − 8i².\nStep 2: i² = −1, so −8i² = −8(−1) = +8.\nStep 3: combine: (3 + 8) + (−12i + 2i) = 11 − 10i.\nWhy not the others: −5 − 10i forgets that i² = −1 (treats −8i² as −8).',
  'am-a4': 'Correct: 127.\nIn an arithmetic sequence you add the same number (the common difference d) each time: nth term = first term + (n − 1)d.\nStep 1: from term 1 to term 10 is 9 steps: 7 + 9d = 52 → 9d = 45 → d = 5.\nStep 2: 25th term = 7 + 24(5) = 7 + 120 = 127.\nWhy not the others: 132 uses 25 steps instead of 24 (an off-by-one error).',
  'am-a5': 'Correct: 189.\nGeometric sequence: each term is multiplied by the same ratio. 6 ÷ 3 = 2, so r = 2.\nThe first 6 terms: 3, 6, 12, 24, 48, 96. Add: 3 + 6 + 12 + 24 + 48 + 96 = 189.\nFormula: sum = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) = 3(2⁶ − 1)/(2 − 1) = 3(63) = 189.\nWhy not the others: 96 is the 6th term, not the sum.',
  'am-a6': 'Correct: 4.\nThe inverse function f⁻¹ undoes f. f⁻¹(11) asks: what input to f gives 11?\nSolve 2x + 3 = 11: 2x = 8, x = 4.\nCheck: f(4) = 2(4) + 3 = 11. ✓\nWhy not the others: 25 is f(11), the wrong direction; 1/25 confuses inverse with reciprocal.',
  'am-a7': 'Correct: −2.\nAn absolute value equation |A| = 9 means A = 9 or A = −9 (the distance from 0 is 9 either way).\nCase 1: 2x − 5 = 9 → 2x = 14 → x = 7 (not negative, so it fails x < 0).\nCase 2: 2x − 5 = −9 → 2x = −4 → x = −2. ✓',
  'am-a8': 'Correct: 2.\nStep 1: write both sides with the same base: 125 = 5³, so 125^(x − 1) = (5³)^(x − 1) = 5^(3(x − 1)).\nStep 2: now 5^(x + 1) = 5^(3x − 3), so the exponents are equal: x + 1 = 3x − 3.\nStep 3: 4 = 2x, x = 2.\nCheck: 5³ = 125 and 125¹ = 125. ✓',
  'am-a9': 'Correct: the column vector with entries 14 and 15.\nTo multiply a matrix by a vector, multiply each row of the matrix by the vector (multiply matching entries and add):\nRow 1 (1, 2): 1 × 4 + 2 × 5 = 4 + 10 = 14.\nRow 2 (0, 3): 0 × 4 + 3 × 5 = 0 + 15 = 15.\nSo Av = (14, 15).',
  'am-a10': 'Correct: 6.\nA system doesn’t have exactly one solution when the lines are parallel (no solution) or the same (infinitely many). That happens when the x and y coefficients are in the same ratio.\nStep 1: k/3 = 2/1 → k = 6.\nStep 2: check the constants: 6/4 ≠ 2, so the lines are parallel but different (no solution). Either way, not exactly one solution.',
  'am-a11': 'Correct: 6.\nx² − y² is a difference of squares: (x + y)(x − y).\nSo 48 = (x + y)(x − y) = 8(x − y).\nDivide by 8: x − y = 6.\nTip: when you see x² − y² with x + y or x − y, factor it. No need to find x and y separately.',
  'am-a12': 'Correct: x⁵.\nStep 1: multiplying same-base powers adds exponents: 1/2 + 1/3 = 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6, so the inside is x^(5/6).\nStep 2: raising a power to a power multiplies exponents: (x^(5/6))⁶ = x^(5/6 × 6) = x⁵.\nWhy not the others: x^(1/36) and x^(5/36) divide instead of multiply.',
  'am-g1': 'Correct: 24.\nArea of a triangle = 1/2 × base × height.\nThe base runs along the x-axis from (0, 0) to (8, 0), so it’s 8 long.\nThe height is the perpendicular distance from the third point (3, 6) to the base: its y-coordinate, 6.\nArea = 1/2 × 8 × 6 = 24.\nWhy not the others: 48 forgets the 1/2.',
  'am-g2': 'Correct: 1/2.\nStep 1: 5π/6 = 150° (multiply by 180/π). It’s in Quadrant II (between 90° and 180°).\nStep 2: the reference angle is 180° − 150° = 30° (π/6). sin 30° = 1/2.\nStep 3: sine is positive in Quadrant II (sine is positive in Quadrants I and II), so sin(5π/6) = 1/2.\nWhy not the others: −1/2 has the wrong sign; √3/2 is sin 60°.',
  'am-g3': 'Correct: 10.\nA line from the center perpendicular to a chord cuts the chord in half. That creates a right triangle:\nhalf the chord = 16 ÷ 2 = 8 (one leg)\ndistance from center = 6 (other leg)\nradius = hypotenuse.\nr² = 8² + 6² = 64 + 36 = 100, so r = 10.\nWhy not the others: √52 uses the half-chord and distance incorrectly; 8 is just half the chord.',
  'am-g4': 'Correct: π/2.\nFor y = a sin(bx), the period (how long before the graph repeats) is 2π ÷ b. The amplitude (height) is a.\nHere b = 4, so the period = 2π/4 = π/2.\nThe 3 is the amplitude; it stretches the wave taller but doesn’t change its period.\nWhy not the others: 4π multiplies instead of divides; π/4 forgets the 2.',
  'am-g5': 'Correct: 24√3.\nA regular hexagon can be split into 6 equilateral triangles, each with side length 4.\nArea of an equilateral triangle = (√3/4) × side² = (√3/4) × 16 = 4√3.\nHexagon area = 6 × 4√3 = 24√3.',
  'am-g6': 'Correct: 10.\nIn a 30-60-90 triangle the sides are in the ratio 1 : √3 : 2. The side opposite 30° is the shortest, exactly half the hypotenuse.\n20 ÷ 2 = 10.\nWhy not the others: 10√3 is the side opposite 60°; 20√3 and 5 don’t fit the ratio.\nAlso: sin 30° = opposite ÷ hypotenuse = 1/2, so opposite = 20 × 1/2 = 10.',
  'am-g7': 'Correct: (x + 2)² + (y − 5)² = 25.\nCircle equation: (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r², with center (h, k).\nStep 1: center (−2, 5) gives (x + 2)² + (y − 5)² (subtracting −2 becomes + 2).\nStep 2: r² is the squared distance from the center to (1, 1): (1 − (−2))² + (1 − 5)² = 3² + (−4)² = 9 + 16 = 25.\nWhy not the others: (x − 2)(y + 5) has the signs flipped; = 5 uses r instead of r².',
  'am-g8': 'Correct: 12π.\nCone volume = (1/3)πr²h, where h is the vertical height, not the slant height.\nStep 1: the radius, height, and slant height form a right triangle: h = √(5² − 3²) = √16 = 4.\nStep 2: V = (1/3)π(3²)(4) = (1/3)π(36) = 12π.\nWhy not the others: 15π uses the slant height (5) as the height.',
  'am-g9': 'Correct: −4/5.\nStep 1: tan θ = opposite/adjacent = 3/4. Build a 3-4-5 right triangle: opposite 3, adjacent 4, hypotenuse 5.\nStep 2: cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse = 4/5 (ignoring signs).\nStep 3: in Quadrant III, both x and y are negative, so cosine (x) is negative: −4/5. (tangent is positive there because negative ÷ negative is positive.)\nWhy not the others: 4/5 ignores the quadrant; ±3/5 is sine.',
  'am-g10': 'Correct: (10, −5).\nThe midpoint is halfway between A and B, so B is as far past the midpoint as A is before it.\nx: from −2 to 4 is +6, so B’s x = 4 + 6 = 10.\ny: from 3 to −1 is −4, so B’s y = −1 − 4 = −5.\nFormula: B = (2 × midpoint − A).\nWhy not the others: (1, 1) is the midpoint of A and M, not B.',
  'am-n1': 'Correct: 220.\nA committee doesn’t care about order (Ann, Ben, Cal is the same as Cal, Ann, Ben), so use combinations:\nC(12, 3) = (12 × 11 × 10) ÷ (3 × 2 × 1) = 1,320 ÷ 6 = 220.\nWhy not the others: 1,320 counts ordered arrangements (permutations), counting each committee 6 times.',
  'am-n2': 'Correct: 5/36.\nTwo dice have 6 × 6 = 36 equally likely outcomes.\nWays to get 8: (2, 6), (3, 5), (4, 4), (5, 3), (6, 2), which is 5 ways.\nProbability = 5/36.\nWhy not the others: 1/6 is the probability of a sum of 7 (6 ways); people often forget that (3, 5) and (5, 3) are different outcomes.',
  'am-n3': 'Correct: 95.\nStep 1: current total = 4 × 85 = 340.\nStep 2: total needed for a mean of 87 over 5 tests = 5 × 87 = 435.\nStep 3: 5th score = 435 − 340 = 95.\nWhy not the others: 89 just adds 2 points to 87 instead of using totals.',
  'am-n4': 'Correct: 360.\nThe least common multiple is the smallest number all three divide into.\nStep 1: prime factor each: 18 = 2 × 3², 24 = 2³ × 3, 30 = 2 × 3 × 5.\nStep 2: take the highest power of each prime: 2³, 3², 5.\nStep 3: 8 × 9 × 5 = 360.\nWhy not the others: 6 is the greatest common factor; 720 and 180 are a multiple and a non-multiple of 24.',
  'am-n5': 'Correct: $48.\nA 25% discount means you pay 75% of the original price: 0.75p = 36.\np = 36 ÷ 0.75 = 48.\nCheck: 25% of 48 is 12, and 48 − 12 = 36. ✓\nTrap: adding 25% of $36 ($9) gives $45. That’s wrong because the discount was 25% of the original price, not of $36.',
  'am-n6': 'Correct: 51 miles.\nEach inch represents 15 miles, so multiply: 3.4 × 15 = 51.\n(3 × 15 = 45, plus 0.4 × 15 = 6, total 51.)',
  'am-n7': 'Correct: 24.\nOne book is fixed in the first spot, so only the other 4 books can be arranged.\nThe number of ways to arrange 4 different items is 4! = 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 24.\nWhy not the others: 120 = 5! arranges all five books, ignoring the restriction.',
  'am-n8': 'Correct: 16.\nStep 1: a 3 : 5 ratio means red = 3k and blue = 5k.\nStep 2: after adding 4 red, the ratio is 1 : 1, so red = blue: 3k + 4 = 5k → 4 = 2k → k = 2.\nStep 3: originally, red = 6 and blue = 10, so 16 marbles total.\nCheck: 6 + 4 = 10 red and 10 blue. ✓',
  /* ================================================================ questions-act2.js */
  'ar-r1a': 'Correct: Boston is farther east, so the sun reached its highest point there first. Paragraph 1 explains that the sun reaches its highest point later as you travel west. Boston is east of New York, so the sun peaked there about twelve minutes earlier, and local noon came first.\nWhy not the others: standard time didn’t exist yet in this example; railroads and stations came later in the passage and didn’t set town noon.\nTip: “According to the passage” means the answer is stated directly. Find the sentence.',
  'ar-r1b': 'Correct: trains moving in opposite directions relied on exact timing to avoid each other. Paragraph 2: on single-track lines, trains going opposite directions “depended on precise timing to avoid one another,” so a timing mix-up could cause a crash.\nWhy not the others: stations did have clocks (sometimes several); missed connections were a general problem, not the deadly one; and nothing says those lines ran only at night.',
  'ar-r1c': 'Correct: describe the railroads’ coordinated switch to standard time and one immediate effect. Paragraph 3 tells how the railroads divided the continent into time zones on November 18, 1883, and the immediate effect: clocks struck noon twice (“the day of two noons”).\nWhy not the others: Congress is in paragraph 4 (and it eventually adopted the zones, not refused); the author doesn’t argue sun time was better; and no other countries are discussed.',
  'ar-r1d': 'Correct: the railroads, acting without a law. Paragraph 3 says the railroads acted “without waiting for any law.” Paragraph 4 says Congress didn’t make the zones official until 1918, 35 years later.\nWhy not the others: Congress acted in 1918, not 1883; no scientists’ conference is mentioned; and towns using sun time resisted the change.',
  'ar-r1e': 'Correct: standard time does not match the sun’s actual position in most places. Within a time zone, every town uses the same clock time, but the sun is highest at slightly different moments in each town. So clock noon is “slightly wrong about the sun” almost everywhere, a trade-off for everyone being “right with one another.”\nWhy not the others: the phrase isn’t about mistakes in setting clocks, scientists’ errors, or inaccurate clocks.',
  'ar-r1f': 'Correct: local sun time was the natural, rightful way to keep time. Calling it “God’s time” suggests it was set by nature itself, not by companies, and refusing to give it up shows they saw it as the legitimate way.\nWhy not the others: these people opposed the railroads’ control; they thought sun time was right, not standard time; and Congress isn’t connected to them.\nTip: inference answers go one step beyond what’s stated, and the words people choose (“God’s time”) reveal what they believe.',
  'ar-r1g': 'Correct: appreciative of its practical value while acknowledging the criticism it drew. The author mentions critics (paragraph 4) but concludes with “Yet the railroads’ solution spread because it solved a problem that local time could not.” “Yet” turns from criticism to the author’s positive view.\nWhy not the others: the author reports critics but isn’t harsh; the closing line shows the author thinks it mattered; and the change clearly took effect.',
  'ar-r2a': 'Correct: it curls into a tun, loses most of its body water, and its metabolism nearly stops. Paragraph 2 describes exactly this: it pulls in its legs, curls into a barrel-shaped “tun,” loses nearly all its body water, and its metabolism slows to an undetectable level.\nWhy not the others: paragraph 3 says many species have little or no trehalose; “permanently immune to all damage” is far too strong; and it waits in place, it doesn’t travel.',
  'ar-r2b': 'Correct: explained how tardigrades survive drying even though many species have little or no trehalose. Scientists thought trehalose protected them, but many species had little or none, which left a puzzle. The 2017 team found special proteins that form a protective glassy matrix, explaining survival without trehalose.\nWhy not the others: the discovery showed trehalose wasn’t the main reason; the space experiment showed they can survive; and bears aren’t biologically related (the name is a nickname).',
  'ar-r2c': 'Correct: describe how researchers revised an earlier explanation of tardigrade survival. Paragraph 3 moves from the old explanation (trehalose) to the problem with it (many species lack it) to the new explanation (unique proteins).\nWhy not the others: extreme conditions are listed in paragraph 1; vaccines are in the last paragraph; and trehalose isn’t called harmful.',
  'ar-r2d': 'Correct: suffered less DNA damage from X-rays. Paragraph 4 says human cells with the tardigrade gene had about 40 percent less DNA damage from X-rays.\nWhy not the others: 40% less damage is not immunity; drying and trehalose weren’t part of this experiment.\nTip: watch for answers that exaggerate (“immune to all”) a more modest result.',
  'ar-r2e': 'Correct: might protect fragile molecules without refrigeration. The last paragraph explains that many vaccines must be kept cold, which makes delivery to remote areas hard. If tardigrade proteins can protect fragile molecules at room temperature, they could solve that problem.\nWhy not the others: cost, presence in every animal, and speeding up medicines aren’t mentioned.',
  'ar-r2f': 'Correct: keeps fixed. The glassy matrix “holds the cell’s other molecules in place, preventing them from unfolding or clumping together.” Holding something in place means keeping it fixed so it can’t move or change shape.\nWhy not the others: owns, celebrates (“holds a party”), and delays (“hold up”) are other meanings of hold that don’t fit.',
  'ar-r2g': 'Correct: exposing dried tardigrades to the vacuum of space. Paragraph 1 says a 2007 European experiment “exposed dried tardigrades to the vacuum of space,” and many revived after returning.\nWhy not the others: near-absolute-zero temperatures were in general lab tests; inserting genes into human cells was the 2016 protein research; and trehalose measurements are in paragraph 3.',
  'ar-r3a': 'Correct: a translation should read as though it were originally written in English. Passage A says a translation that reads like it was written in English “has… failed,” because readers should feel they’re somewhere else. Passage B says translators should recreate the original effect in natural language, even if the words look nothing like the original. That’s their core disagreement.\nWhy not the others: both assume translators read the original and that foreign works are worth reading; jokes in novels aren’t disputed.',
  'ar-r3b': 'Correct: recreating the original’s effect matters more than preserving its exact wording. Passage B’s standard is how a passage affected its first readers. A translator should find words that create the same effect, even if they look nothing like the original. Smoothing over a strange phrase could be the right choice if it keeps the effect.\nWhy not the others: B never says readers want no strange phrases; “never change a word” is the opposite of B; and B wants puns adapted, not removed.',
  'ar-r3c': 'Correct: sometimes make readers feel the foreignness of the original. Passage A says a reader should “feel, at least sometimes, that she is somewhere else.” That means keeping some of the original’s strangeness.\nWhy not the others: replacing unfamiliar images is what A criticizes; length and avoiding difficult novels aren’t discussed.',
  'ar-r3d': 'Correct: illustrate how a word-for-word translation can lose the original’s effect. A pun is a joke based on a word with two meanings in one language. Translated word for word, the double meaning disappears and the joke “will fall flat.” This example supports B’s point that translators should recreate effects rather than copy words.\nWhy not the others: the example isn’t about learning Portuguese, B wants jokes adapted (not removed), and A’s author isn’t mentioned.',
  'as-d1': 'Correct: decreases only. Read the fresh water column from 0°C to 40°C: 14.6 → 11.3 → 9.1 → 7.6 → 6.4. Each value is lower than the one before, so it only decreases.\nWhy not the others: it never increases or levels off.\nScience fact: warmer water holds less dissolved gas, which is why fish can struggle in warm water.',
  'as-d2': 'Correct: 1.7 mg/L. Find the 20°C row: fresh water = 9.1 and seawater = 7.4. “How much more” means subtract: 9.1 − 7.4 = 1.7.\nWhy not the others: 16.5 adds the values; 0.7 and 2.7 are subtraction errors.',
  'as-d3': 'Correct: 8.4 mg/L. 25°C isn’t in the table, but it’s halfway between 20°C (9.1) and 30°C (7.6). The value should be about halfway: (9.1 + 7.6) ÷ 2 = 8.35 ≈ 8.4. This is called interpolation (estimating between known data points).\nWhy not the others: 9.8 is higher than the 20°C value; 7.0 is below the 30°C value; 6.4 is the 40°C value.',
  'as-d4': 'Correct: fresh water at 40°C. The fish needs at least 6.5 mg/L. Check each choice:\nfresh water at 40°C: 6.4, below 6.5 ✗\nfresh water at 30°C: 7.6 ✓\nseawater at 10°C: 9.0 ✓\nfresh water at 20°C: 9.1 ✓\nOnly 40°C fresh water falls short.',
  'as-d5': 'Correct: at every temperature listed, seawater holds less dissolved oxygen than fresh water. Compare row by row: 11.2 < 14.6, 9.0 < 11.3, 7.4 < 9.1, 6.2 < 7.6, 5.3 < 6.4. Seawater is lower every time.\nWhy not the others: seawater is never higher; oxygen decreases (not doubles) as temperature rises; and seawater values clearly change with temperature.',
  'as-d6': 'Correct: less than 5.3 mg/L. Seawater oxygen drops steadily as temperature rises: 11.2, 9.0, 7.4, 6.2, 5.3. If the trend continues to 50°C, the value should be lower than 5.3. Predicting beyond the data is called extrapolation.\nWhy not the others: every other choice would require the trend to reverse.',
  'as-r1': 'Correct: 16. Average = sum of trials ÷ number of trials. Damp side in Experiment 1: (16 + 17 + 15) ÷ 3 = 48 ÷ 3 = 16.\nWhy not the others: 4 is the dry side average; 12 is from Experiment 3; and 48 is the total, not the average.',
  'as-r2': 'Correct: the condition that differed between the two sides of the chamber. Experiment 1 tested moisture (damp vs. dry, both lit). Experiment 2 tested light (dark vs. lit, both damp). Everything else stayed the same: 20 pill bugs, 10 minutes, 3 trials.\nWhy not the others: both experiments used 20 pill bugs, the same 10 minutes, and the same animal.',
  'as-r3': 'Correct: prioritize moisture over darkness. In Experiment 3, the bugs had to choose between moisture (damp & lit) and darkness (dry & dark). Averages: damp & lit = (12 + 11 + 13) ÷ 3 = 12; dry & dark = (8 + 9 + 7) ÷ 3 = 8. More chose moisture, so it matters more to them.\nWhy not the others: fewer chose darkness; a 12-to-8 split shows a preference; and dry & lit wasn’t an option.',
  'as-r4': 'Correct: check that the results were consistent and not due to chance. A single trial could be a fluke. Repeating it and getting similar results (16, 17, 15) shows the result is reliable.\nWhy not the others: there’s no mention of using up supplies, other species, or changing temperatures; changing conditions would defeat the purpose of repeating.',
  'as-r5': 'Correct: make sure any difference in where the pill bugs went was caused by light. To test one variable (light), every other factor must stay the same (be controlled). If one side were also damper, you couldn’t tell whether the bugs were choosing darkness or moisture.\nWhy not the others: bacteria, speed, and dry preferences aren’t what Experiment 2 tests.',
  'as-r6': 'Correct: yes; pill bugs preferred damp over dry and dark over lit, so a side with both preferred conditions should attract the most. Experiment 1 shows they prefer damp (16 vs. 4). Experiment 2 shows they prefer dark (14 vs. 6). A damp, dark side combines both preferences against a dry, lit side that has neither.\nWhy not the others: they preferred dark (not lit) in Experiment 2 and damp (not dry) in Experiment 1; and Experiment 3 showed moisture beats darkness.',
  'as-v1': 'Correct: the arrival of humans in a region is closely linked to megafauna extinctions there. Scientist 1 says extinctions followed soon after humans spread across North America and that island animals vanished soon after humans arrived “whatever the climate was.” Both points link human arrival to extinction.\nWhy not the others: climate alone is Scientist 2’s view; Scientist 1 says large mammals reproduce slowly; and Scientist 1 says island animals did vanish after humans arrived.\nTip: “megafauna” means large animals.',
  'as-v2': 'Correct: many large mammals in North America went extinct near the end of the last ice age. Both scientists accept that the extinctions happened around 13,000–11,000 years ago. They disagree only about why.\nWhy not the others: hunting is Scientist 1’s cause only; Scientist 2 says grasslands shrank, not expanded; and only Scientist 1 discusses islands (blaming humans).\nTip: for “both would agree,” look for the shared facts underneath the disagreement.',
  'as-v3': 'Correct: large mammals survived several earlier warming periods of similar speed without mass extinctions. If similar climate changes happened before without wiping them out, climate change alone can’t easily explain the extinctions this time, which weakens Scientist 2.\nWhy not the others: shrinking grasslands, rapid temperature swings, and declines before humans arrived all support Scientist 2.\nTip: to weaken a cause, find a case where the cause happened but the effect didn’t.',
  'as-v4': 'Correct: on an island humans reached much later, mammoths survived until shortly after humans arrived there. If mammoths survived the climate change on the mainland’s schedule but died only when humans showed up, extinction tracked humans, not climate. That strongly supports Scientist 1.\nWhy not the others: forests replacing grasslands supports Scientist 2; slow reproduction is a fact Scientist 1 uses but doesn’t show humans caused the extinctions; and fast warming supports Scientist 2.',
  'as-v5': 'Correct: large mammals reproduce slowly, so losses could outpace births. Scientist 1 says it directly: because large mammals reproduce slowly, even modest hunting could reduce their numbers faster than births could replace them.\nWhy not the others: hunting the youngest, destroying grasslands, and having no predators aren’t reasons Scientist 1 gives.',
  'as-v6': 'Correct: parts of both Scientist 1’s and Scientist 2’s viewpoints. The third scientist combines hunting (Scientist 1’s cause) and climate-driven habitat loss (Scientist 2’s cause), so it agrees with part of each.\nWhy not the others: it doesn’t fully match either one alone, and it clearly includes ideas from both.',
};
